#define FF8OPC_VERSION "0.15.6.2"  // v0.15.6.2: Phase 2b fix follow-up. v0.15.6.1 BAT confirmed our pointer swap landed cleanly: '[POST-ASK-OVERRIDE] Patched slot[2]+0x08: 0x16C3EE98 -> 0x6E98E020' fired, post-call 'text1=0x6E98E020 (override=0x6E98E020)' confirmed both pointers match. But Aaron still heard Selphie's natural elevator dialog. Two log signals identified the bug: (1) NO '[ASK] win[2] Speaking:' line in the log -- ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows silently skipped slot 2; (2) '[SHOW_DIALOG-TEXT] win[2] ... [T2] text=\"Selphie...\"' -- show_dialog fell back to text_data2 (slot+0x0C) which still held the engine's natural pointer (0x16C3EE98, in FF8 heap range). Root cause: field_dialog.cpp's IsValidTextPointer caps the accepted address range at 0x30000000 (a heuristic for FF8's heap range). Our override buffer at 0x6E98E020 lives in the DLL data section, well above the cap. Both ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows and Hook_show_dialog use IsValidTextPointer to gate which pointers they decode, so they treat our buffer as invalid and either skip the slot or fall back to text2. v0.15.6.2 fix: expose the override buffer's stable address range via new DialogInject::GetOverrideBufferStart()/GetOverrideBufferSize() public APIs, and add a whitelist clause to IsValidTextPointer that accepts pointers within that exact range. The buffer is statically allocated and its location is fixed for the DLL's lifetime, so range comparison is safe -- we don't accept arbitrary addresses above 0x30000000, only our specific buffer's bytes. Predicted v0.15.6.2 BAT outcome (clean save reload, slot 2 fresh, Shift+F12): '[POST-ASK-OVERRIDE] Patched slot[2]+0x08' fires (same as v0.15.6.1), then NEW '[ASK] win[2] Parsed 3 choices (firstQ=1 lastQ=3 curChoice=N)' line followed by '[ASK] win[2] Speaking: \"Mode?. Selected: Manual. Auto. Original\"', then queued diagnostic 'Dialog inject phase two B. Slot 2. Return code 1.' Aaron hears 'Mode?' first, then the diagnostic. Arrow keys move cursor between Manual/Auto/Original with cursor-move SFX. Note: subsequent show_dialog calls during the slot poll may also speak our text (text1 is now valid for them too) but should be deduped via ws.lastRawText/lastSpokenText since ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows ran first. Files changed: src/dialog_inject.h (~12 lines: design rationale block extended for v0.15.6.2, new GetOverrideBufferStart/GetOverrideBufferSize public decls). src/dialog_inject.cpp (~25 lines: comment trail extended, two new accessor implementations returning s_overrideBuffer's address and OVERRIDE_BUFFER_SIZE). src/field_dialog.cpp (~20 lines: forward-decl block extended with new APIs, IsValidTextPointer body rewritten to check whitelist after the heap-range check). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.6.1: Phase 2b fix. v0.15.6 BAT failed (Aaron heard Selphie's natural elevator dialog instead of our injected 'Mode? / Manual / Auto / Original'). Diagnosis: zero [GETSTR-RAW] log lines despite Hook_field_get_dialog_string's unconditional first-10-calls logging. FFNx's replace_call rewrote the engine's internal CALL field_get_dialog_string operand to point at FFNx's own function -- our hook on engine 0x00530750 is dead under FFNx. v0.15.6.1 ships post-ASK slot+0x08 patching: in Hook_opcode_ask (field_dialog.cpp), after s_origAsk has populated slot+0x08 with the natural pointer and BEFORE ScanAndSpeakChoiceWindows reads the slot, check IsOverrideActive() and overwrite slot+0x08 with the override buffer pointer. New API: DialogInject::GetOverrideSlot() returns the target slot. v0.15.6.1 BAT result: pointer swap landed (POST-ASK-OVERRIDE log fired, text1 matches override) but the IsValidTextPointer check rejected our DLL-data-section pointer, so the TTS path skipped our slot and show_dialog fell back to text2. Fix shipped as v0.15.6.2 (whitelist override buffer's address range).
  // v0.15.6: Phase 2b ships. Custom FF8-encoded dialog text injection via field_get_dialog_string hook override pattern. Predicted v0.15.6 BAT outcome: Aaron hears 'Mode?. Selected: Manual. Auto. Original' from FieldDialog [ASK] hook decoding our injected buffer; arrow keys move cursor between options with cursor-move SFX. v0.15.6 BAT FAILED: zero [GETSTR-RAW] lines proved FFNx's replace_call had bypassed our get_dialog_string hook entirely. Fix shipped as v0.15.6.1 (post-ASK slot+0x08 patching).
  // v0.15.5.3: Two-character SAPI fix on top of v0.15.5.2 BAT. v0.15.5.2 BAT showed the cursor input fix worked perfectly: log line 'sub_49FD50(2): pCurrentDialogSlot 0xFF -> 0x02' confirmed pCurrentDialogSlot was set, Aaron heard the cursor-move SFX when pressing arrows during the open dialog, opcode_ask returned 1, slot 2 trans 0->0x400->0x1000 with state 0->1->0xD, and the FieldDialog [ASK] hook fired in TWO BATs (Test #1 in doani1_2 spoke 'Selphie. "Wanna go up?". Selected: Go up. Stay'; Test #2 in doan1_2 spoke 'Battle. Battle' since that field's msg 0 is just the word 'Battle'). HOWEVER Aaron reported he heard only the mod's diagnostic announcement, NOT the dialog text. Diagnosis from the SAPI call sequence: the FieldDialog [ASK] hook fires DURING opcode_ask (after field_get_dialog_string returns the encoded text and set_window_object_ASK populates the slot, the engine's per-frame text path triggers our hook which calls ScreenReader::Speak(text, true)). Then opcode_ask returns and our Phase2_TestAsk's AnnouncePhase2Result calls ScreenReader::Speak("Dialog inject phase two A. Slot 2. Return code 1.", true) -- the second arg true means INTERRUPT in-flight speech. SAPI was halfway through speaking the dialog text when our diagnostic announcement preempted it. Aaron heard only 'Dialog inject phase two A...'. v0.15.5.3 fix: change ScreenReader::Speak's interrupt flag from true to false in BOTH AnnouncePhase1Result and AnnouncePhase2Result. This QUEUES the diagnostic announcement after the dialog text rather than cutting it off. Aaron will now hear: (1) the dialog text from the FieldDialog hook ('Selphie. \"Wanna go up?\". Selected: Go up. Stay'), then (2) the queued diagnostic announcement ('Dialog inject phase two A. Slot 2. Return code 1.'). Phase 1 gets the same fix proactively for consistency -- the v0.15.4 BAT had the same race but Aaron didn't notice (likely because Phase 1's MES dialog and the diagnostic announcement are textually similar enough). Other diagnostic Speak calls (error guards like 'Dialog inject opcode address missing' or 'Dialog inject crashed') still use interrupt=true intentionally -- those are error paths where preemption is correct. Two new comment blocks added explaining the rationale. Aaron also reported arrows moved Squall AND the dialog cursor SIMULTANEOUSLY -- this is a known limitation: our injected dialog doesn't suspend field input because we don't run the script-VM polling loop that normally blocks field movement during ASK. Phase 2b (chase wiring) will resolve this naturally since chase_ask_overlay already handles input gating during ASKs. Not addressed in v0.15.5.3. Files changed: src/dialog_inject.cpp (2 char changes + ~15 lines of new comment/rationale), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body), DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.5.2: Quick follow-up to v0.15.5.1 BAT. v0.15.5.1 BAT was a clean SUCCESS for Phase 2a's first press: Test #2 at 19:45:49 in doani1_2 saw opcode_ask return 1, gameObj.D2 acquire bit 2 (0x04), slot 2 transition advance 0 -> 0x400 -> 0x1000, state machine progress 0 -> 1 -> 0xD (ASK active with cursor), and the FieldDialog [ASK] hook fire on win[2] with 'Parsed 3 choices (firstQ=1 lastQ=3 curChoice=2)' speaking 'Selphie. "Wanna go up?". Selected: Go up. Stay'. Subsequent Shift+F12 presses correctly returned 5 (slot busy) since slot 2 was still locked. Empirical SWO_ASK arg map nailed: stack[SP-3]=arg2 -> slot+0x29 firstQ (got 1), stack[SP-2]=arg3 -> slot+0x2A lastQ (got 3), stack[SP-1]=arg4 -> slot+0x2C curQ (got 2, the cursor position; clamped to [firstQ, lastQ] by SWO_ASK's entry logic). Aaron didn't hear FF8's cursor-move SFX when pressing arrows during the open dialog because the .alloc branch we successfully reached does NOT call sub_49FD50; only the stage-1 setup path at 0x529683 does. Without sub_49FD50(slot), pCurrentDialogSlot (BYTE at 0x01D2B51C) is not pointing at our slot, so the engine's input handler doesn't send arrow keys to slot 2's curQ field. v0.15.5.2 fix: add sub_49FD50(slot) call before opcode_ask in Phase2_TestAsk. ~30 lines added (the call + pre/post pCurrentDialogSlot diagnostic logging + comment block). Hardcoded address 0x0049FD50 with __cdecl(int) signature, SEH-wrapped. Not promoted to FF8Addresses since sub_49FD50 is not in the JSM opcode dispatch table (no FFNx wrapping concern). Predicted v0.15.5.2 BAT outcome: clean save reload (slot 2 fresh), Shift+F12 press, opcode_ask returns 1 same as v0.15.5.1, dialog renders, but NOW pressing arrows moves the cursor and triggers FF8's cursor-move SFX -- the unambiguous 'this is a navigable menu' audio cue Aaron was looking for. Log shows 'sub_49FD50(2): pCurrentDialogSlot 0xFF -> 0x02' confirming the address resolved and was written. Files changed: src/dialog_inject.cpp (~30 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body), DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.5.1: Fix Phase 2a PARTIAL outcome from v0.15.5 BAT. v0.15.5 BAT confirmed Phase 1 still works perfectly (F12 -> opcode_mes returns 3, slot 1 trans 0->0x400->0x1000, gameObj.D3=D4=0x02, FieldDialog hook spoke the elevator dialog), but Phase 2a (Shift+F12 -> opcode_ask) returned 1 with slot 2 entirely untouched: trans=0x0000 vel=0x0000 state=0x0 field16=0x00, gameObj.D2=0x00 across all 28 polls, slot+0x29 firstQ=0xFF and +0x2A lastQ=0xFF (uninitialized). Diagnosis from extending the disassembly walk through 0x52956D-0x5296B6: opcode_ask checks two persistent script_context bytes ctx[+0x174] ("current pending ASK slot" tracker) and ctx[+0x175] ("ASK pending bitmask") via test al, dl where al=ctx[+0x175] and dl=1<<ctx[+0x174]. If the test result is zero (i.e., ctx[+0x175] doesn't have the bit at position ctx[+0x174] set), the je 0x529622 branches AWAY from the .alloc path that would call set_window_object_ASK. Instead it lands at the answer-correlation path which checks word [esi+0x204]: if 0 (initial), takes a side path at 0x529683 that calls sub_49FD50 / sub_49FD70 / sub_4A0660 (the third just sets slot.velocity to 0xFE00, none call SWO_ASK), increments [esi+0x204] to 1, and returns 1. That's exactly what v0.15.5 hit. The natural FF8 script-VM sets ctx[+0x174]/[+0x175] via a preparatory opcode before opcode_ask runs; our injected call skipped that opcode. v0.15.5.1 fix: add s_phantomCtx[0x174] = 0 and s_phantomCtx[0x175] = 1 BEFORE writing the script-stack args. With these set, cl=0, al=1, edx=1<<0=1, test al, dl = 1 & 1 = 1 (non-zero), je is NOT taken, execution falls through to the slot-busy check at 0x529588 (gameObj.D2 & (1<<slot)), then -- if D2 bit for our slot is clear (which v0.15.5 PRE confirmed for slot 2: D2=0x00) -- to .alloc at 0x5295AB. .alloc calls field_get_dialog_string for msg 0 of the current field, calls set_window_object_ASK(slot, text_ptr, arg2, arg3, arg4, arg5) with our four script-stack values, sets gameObj.D2 |= (1<<slot) at 0x529613, and returns 1. The slot is now populated with the elevator ASK text and the cursor range, and the engine renders it on subsequent frames. Implementation surface: ~30 lines added in dialog_inject.cpp (the two ctx-byte writes plus a documentation block explaining the 0x52956D-0x529582 test mechanic and a new log line confirming the values were set). NO changes to dialog_inject.h, dinput8.cpp, or any other file. Predicted v0.15.5.1 BAT outcome (replays Phase 2a in doani1_2): ret=1, slot 2 trans advances 0 -> 0x400 -> 0x1000 (matching Phase 1 MES pattern), gameObj.D2 acquires bit 2 (0x04) post-call, slot+0x29 firstQ and slot+0x2A lastQ acquire values from our four script-stack args (1/3/2/2 in some order), the existing FieldDialog [ASK] hook fires and SAPI speaks the elevator dialog with parsed choices, and the dialog renders visually. F11 screenshot post-fire confirms render. If SUCCESS: empirical map of which arg lands in slot+0x29/0x2A/0x2C is logged for v0.15.6 Phase 2b (custom text + answer detection + chase wiring). If still PARTIAL: the slot got populated but cursor doesn't track input, indicating the engine's input handler needs additional state we haven't mirrored (suspect: an entry in the ASK pending list elsewhere, or a slot-state byte not in our model). Both outcomes leave the empirical SWO_ASK arg map in the log either way -- the slot+0x29/0x2A/0x2C decode runs even on partial, so we learn something. Risk: very low. The two ctx bytes are persistent script_context state which the natural FF8 script-VM writes anyway during ASK preparation; we're just preempting that write with values matching what would naturally land there. The phantom ctx is still our private buffer, not FF8's real script_context, so no engine state is being overwritten externally. Files changed: src/dialog_inject.cpp (~30 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body), DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.5: Phase 2a -- experimental opcode_ask call. v0.15.4 BAT was complete success: the F12 fire of opcode_mes(&phantom_ctx) in field doani1_2 produced ret=3, the existing dialog hook spoke the field's msg 0 ("Selphie / Wanna go up? / Go up / Stay"), the slot poll showed +0x1C advancing 0 -> 0x400 (+15ms) -> 0x1000 (+125ms) and held, the state machine progressed 0 -> 1 -> 7, gameObj.D3/D4 bits set for slot 1, show_dialog callback fired for slot 1, and the F11 screenshot at 17:36:00 confirmed the dialog visually rendered indistinguishable from any natural FF8 MES. Recipe is unambiguously proven. Useful incidental finding: dispatch table opcode_mes value (0x649E57F0) differed from the cached value (0x00528F20) -- FFNx wraps these table entries; the defensive table-with-cached-fallback pattern correctly chained through FFNx. v0.15.5 ships Phase 2a: same recipe extended to opcode_ask. Adds Phase2_TestAsk to dialog_inject.cpp/.h, bound to Shift+F12 (Phase 1 stays on F12 alone per the F12 rule of one diagnostic per physical key state). Phantom script_context with SP=6 (opcode_ask pulls 6 args off the script-VM stack vs opcode_mes's 2). Stack layout from disassembly walk of opcode_ask body 0x00529520-0x005295D7: stack[SP-5] = slot index (CONFIRMED via the assertion check at 0x52955A: cmp edi, eax; jl .ok), stack[SP-4] = msg_id (CONFIRMED via mov eax, [esp+0x18] then push eax; call field_get_dialog_string at 0x5295CD), stack[SP-3] -> SWO_ASK arg2 (clamp lower bound), stack[SP-2] -> SWO_ASK arg3 (clamp upper bound, also written to slot+0x29 firstQ), stack[SP-1] -> SWO_ASK arg4 (clamped value, written to slot+0x2A lastQ), stack[SP] -> SWO_ASK arg5 (aux byte at slot+0x2B in slot-indexed aux table). Phase 2a target: slot 2 (avoids Phase 1's slot 1 to prevent gameObj.D2 collision -- the slot-busy gate at 0x529588-0x52959E rejects with ret=5 if (1<<slot) bit already set). msg_id = 0 (every field has msg 0; in Aaron's BAT field doani1_2 msg 0 is the Selphie elevator ASK with two choice lines). Phantom values: stack[SP-3]=1 (clamp lo), stack[SP-2]=3 (clamp hi/firstQ), stack[SP-1]=2 (lastQ), stack[SP]=2 (aux). Verification mirrors Phase 1: SEH-wrap the call, log return code (1=wait, 5=slot-busy, 3=advance, exception caught), pre/post snapshots of slot bytes (+0x1C trans, +0x1E vel, +0x24 state, +0x16 field16) and gameObj.D2/D3/D4 masks, post-fire decode of slot+0x29/0x2A/0x2C choice fields to empirically map the SWO_ASK args, 3-second slot poll at 100ms cadence, SAPI announces "Dialog inject phase two A. Slot N. Return code X." Phase 2a does NOT yet detect answer commit -- opcode_ask returns 1 (wait for answer) and the engine's input handler updates slot+0x2B (curQ) on arrows, but our injected call doesn't run the script-VM polling loop that reads the answer back from gameObj. Phase 2b (next ship -- v0.15.5.1 or v0.15.6) will layer on FF8 text encoding for custom prompt + options ("Manual / Auto / Original") and answer-detection polling, then wire into chase_ask_overlay::OpenAsk as the primary path. Three predicted BAT outcomes: (a) SUCCESS -- ret=1, dialog renders with cursor on "Go up" / "Stay", arrows move cursor (engine input handler picks up our slot), state machine progresses, slot poll shows trans 0 -> 0x1000. Empirical SWO_ASK arg map nailed via slot+0x29/0x2A/0x2C decode. (b) PARTIAL -- ret=1 but cursor doesn't render or input doesn't track. Means engine's ASK input handling needs additional state we haven't mirrored (likely script-VM ctx[+0x174/0x175] or a per-frame tracking var). (c) FAIL/CRASH -- SEH catches an exception, arg layout needs more decoding. Files changed: src/dialog_inject.h (~30 lines: header rewrite documenting Phases 1 + 2a, new Phase2_TestAsk decl). src/dialog_inject.cpp (~210 lines: Phase 2a constants block, AnnouncePhase2Result helper, full Phase2_TestAsk implementation with SEH-wrapped opcode_ask call and post-fire choice-field decode). src/dinput8.cpp (~10 lines: F12 handler now calls Phase1_TestMes by default and Phase2_TestAsk when shift held; comment block updates documenting the addition). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, push-quality body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.4: Engine-rendered dialog injection Phase 1. New module src/dialog_inject.{h,cpp} synthesizes a phantom script_context and calls opcode_mes(&ctx) directly to prove mod-driven engine dialog rendering. If Phase 1 succeeds (a dialog box renders from the F12 trigger), Phase 2 (chase ASK via opcode_ask) is mechanical. Background: v0.15.0 through v0.15.2.1 attempted to populate ff8_win_obj slot 1 with byte-perfect contents derived from a captured engine ASK snapshot. Every iteration ended with the engine ignoring the populated slot. The Field dialog system disassembly analysis follow-up section identifies why: show_dialog is registered as a per-slot callback via sub_4B6210/sub_4B6230 inside sub_4A0880 (window-system init) at engine startup, and externally-populated slots are never part of that registry. The gameObj+0xD2/0xD3/0xD4 bitmasks that v0.15.x targeted are per-slot allocation flags (used by script-VM opcodes to refuse double-allocation), NOT the render trigger. The deep research recommends Path A: synthesize a fake script_context and call opcode_mes(&ctx) / opcode_ask(&ctx) directly. Path A reuses the engine's full setup path verbatim, including the per-slot callback registration that triggers actual rendering. Phase 1 implementation: 0x300-byte zero-init phantom script_context buffer (covers ASK's [+0x204] write with margin); set ctx[0x184]=2 (SP byte), ctx[8]=0 (msg_id arg; every field has msg 0), ctx[4]=1 (slot index arg; slot 1 leaves slot 0 free for the engine's main MES). Verification (all automated for blind dev): (a) log opcode_mes return code (3=advance/success, 5=wait/slot-busy; exceptions caught and reported); (b) the existing v0.04.36 dialog hook fires on opcode_mes entry, so Aaron hears the dialog text via SAPI as the 'call entered' signal; (c) per-frame slot poll for 3 seconds at 100ms cadence logging pWindowsArray[1]+0x1C (open_close_transition), +0x1E (velocity), +0x24 (state), +0x16 (field16), and gameObj.D2/D3/D4 bitmasks; (d) SAPI announces 'Dialog inject phase one. Slot N. Return code X.' so Aaron immediately knows the result without checking logs. SEH wrap on the opcode_mes call to catch crashes from malformed phantom context. Field-mode guard (IsOnField()) and address-resolved guards before fire. F12 hotkey rebinding: replaces v0.15.0's ChaseDiag::Toggle binding. Per the F12 rule (only one diagnostic active on F12 at a time), the chase diagnostic is retired; chase chapter shipped end-to-end as v0.15.3 and ChaseDiag module remains in source but is no longer hot-keyed. If a future session needs chase-diag, it can be re-bound. Files changed: src/dialog_inject.h (NEW, ~80 lines: design rationale documenting Path A and why the bitmask recipe was outdated; public API). src/dialog_inject.cpp (NEW, ~280 lines: phantom ctx buffer, slot snapshot helpers, gameObj mask read helpers, Phase1_TestMes implementation with pre/post snapshots and SEH-wrapped opcode call, Update slot poll). src/dinput8.cpp (~10 lines: include dialog_inject.h, DialogInject::Initialize after ChaseBattleFreeze::Initialize, DialogInject::Update in main loop after ChaseDiag::Update, DialogInject::Shutdown before ChaseBattleFreeze::Shutdown, F12 handler swap from ChaseDiag::Toggle to DialogInject::Phase1_TestMes, comment block updates documenting the swap). src/deploy.bat (1 line: dialog_inject.cpp added to compile list after chase_battle_freeze.cpp). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.3: SINGLE-PRONGED CLEANUP per the v0.15.2.15 BAT decision criterion. v0.15.2.15 BAT result: SUCCESS end-to-end. Aaron made it through the entire X-ATM092 chase scene -- five mountain-trail fields (domt1_1..domt5_1), the bridge (doopen2a), Town Square (dotown_3), and the Lapin Beach FMV (disc00_07h.avi played all 8 audio descriptions cleanly across 74 seconds, control returned to dotown_2). One battle per field, no crashes, no hangs, no robot-walking-around. Cross-field signatures all clean: each chase field except doopen2a logged one [CBF] PASS + one [CHASE-AGENT] line with the dynamic agent pin holding the actual robot at zero-or-near-zero changed bytes; doopen2a logged the [CBF] PASS in doopen2a -- skipping RegisterChaseAgent line (NO [CHASE-AGENT] for that field, NO CHASE-AGENT FINAL SUMMARY block); fieldId-flip deactivation fired correctly on the doopen2a -> dotown_3 handoff (KaniFreeze: FREEZE DEACTIVATED -- fieldId changed 0x014D -> 0x0158 (pre-debounce)). The OTHERS-DIAG scanner data confirmed across every chase field that kani had at most 7 changed bytes and battleyarou had zero -- both static pins were inert because the actual chase agents were rinoa-slot in domt5_1, director0 in doopen2a, and various robot-slots in the trail fields, NOT the kani or battleyarou symbols the static pin was targeting. The decision criterion in DEVNOTES ("if [CHASE-AGENT] resolves AND CHASE-AGENT FINAL SUMMARY shows few/zero byte changes AND freeze# count is low for that field, the agent pin is sufficient") was satisfied for every chase field. v0.15.3 acts on that: the static kani+battleyarou pin in chase_kani_freeze.cpp is removed entirely. What goes: ReadKaniBlock helper, LogInitialSnapshot, LogChangeSummary, DiffAndLogFirstChanges per-tick diff loop, MID-WINDOW heartbeat, all s_kaniPtr/s_strideBytes/s_arrayKind/s_haveFullSnapshot/s_fullSnapshot/s_initial/s_prev/s_byteFirstChangeLogged state, all s_battleyarouPtr/s_battleyarouStrideBytes/s_battleyarouArrayKind/s_battleyarouInitial/s_haveBattleyarouSnapshot/s_battleyarouSnapshot state, the kani INITIAL/snapshot/memcpy/FINAL blocks in StartCapture/ApplyFreezePin/EndCapture, the battleyarou INITIAL/snapshot/memcpy/FINAL blocks in the same three functions, and the battleyarou cleanup lines in DeactivateFreeze. What stays: the dynamic chase-agent pin (RegisterChaseAgent + agent INITIAL/snapshot/memcpy/FINAL SUMMARY), the v0.15.2.14 fieldId-flip deactivation (ReadCurrentFieldId helper, s_freezeFieldId capture, raw-fieldId check before debounced-name check), the v0.15.2.9 OTHERS-DIAG diagnostic scanner (kept for future agent-resolution audits), the v0.15.2.3.1 capture trigger (s_battleSeenRecently mode 3->1 detection), the SEH probe pattern before agent writes. Net: chase_kani_freeze.cpp goes from ~700 lines to ~580 lines; the field log gets much quieter during chase battles (no per-tick FIRST CHANGE spam, no kani/battleyarou FINAL SUMMARY blocks); the per-frame cost in chase fields drops by two memcpys + one ReadKaniBlock per Update tick. chase_kani_freeze.h's design comment block is rewritten to document the v0.15.3 single-pronged design with v0.15.2.x history retained as terse trail. Initialize log line updated to "v0.15.3 DYNAMIC AGENT PIN ONLY" wording. ALSO bundled in v0.15.3: cosmetic fix to src/deploy.bat "Version: World" regex bug. The v0.15.2.x deploy log lines all printed "Deployment Complete -- Version: World" instead of "Version: 0.15.2.15" because the findstr /C:"FF8OPC_VERSION " pattern matched lines beyond the actual #define (the comment trail in ff8_accessibility.h contains lines mentioning FF8OPC_VERSION as part of prose, and the for /f loop reassigns VERSION on every iteration so the LAST match wins, with token 3 of one of those lines being "World"). Fix: tighten the findstr to /C:"#define FF8OPC_VERSION " so only the actual #define line matches. Drop the now-redundant ^| findstr /V "DATE" filter. The %%~V modifier strips quotes from "0.15.3" to give VERSION=0.15.3 in the deploy log. v0.15.2.14 dynamic agent pin design UNCHANGED. v0.15.2.14 tightened deactivation UNCHANGED. v0.15.2.15 doopen2a strcmp guard in chase_battle_freeze UNCHANGED. v0.15.2.x kani+battleyarou pin REMOVED. Predicted v0.15.3 BAT outcome: identical chase behavior to v0.15.2.15 (single-fight chase, robots stay down, dotown_3 transition succeeds, Lapin Beach FMV plays through to dotown_2), but field log is shorter and cleaner -- no INITIAL/FINAL kani hex dumps, no battleyarou hex dumps, no per-tick FIRST CHANGE lines, no MID-WINDOW heartbeat. CHASE-AGENT lines and OTHERS-DIAG block remain. Deploy log shows Version: 0.15.3 instead of Version: World. Risk: very low. The removed code paths only ran during chase-field battle exits, never wrote to entities outside their resolved kani/battleyarou pointers, and v0.15.2.15 BAT's OTHERS-DIAG already proved kani and battleyarou were inert -- removing the inert pins changes nothing the engine observes. Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (rewritten, ~580 lines down from ~700), src/chase_kani_freeze.h (design comment rewrite), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), src/deploy.bat (1 line: tighten findstr to #define-prefixed), CHANGELOG.md (top entry, ASCII-only body), DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.15: Surgical doopen2a fix on top of v0.15.2.14. v0.15.2.14 BAT confirmed five mountain-trail chase fields (domt1_1..domt5_1) all worked beautifully -- dynamic chase-agent pin held the robots down, auto-announce field name fired correctly, no crashes. Failure was in doopen2a (the bridge field, fieldId=0x014D, which Aaron called "town square" but is actually one field upstream of dotown_3). After the chase battle there, the field froze before transitioning to dotown_3. Music kept playing, no crash, no dotown_3 entry ever logged. Diagnosis from v0.15.2.14 BAT logs: CHASE-AGENT in doopen2a registered as director0 (Others slot 4, symIdx 9, 41 changed bytes pre-snapshot); cross-reference with v0.15.2.13 BAT confirmed director0 is the same entity (entityPtr=0x0188CA04) that called BATTLE. In doopen2a alone, the BATTLE caller and the chase-progress-tracker are the SAME entity -- the v0.15.2.10 deferred concern about director0 was prescient. Pinning director0's full state for the rest of the field session blocks the chase-end script from advancing to dotown_3 (director0 presumably waits on a flag/counter byte at one of the 41 offsets we're now overwriting every frame). v0.15.2.15 fix: chase_battle_freeze.cpp Hook_opcode_battle PASS branch now captures the debounced field name and wraps the RegisterChaseAgent call in a strcmp against "doopen2a". When the field IS doopen2a, log a single '[CBF] PASS in doopen2a -- skipping RegisterChaseAgent' line and skip the call; when it isn't, register as before. The BATTLE NO-OP gate (battleCount>=1) is unchanged and carries the load in doopen2a since that field has exactly one chase battle in the whole sequence. Other chase fields (domt1_1..domt5_1) keep the dynamic pin -- they don't share the "agent doubles as director" pattern and worked perfectly with the pin in v0.15.2.14. The static kani+battleyarou pin in chase_kani_freeze still runs in doopen2a (those entities are dormant per OTHERS-DIAG -- kani 7 changed bytes, battleyarou 0 -- so the pin is inert there). Implementation surface: ~30 lines in chase_battle_freeze.cpp (capture fieldName once, wrap RegisterChaseAgent in if/else, add <cstring> include for std::strcmp, update Initialize log line to v0.15.2.15 wording "skip register-agent in doopen2a only"); ~20 lines in chase_battle_freeze.h (new design rationale paragraph at top). v0.15.2.14 dynamic agent pin design otherwise UNCHANGED. v0.15.2.14 tightened deactivation (raw fieldId check + SEH probe) UNCHANGED. v0.15.2.14 field_announce auto-announce module UNCHANGED. v0.15.2.13/.12/.11/.10/.9/.8/.7/.6/.5/.4/.3.1/.3/.2/.1 mechanisms all preserved unchanged. Predicted v0.15.2.15 BAT outcome: each chase field except doopen2a logs one '[CBF] PASS' + one '[CHASE-AGENT]' line (same as v0.15.2.14); doopen2a logs one '[CBF] PASS in doopen2a -- skipping RegisterChaseAgent' line, NO '[CHASE-AGENT]' line for that field, no CHASE-AGENT FINAL SUMMARY block; a 'KaniFreeze: FREEZE DEACTIVATED -- fieldId changed 0x014D -> 0x0158 (pre-debounce)' line shortly after the doopen2a battle ends; dotown_3 cutscene plays through to the chase-end FMV without crashing. If the freeze recurs even with the pin disabled in doopen2a, the cause is something else (next candidates: the kani+battleyarou pin itself causing trouble in doopen2a, or the BATTLE NO-OP breaking the chase-end script). Risk: very low. The only behavioral change is one less per-frame memcpy in doopen2a; nothing else touches engine state differently. Files changed: src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (~30 lines), src/chase_battle_freeze.h (~20 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md (top entry, ASCII-only body), DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.14: Two-fix update plus auto-announce on field load. (1) DYNAMIC CHASE-AGENT PIN. v0.15.2.13 BAT confirmed traversal of the entire X-ATM092 chase but exposed two issues: (a) a crash ~16s after entering dotown_3 from doopen2a, same pattern as v0.15.2.10 -- the v0.15.2.11 dotown removal didn't actually fix the crash, just shifted the cause from 'pin tracking dotown_3 kani during cutscene' to 'pin still writing to old doopen2a kani pointer during the 2s name-debounce window after fieldId flip, while dotown_3 entities are being allocated into the same memory'; (b) the kani+battleyarou pin was hitting the wrong entities (kani=5 changed bytes, battleyarou=0 changes, but rinoa-slot Others slot 3 had 47 changes = the actual chase agent in domt5_1 wearing model 12). v0.15.2.13's BATTLE NO-OP suppressed combat but the agent was waking up and walking around following Squall silently. Aaron's design feedback: pin should keep the agent down on the ground, BATTLE NO-OP should be a safety net for when the pin misses. Implementation: chase_kani_freeze.h adds public RegisterChaseAgent(uintptr_t entityPtr) entry point. New module state s_chaseAgentPtr / Stride / ArrayKind / Slot / SymIdx / SymName / FieldName / Initial / Snapshot / haveAgentInitial / haveAgentSnapshot. RegisterChaseAgent resolves entityPtr to (arrayKind, slot, symIdx, symName) by reading pFieldStateOthers / pFieldStateBackgrounds bases via SEH-guarded deref, walking with the appropriate stride (0x264 / 0x1B4), checking offset modulo and slot range bounds. On success, logs structured '[CHASE-AGENT] field=X entityPtr=0xADDR -> array=Y slot=N symIdx=K sym=NAME stride=0xS header[0x00..0x10]: 16 hex bytes' with full per-field identity audit. On failure, logs '[CHASE-AGENT-UNRESOLVED]' with reason (JSMCounts unavailable, pointer outside both arrays, etc.) -- pin stays inactive, BATTLE NO-OP carries the load for that field. Idempotent within a field session. Writes identity fields first, then s_chaseAgentPtr last (atomic on x86, store-store reordering forbidden) so mod thread sees coherent armed view. StartCapture extension: if s_chaseAgentPtr armed, snapshot the agent's INITIAL state alongside kani/battleyarou and log a hex dump tagged AGENT-INIT. ApplyFreezePin extension: parallel SEH-guarded block snapshots the agent's full-state region (+0x140..stride) at SNAPSHOT_DELAY_MS=1500ms post-activation, then memcpy's the snapshot back over the agent every frame. EndCapture extension: CHASE-AGENT FINAL SUMMARY logs changed_bytes count + per-byte deltas vs INITIAL. Field-change deactivation clears all agent state. chase_battle_freeze.cpp Hook_opcode_battle calls ChaseKaniFreeze::RegisterChaseAgent in the PASS branch (battleCount==0), keeping the existing PASS log and adding the agent registration as a side effect. NO-OP gate (battleCount>=1) unchanged. (2) TIGHTENED DEACTIVATION (dotown_3 crash fix). chase_kani_freeze.cpp ApplyFreezePin previously deactivated only when GetDebouncedFieldName settled on a different non-empty name; the v0.15.2.4 design explicitly preserved the cached pointer through the 2s debounce window. With entity arrays getting freed and reallocated on the fieldId flip, that 2s window was the crash window. Fix: capture pCurrentFieldId at FREEZE ACTIVATED time (s_freezeFieldId, new state); read pCurrentFieldId every Update call (SEH-guarded ReadCurrentFieldId helper); if curFieldId differs from s_freezeFieldId, call DeactivateFreeze(reason) which clears all pin state including kani+battleyarou+agent. Existing debounced-name check preserved as backup. SEH-guarded probe read of one byte before each entity write to catch torn pointers (skip silently if the read faults). DeactivateFreeze is now a centralized helper consolidating clear logic that was scattered across ApplyFreezePin's deactivation branch in v0.15.2.13. (3) NEW MODULE: field_announce. Aaron's unrelated request -- automatically announce field display name when the party enters a new field. New files src/field_announce.{h,cpp} (~100 lines total). State: s_lastAnnouncedFieldId (0xFFFF init), s_observedFieldId, s_changeTick, s_pendingAnnounce. Update logic: gate on FF8Addresses::IsOnField() (mode==1 only); SEH-read pCurrentFieldId; on fieldId change reset debounce timer; after 800ms stable hold, look up FIELD_DISPLAY_NAMES[fieldId] (982-entry catalog from field_display_names.h) and call ScreenReader::Speak(name, false /*no interrupt*/). Skip conditions: fieldId==0 (title), fieldId>=982 (out of range), already-announced (no spam on battle/menu re-entry to same field). Wired into dinput8.cpp: include field_announce.h, FieldAnnounce::Initialize after FieldNavigation::Initialize, FieldAnnounce::Update in main loop after FieldNavigation::Update, FieldAnnounce::Shutdown before FieldNavigation::Shutdown. Risk surfaces: (a) agent pin could pin the wrong entity if RegisterChaseAgent resolves an unexpected slot -- mitigated by the structured [CHASE-AGENT] log line which surfaces identity in plain text for audit; (b) field_announce could over-fire on rapid scripted cuts -- mitigated by the 800ms debounce + lastAnnouncedFieldId guard; (c) new SEH probe read in ApplyFreezePin adds a tiny per-frame cost (~one MOV instruction) -- negligible. (d) RegisterChaseAgent runs on the game thread while ApplyFreezePin runs on the mod thread; pointer write is atomic on x86 and ordered LAST after identity fields are populated, so no torn-state hazard. Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.h (rewritten ~70 lines: new RegisterChaseAgent decl, full v0.15.2.14 design rationale documenting the three-pronged design [static pin, dynamic agent pin, tightened deactivation]). src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (rewritten ~700 lines: new state vars block for agent, ClearChaseAgent helper, ReadCurrentFieldId helper, DeactivateFreeze centralized helper, RegisterChaseAgent implementation with array/slot resolution arithmetic and structured logging, StartCapture extension for AGENT-INIT, ApplyFreezePin three-pin block with SEH probe + fieldId deactivation, EndCapture extension for CHASE-AGENT FINAL SUMMARY, Initialize/Shutdown clear including ClearChaseAgent). src/chase_battle_freeze.h (rewritten ~50 lines: v0.15.2.14 role pivot from primary suppression to safety-net + agent identifier, audit trail of v0.15.2.13 BAT findings preserved). src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (rewritten ~135 lines: include chase_kani_freeze.h, RegisterChaseAgent call in PASS branch, Initialize log line updated to v0.15.2.14, Shutdown summary notes 'NO-OP count should be low when pin healthy'). src/field_announce.{h,cpp} (NEW, ~30 + ~110 lines). src/dinput8.cpp (~20 lines: include field_announce.h, FieldAnnounce::Initialize/Update/Shutdown wired in, ChaseKaniFreeze + ChaseBattleFreeze comment blocks rewritten to v0.15.2.14 design). src/deploy.bat (1 line: field_announce.cpp added to compile list after chase_battle_freeze.cpp). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry, ASCII-only body). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.13: Flip chase_battle_freeze.cpp from PASSIVE OBSERVER to ACTIVE FREEZE based on the v0.15.2.12 BAT result. v0.15.2.12 BAT in domt5_1 produced three '[CBF]' lines at 11:51:32 / 11:53:21 / 11:54:47 -- one per chase battle, all with caller=other and identical entityPtr=0x0188C5D8, paired one-to-one with 'ChaseDetector battle entered' lines (count=1, 2, 3). opcode_battle FIRES for every chase battle in domt5_1; the v0.15.2.2 'opcode_battle is dead in domt5_1' finding was wrong (v0.15.1's pass-through logger sampled every-50th calls only and the freeze branch only logged kani-driven calls, so non-kani chase BATTLE calls were essentially invisible). entityPtr 0x0188C5D8 = othersBase(0x0188BEAC) + 3*0x264 = Others slot 3 = symIdx 7 = 'rinoa' SYM entry. Same-window OTHERS-DIAG showed rinoa-slot had changed_bytes=47/612 (second-most-active entity in domt5_1) and the prior TALKRAD line for that pointer had model=12 (kani's robot model per SETMODEL). The rinoa-slot is the actual chase agent in domt5_1, wearing model 12 -- not the kani-slot (slot 8, only the 5 pinned bytes changed) or battleyarou-slot (slot 10, 0 changes). v0.15.2.8/9/10 had been pinning the wrong two entities the whole time in domt5_1; the pin worked perfectly but missed the agent. v0.15.2.13 fixes this with a simple BATTLE-opcode NO-OP that doesn't depend on knowing which entity is the agent. Implementation: chase_battle_freeze.cpp Hook_opcode_battle reactivates the freeze branch with gate (mode==MANUAL && IsInChaseField() && GetCurrentFieldBattleCount()>=1), returns JSM_RC_ADVANCE=3 without calling s_origBattle. Caller-identity check (kani/battleyarou) DROPPED -- still computed for log tagging only. The first chase battle per field still passes through so the scripted opening encounter fires; only the second-and-later calls are NO-OP'd. Same mechanism v0.15.1 used successfully in domt4_1, just with a broader gate. Per-call logging tagged 'NO-OP' or 'PASS' so the next BAT can verify the freeze pattern. New s_freezeCount global tracks NO-OP count for Shutdown summary. chase_battle_freeze.h header rewritten to document v0.15.2.13 design rationale and include the v0.15.2.12 BAT findings inline so future maintainers don't repeat the v0.15.2.2 misinterpretation. dinput8.cpp Initialize/Shutdown comment blocks updated to reflect ACTIVE FREEZE role. ChaseKaniFreeze kani+battleyarou pin stays in place as belt-and-suspenders -- harmless when those entities aren't the chase agent (e.g. domt5_1 rinoa-slot case) and may still help in fields where kani-slot IS the agent (domt4_1 per v0.15.2.5/.6/.7 BAT data). Risk: very low. (1) No entity bytes touched, so the v0.15.2.11 dotown_3 cutscene fix is not at risk. (2) Returning JSM advance code (3) without calling original is exactly what v0.15.1 did in domt4_1 and that worked. (3) Aaron's gameplay confirmation: there are no random encounters during the chase scene, so the 'cap at 1 battle per chase field' policy doesn't suppress legitimate non-chase battles. (4) The v0.15.2.12 BAT showed Aaron got stuck in domt5_1 with three battles and no forward progress -- this build directly addresses that failure mode. Predicted v0.15.2.13 BAT outcome: enter domt5_1, fight battle #1 normally (PASS log line), walk toward field exit, kani/rinoa-slot collides, opcode_battle fires but is NO-OP'd (NO-OP log line), Squall continues walking, exits to next chase field (probably domt4_1), same pattern repeats. End result: traverse entire chase scene through Lapin Beach FMV. v0.15.2.14+ cleanup if BAT confirms working: consider deleting (or ignoring) the now-redundant kani+battleyarou pin since the BATTLE NO-OP makes it functionally unnecessary. doopen2a 'second chase battle' issue (v0.15.2.10 deferred concern about director0) is ALSO solved by this fix without ever needing to pin director0 -- v0.15.2.13 caps doopen2a at 1 battle the same way it caps every other chase field. v0.15.2.11 dotown removal preserved, v0.15.2.10 domt1_1 chase coverage preserved, v0.15.2.9 OTHERS-DIAG scanner preserved, v0.15.2.8 dual-entity pin preserved (now just defensive), all earlier kani-pin layers preserved. Files changed: src/chase_battle_freeze.h (rewritten ~50 lines: full v0.15.2.13 design rationale with v0.15.2.12 BAT findings included). src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (rewritten ~145 lines: re-enabled freeze branch, dropped caller-identity gate, added s_freezeCount, updated PASS/NO-OP log tags, updated Initialize/Shutdown log lines). src/dinput8.cpp (~14 lines: Initialize comment block describes ACTIVE FREEZE role, Shutdown comment label updated). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.12: Reactivate chase_battle_freeze.cpp as a PASSIVE OBSERVER (hook installed, every call inside a chase field logged, ALWAYS passes through to original handler). Reason: v0.15.2.10 BAT confirmed doopen2a fires a second chase battle even with kani+battleyarou pinned. director0 (31 changes/612 in clean OTHERS-DIAG at 22:03:34) is the prime suspect for the second battle, but before pinning a third entity (which carries risk if director0 turns out to be the chase-progress-tracker) we want empirical data on whether suppressing BATTLE at the opcode level would even work for doopen2a. v0.15.2.2 BAT proved opcode_battle is DEAD CODE in domt2_1 and domt5_1 (ChaseDetector saw both battles via game-mode 1->3 polling but ZERO ChaseBattleFreeze hits) and LIVE in domt4_1 (NO-OP fired correctly). doopen2a was never instrumented because v0.15.2.3 onwards switched to entity-pin approach and orphaned chase_battle_freeze.{h,cpp} (left on disk, removed from build). v0.15.2.12 brings the file back as observation-only: rewrites chase_battle_freeze.cpp so Hook_opcode_battle ALWAYS calls s_origBattle and never short-circuits; emits '[CBF] chase BATTLE call #N (total #M) field=X mode=Y battleCount=Z caller=kani|battleyarou|other entityPtr=0xADDR' for every call inside ChaseDetector::IsInChaseField()=true so we can correlate with OTHERS-DIAG entity addresses; outside chase fields silent (no random-encounter spam). Three predicted BAT outcomes: (A) FIRES-BOTH: two [CBF] lines in doopen2a (battleCount=0 and battleCount=1) -- Strategy 1 (NO-OP second+ call) is viable, v0.15.2.13 ships the active freeze. (B) FIRES-FIRST-ONLY: one [CBF] line for first battle, none for the second that ChaseDetector still observes -- same dead-code pattern as domt2_1/domt5_1, move to Strategy 2 (targeted byte pin on director0's changing offsets) or Strategy 3 (full director0 freeze, riskiest). (C) FIRES-NEITHER: no [CBF] lines but ChaseDetector reports battles -- opcode_battle fully dead in doopen2a, same conclusion as B. Risk: ZERO -- the hook never modifies engine behavior. v0.15.2.11 dotown removal preserved, v0.15.2.10 domt1_1 chase coverage preserved, v0.15.2.9 OTHERS-DIAG scanner preserved, v0.15.2.8 dual-entity (kani+battleyarou) pin preserved, all earlier kani-pin layers preserved. Files changed: src/chase_battle_freeze.h (rewritten ~30 lines: design rationale for v0.15.2.12 passive observer role, public API unchanged). src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp (rewritten ~140 lines: removed freeze branch entirely, removed pass-through periodic logging, added per-call chase-field logging with kani/battleyarou caller identification via ChaseDetector::IsKaniEntityPtr/IsBattleyarouEntityPtr, kept SEH-guarded address read and MinHook install, updated Initialize log line to v0.15.2.12, added total/chase counters to Shutdown log line). src/deploy.bat (1 line: chase_battle_freeze.cpp added back to compile list after chase_kani_freeze.cpp). src/dinput8.cpp (~12 lines: include chase_battle_freeze.h, ChaseBattleFreeze::Initialize call after ChaseKaniFreeze::Initialize, ChaseBattleFreeze::Shutdown call before ChaseKaniFreeze::Shutdown). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.11: Remove dotown_3, dotown_2, dotown_1 from CHASE_FIELD_NAMES[]. v0.15.2.10 BAT crashed/hung ~16 seconds after entering dotown_3 from doopen2a (Fri 2026-05-08 22:05:13–22:05:31). Aaron's diagnosis: dotown_3's chase-end cutscene plays an animation where X-ATM092 (kani) walks across the town square and shorts out, driven by the dotown_3 kani entity in Backgrounds slot 1. With dotown_3 in CHASE_FIELD_NAMES, our chase_kani_freeze module kept tracking dotown_3's kani address and — if a mode 4→1 transition fired during the cutscene — would StartCapture and pin the dotown_3 kani's anim ID bytes (+0x150/+0x154/+0x1FA/+0x23F/+0x241), directly fighting the cutscene's animation script every frame. v0.15.2.9 BAT didn't crash on this transition because timing happened to skip the StartCapture trigger; v0.15.2.10 got unlucky. dotown_3 is the chase END field — there are no kani battles there; the chase is over. Same logic applies to dotown_2 and dotown_1 (post-chase town fields). Removing all three from the chase set means: (1) ChaseDetector reports chase DEACTIVATED on entering dotown_3, (2) chase_kani_freeze.StartCapture won't fire there (IsInChaseField()=false), (3) any leftover pin state already deactivated cleanly via the field-change branch, (4) the dotown_3 cutscene plays unimpeded. The doopen2a 'second chase battle' issue (kani+battleyarou pinned but battle still triggers via director0, 31 changes/612 in clean OTHERS-DIAG at 22:03:34) is DEFERRED to v0.15.2.12 — fixing one issue at a time, and the director0 pin carries its own risk of breaking the chase-end logic since director0 might be the chase-progress-tracker. Ship dotown removal first, verify no crash, then evaluate director0 pin separately. domt5_1 OTHERS-DIAG (clean, 18 slots, in-field at 21:53:20) provided this BAT's other major datum: every Director candidate (plane1, dic, onkyou, Garutyan, liti, gura, saidotoujou, Gakekuzure) shows 0/612 changes — the previous hypothesis that one of these was the chase agent in domt5_1 is FALSE. Active entities in domt5_1 are all party members: selphie2 (73), irvine (64), rinoa (47), zell2 (31). But the kani+battleyarou pin worked in domt5_1 (only one chase battle fired, no second-battle issue), so the chase IS triggered by kani contact in domt5_1; the party members are running normal chase-cutscene scripts (Selphie/Irvine running animations, dialogue triggers) that don't trigger battles. domt1_1 chase coverage from v0.15.2.10 confirmed working: log line at 21:59:51 'battle entered ... field=domt1_1 chaseActive=1 count=1' (was chaseActive=0 in v0.15.2.9). Risk: minimal. The change is a deletion of three array entries. ChaseDetector.IsInChaseField() returns false earlier (on entry to dotown_3 instead of on entry to lapin beach FMV); chase_ask_overlay's already-fired flag stays true since the ASK plays at chase START in domt4_1 (long before reaching dotown_3); no other modules rely on chase-active state in dotown_x fields. Files changed: src/chase_detector.cpp (~5 lines: array entries removed + comment block extended). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.10: Add 'domt1_1' to the chase-field set in chase_detector.cpp. v0.15.2.9 BAT (Fri 2026-05-08 ~20:40-20:58) showed Aaron fought 4 battles: chase battle in domt4_1 at 20:46:11 (kani pin worked), TWO battles in domt1_1 at 20:52:15 and 20:53:33 with chaseActive=0 (NOT in chase set!), and chase battle in doopen2a at 20:55:27 (transition-contaminated capture). Per Aaron's clarification "no random encounters during the chase scene," those domt1_1 battles were chase-robot fights that escaped the kani pin entirely because domt1_1 wasn't in CHASE_FIELD_NAMES[]. Added "domt1_1" as the first entry; reordered the array to ascending mountain-trail numbering for readability (domt1_1 → domt2_1 → domt3_2 → domt4_1 → domt5_1) followed by bridge/town fields. The doopen2a OTHERS-DIAG capture in v0.15.2.9 BAT was contaminated by the field transition (doopen2a → dotown_3 fired during the 10s window) — kani's full state went 0xFF→0x00 across many bytes from engine teardown, inflating director0/director1/dog/jumptotown0 byte counts to 100+ each (deallocation noise, not active script behavior). domt5_1 — the field where v0.15.2.8 "Still getting up" was reported — was NOT visited in this BAT, so the all-Others scanner data we need is still pending. v0.15.2.10 BAT plan: drive Squall through the chase scene starting from domt5_1; verify the kani pin now activates in domt1_1 (chaseActive=1, FREEZE ACTIVATED log line). The OTHERS-DIAG output for domt5_1 will be the next decision point. Risk: zero — adding a field name to a string array activates more chase logic, never less; v0.14.x and earlier behavior in non-chase contexts unchanged. Files changed: src/chase_detector.cpp (~5 lines: array reordered + domt1_1 added + comment update). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.9: All-Others diagnostic scanner. v0.15.2.8 BAT in domt5_1 confirmed BOTH kani (slot 8) AND battleyarou (slot 10) are dormant: kani INITIAL has substantial state (+0x230/+0x250 byte arrays) but FINAL SUMMARY changed_bytes=0/612 — entity is allocated but its state never changes during the 10s post-battle window. Battleyarou INITIAL is almost entirely zero (just +0x020=0x09) and FINAL SUMMARY changed_bytes=0/612. Yet ChaseDetector logged 'battle entered ... count=3' at 20:21:32. Both pins are working perfectly (no byte changes during the window) but the chase battle still triggers from a different mechanism. Candidates from domt5_1 JSMScan (Others slots 4..21): dic, onkyou, plane1 (Director with REQ opcodes and dialog targets), liti, gura, saidotoujou — or potentially a SETLINE LineEvent trigger zone (JSMScan reports LineEvent=1 for domt5_1) that fires battle from Squall walking across an invisible line. v0.15.2.9 ships an all-Others diagnostic scanner: at StartCapture, snapshots ALL Others slots' INITIAL state (up to MAX_OTHERS=32 slots, ~20KB buffer s_othersInitial[MAX_OTHERS][MAX_STRIDE]). At EndCapture, computes per-slot changed_bytes count vs INITIAL and logs 'OTHERS-DIAG slot=N symIdx=K sym=X changed_bytes=K/612' for every slot. Slots with changed_bytes > 0 during the 10s window are running scripts/AI/anim and are candidate chase agents; slot with most changes is prime suspect. NO PIN at this stage — diagnostic only. v0.15.2.10 will pin whichever slot the data identifies. Implementation: (1) chase_detector.h adds GetSymName(int idx) and GetSymNameCount() public functions; chase_detector.cpp implements them by indexing into the existing s_symNames[MAX_SYM_NAMES][32] cache populated by FieldArchive::LoadSYMNames in OnDebouncedFieldChange. (2) chase_kani_freeze.cpp adds #include 'field_archive.h', new state vars MAX_OTHERS=32, s_othersCountSnapshot, s_othersBaseSnapshot, s_othersStartSymIdx, s_othersInitial[32][612]. StartCapture (after the existing kani/battleyarou capture blocks): calls FieldArchive::LoadJSMCounts to get doors/lines/bgs/others, computes othersStartSymIdx = doors+lines+bgs, dereferences pFieldStateOthers, snapshots up to wantCount=min(others, MAX_OTHERS) slots via SEH-guarded memcpy at base + slot*STRIDE_OTHER, logs 'OTHERS-DIAG snapshot taken: N/M slots captured, othersStartSymIdx=K, othersBase=0xPPPPPPPP'. EndCapture (after the existing kani+battleyarou FINAL summaries): for each snapshotted slot, SEH-guarded memcpy current state, compute changed_bytes count vs s_othersInitial[slot], look up sym name via ChaseDetector::GetSymName(othersStartSymIdx + slot), log per-slot result and overall 'N/M slots had byte changes'. Field-change deactivation, Initialize, Shutdown all reset the new state vars. Initialize log line updated to v0.15.2.9 wording. Predicted v0.15.2.9 BAT outcomes in domt5_1: (A) ENTITY-DRIVEN-CASE: one or two Others slots show high changed_bytes counts (likely 50-200 bytes). The slot with the most changes is the chase agent. v0.15.2.10 generalizes the pin infrastructure to cover that entity. (B) NO-ENTITY-CASE: all Others slots show changed_bytes=0/612 (every entity is dormant). The chase battle is not entity-driven; it must come from a SETLINE trigger zone (LineEvent=1 in domt5_1's JSMScan), a scripted timer in the field's script-VM main loop, or some other mechanism we haven't traced. v0.15.2.10 would investigate: hook the SETLINE entity's Line script to intercept battle triggers, OR hook an alternate battle-start function (the chase_battle_freeze module previously found opcode_battle dead in these fields). (C) MIXED-CASE: a few slots show small changed_bytes counts (1-10 bytes) — likely heartbeat/idle counters, not chase scripts. Combined with no big standout, this also points to NO-ENTITY-CASE. The diagnostic data tells us which path forward. Risk: the scanner is read-only, no behavior change beyond v0.15.2.8 — kani and battleyarou pins still active and unchanged. domt4_1 should still work identically to v0.15.2.8. Files changed: src/chase_detector.h (~7 lines: 2 new function decls + comment). src/chase_detector.cpp (~13 lines: 2 new function implementations). src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~120 lines: new include, new state vars block with comment, StartCapture all-Others snapshot block, EndCapture all-Others FINAL summary block, deactivation cleanup, Initialize log line + reset, Shutdown reset). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.2.8: Dual-entity pin (kani + battleyarou) per v0.15.2.7 BAT data. v0.15.2.7 BAT in domt5_1 ran the full 10s diagnostic and produced FINAL SUMMARY changed_bytes=0/612 — ZERO byte changes in kani's entire entity over 10 seconds, but battle still triggered (count=3 at 19:47:33). MID-WINDOW heartbeat at t=5000ms confirmed diff_this_tick=0 total_changed_so_far=0. The resolved kani entity is dead code in domt5_1; the chase battle comes from a different entity entirely. Aaron also clarified there are no random encounters during the chase scene, so the battle MUST come from another entity in the field. Top candidate: 'battleyarou' (Japanese for "battle guy") which has the same 3-method JSM signature as kani in BOTH domt4_1 (grp[7] raw=0x0F03 methods=3) and domt5_1 (grp[14] cat=3 type=Interactive Object), and appears as Interactive Object with no position (param=-1) in domt5_1's JSMScan — runtime-driven entity, classic chase-agent profile. v0.15.2.8 implementation: (1) ChaseDetector (chase_detector.h/cpp): added GetBattleyarouEntityPtr(), IsBattleyarouEntityPtr(ptr), GetBattleyarouLocation() public functions. New static s_battleyarouLoc cache. Refactored existing ResolveKaniLocation into generic ResolveEntityLocation(targetSym, fieldName, logTag, outLoc) helper; ResolveKaniLocation and new ResolveBattleyarouLocation are thin wrappers calling the generic. ResolveLocPtr static helper used by both Get*EntityPtr() functions to walk pFieldStateBackgrounds/pFieldStateOthers via JSM-counts arithmetic (doorsLines = doors+lines, bgStart = doorsLines, othersStart = doorsLines+backgrounds). OnDebouncedFieldChange now calls ResolveBattleyarouLocation in addition to ResolveKaniLocation on every field transition. Initialize resets s_battleyarouLoc. (2) chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~150 lines): new state vars s_battleyarouPtr, s_battleyarouStrideBytes, s_battleyarouArrayKind, s_battleyarouInitial[612], s_haveBattleyarouSnapshot, s_battleyarouSnapshot[612]. StartCapture: after the existing kani INITIAL hex dump, resolves battleyarou via GetBattleyarouEntityPtr/GetBattleyarouLocation, captures INITIAL state via SEH-guarded memcpy, logs BATTLEYAROU INITIAL hex dump (16 bytes per row, BYOU-INIT label). If battleyarou is absent (symIdx<0 or arraySlot<0), logs single-line skip and continues; battleyarou pin will be inert for that field, kani-only behavior identical to v0.15.2.7. ApplyFreezePin: NEW second SEH-guarded block after the existing kani pin block. Snapshot battleyarou at t=SNAPSHOT_DELAY_MS=1500ms via memcpy from s_battleyarouPtr+0x140 (length = s_battleyarouStrideBytes - 0x140 bytes) into s_battleyarouSnapshot, set s_haveBattleyarouSnapshot=true, log. After snapshot taken, memcpy s_battleyarouSnapshot back to s_battleyarouPtr+0x140 every frame. NO belt-and-suspenders byte writes for battleyarou — kani's 0x21 and 0x14 magic values are kani-specific (derived from kani's BAT data, +0x150 anim ID byte etc.); applying them blindly to battleyarou could corrupt its state. Snapshot-only is safer. Field-change deactivation clears s_haveBattleyarouSnapshot, s_battleyarouPtr, s_battleyarouStrideBytes, s_battleyarouArrayKind in addition to the v0.15.2.7 kani state. EndCapture: parallel BATTLEYAROU FINAL SUMMARY block after the existing kani FINAL SUMMARY — SEH-guarded memcpy of current battleyarou state, byte-by-byte comparison against s_battleyarouInitial, list every changed byte with delta. Per-tick FIRST CHANGE diff is NOT done for battleyarou (would double the log volume); INITIAL/FINAL pair is enough to answer (a) is battleyarou active in this field? (b) did the pin hold? Initialize/Shutdown reset all new state vars. Initialize log line updated to v0.15.2.8 wording. FREEZE ACTIVATED log line unchanged (kani-focused; the new battleyarou-related lines are emitted separately). Predicted v0.15.2.8 BAT outcomes: (A) GOOD-CASE: in domt5_1, BATTLEYAROU INITIAL has non-zero state (entity is active); BATTLEYAROU FINAL SUMMARY shows few-to-zero changed bytes (pin held); ChaseDetector battle entered count remains <2 (no second battle). Aaron observes: no chase battle in domt5_1, no audible kani movement. Hypothesis confirmed; v0.15.3 cleans up. (B) PARTIAL-CASE: in domt5_1, BATTLEYAROU INITIAL shows non-zero state (active entity), but the FINAL SUMMARY shows many changes — our pin isn't winning the write race. Investigate hook ordering or alternate pin offsets. (C) WRONG-CANDIDATE-CASE: in domt5_1, BATTLEYAROU INITIAL shows all zeros (battleyarou is also dead code there). Battle still triggers from yet another entity. v0.15.2.9 will try the next candidate (plane1 Director, dic, onkyou, gura, or saidotoujou — all Interactive Objects in domt5_1's JSMScan). The diagnostic data tells us which case we're in. Risk: pinning battleyarou could break field functionality if battleyarou is involved in scripted events outside the chase (e.g. cutscene triggers). Mitigation: pin only kicks in after a chase-field battle exit (StartCapture trigger), not on initial field entry; pin clears on field change. So battleyarou's pre-battle behavior (which presumably includes any cutscene triggers) is unaffected. Files changed: src/chase_detector.h (~12 lines: 3 new function decls, 1 new struct comment). src/chase_detector.cpp (~75 lines: refactored resolver, parallel ResolveBattleyarouLocation, new s_battleyarouLoc, OnDebouncedFieldChange addition, Initialize reset, ResolveLocPtr helper, GetBattleyarouEntityPtr / IsBattleyarouEntityPtr / GetBattleyarouLocation public functions). src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~150 lines: new state vars block, StartCapture battleyarou block, EndCapture battleyarou FINAL summary block, ApplyFreezePin parallel battleyarou pin block, deactivation cleanup, Initialize/Shutdown reset, header comment trail extended). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.2.7: Brute-force full-state pin per Aaron's design pivot. v0.15.2.6 BAT in domt4_1 prevented kani collision (no battle) but Aaron heard kani's running animation playing in place — the +0x150 anim ID pin doesn't actually drive rendered animation; the engine reads other unpinned bytes (+0x162 candidate, anim shadows, etc.) for actual playback. Aaron's design preference: "keep the robot from getting up, rather than locking it in place when it does." v0.15.2.7 implements this by snapshotting kani's full post-header state region (+0x140..stride, ~292 bytes for Others stride 0x264) at t=1500ms post-FREEZE-ACTIVATED — well past Phase A re-init last write at t=765ms (per v0.15.2.5 BAT timing) and well before Phase C wakeup at t=5375ms. Then memcpy that snapshot back over every frame in ApplyFreezePin. Every byte the engine would otherwise modify to drive wakeup — anim playback drivers, AI state bytes, position regions, collision flags, sub-state mirrors, all the unidentified bytes — stays at "down and settled" forever. Header bytes (+0x000..+0x028) NOT pinned so the engine's heartbeat/frame counter at +0x028 still ticks (avoid tripping engine life-detection). Subsumes v0.15.2.6's three-region position pin (those regions are inside +0x140..stride). Belt-and-suspenders byte pins on +0x150/+0x23F/+0x241=0x21 and +0x154/+0x1FA=0x14 preserved during the t<1500ms grace period so kani stays down through Phase A; once snapshot kicks in the memcpy overwrites those with the same values. Implementation: new state vars s_haveFullSnapshot (bool), s_fullSnapshot[MAX_STRIDE] (612 bytes), s_freezeStartTick (DWORD). Constants SNAPSHOT_DELAY_MS=1500, SNAPSHOT_OFFSET_START=0x140. StartCapture sets s_freezeStartTick=GetTickCount() if !s_haveFullSnapshot (only on first activation per field). ApplyFreezePin: if !s_haveFullSnapshot && elapsed >= SNAPSHOT_DELAY_MS, memcpy from kani+0x140 (snapLen=stride-0x140 bytes) into s_fullSnapshot, set flag, log; else if s_haveFullSnapshot, memcpy from s_fullSnapshot to kani+0x140. Field-change deactivation clears s_haveFullSnapshot AND s_freezeStartTick so next chase field gets a fresh post-Phase-A snapshot for its own geometry. Initialize/Shutdown reset new state. v0.15.2.6 position-pin state vars (s_havePinnedPosition, s_pinnedPos_140/190/1B4) all removed. Risk: pinning ~292 bytes including unidentified ones could trip unexpected engine behavior. Mitigation: header (heartbeat) excluded; if engine-life-detection logic uses post-header bytes too, kani might appear "hung" to the engine — which is what we want anyway (he should be incapacitated for entire chase scene, exit via the Lapin Beach FMV which is position-independent). Aaron confirmed there are no random encounters during the chase scene so we don't need to worry about random-encounter dependencies on kani state. Predicted v0.15.2.7 BAT outcomes: GOOD = no battle, no audible kani movement in domt4_1; FINAL SUMMARY shows only +0x028 heartbeat changed. PARTIAL = no battle but some other audible artifact (e.g., a sound triggered by a header byte we don't pin). FAILED = battle still triggers — means kani isn't the chase entity in the field (especially relevant for domt5_1 where the v0.15.2.6 BAT showed all-zero kani entity at our resolved address). For domt5_1-style fields where kani is dead code, the BAT log will show the full-state snapshot of all-zeros, and any battle that fires comes from a different entity (likely battleyarou — same 3-method signature appears in both fields). Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~120 lines: new state vars, new SNAPSHOT_DELAY_MS/SNAPSHOT_OFFSET_START constants, StartCapture rewrite for new snapshot timing, ApplyFreezePin rewrite with delayed snapshot + full memcpy + heartbeat-preserving offset start, header comment trail extended with v0.15.2.7 design pivot, FREEZE ACTIVATED + Initialize log line updates, Shutdown reset). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.2.6: Layer position pin on top of v0.15.2.5's full sub-state pin. v0.15.2.5 BAT in domt4_1 (build 18:49:39, captures at 18:53:36 and 18:54:47) proved the full sub-state pin held perfectly: FINAL SUMMARY of capture #1 had 17 changed bytes vs v0.15.2.4's 19, with +0x150, +0x154, +0x1FA, +0x23F, +0x241 ALL ABSENT from the changed-bytes list. Capture #2 INITIAL +0x154 = 0x14 (vs v0.15.2.4 BAT's 0x0C) confirmed the engine never successfully decremented our pinned bytes. BUT kani still triggered battle #2 (count=2 at 18:53:43, 7s into capture #1) and battle #3 (count=3 at 18:54:53, 6s into capture #2). Diagnosis: kani has TWO independent wakeup paths. The +0x154 sub-state countdown is decoupled from the AI/movement subsystem. Phase C onset (~5400ms) is timer-driven, not state-gated. Position bytes (+0x140-0x148 X/Y/Z primary, +0x190-0x199 secondary copy, +0x1B5-0x1BD tertiary copy) drift at Phase C regardless of whether sub-state is pinned. v0.15.2.6 fix: ApplyFreezePin now also writes 12-byte position snapshots back every frame for three regions: +0x140 (X/Y/Z dwords), +0x190 (second copy), +0x1B4 (third copy). Snapshot taken on FIRST freeze activation per field via memcpy from kani+offset, NOT on subsequent captures — we want capture #1's INITIAL spawn position (-995, 3562, 230 in domt4_1), NOT capture #2's INITIAL which carries kani's chase-end position from prior battle. New state vars s_havePinnedPosition (bool), s_pinnedPos_140/190/1B4 (12 bytes each). Snapshot in StartCapture if !s_havePinnedPosition; SEH-guarded read with diagnostic log line on success and on read failure. Field-change deactivation in ApplyFreezePin clears s_havePinnedPosition so next field gets fresh snapshot. Initialize and Shutdown reset the new state. Position pin only writes if s_havePinnedPosition is true (defensive). Risk: pinning kani's coordinates may produce visual rendering glitch (kani standing still in chase scene) but is functionally safe — chase scene ends via Lapin Beach FMV trigger which is position-independent (scripted on Squall reaching field exit). Aaron is blind so visual glitch doesn't affect UX. Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~50 lines: 4 new state vars, snapshot block in StartCapture, ApplyFreezePin position memcpys + s_havePinnedPosition clear in deactivate branch, Initialize/Shutdown reset, FREEZE ACTIVATED + Initialize log line updates, ApplyFreezePin header comment trail). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.2.5: Layer +0x154 and +0x1FA sub-state pins on top of v0.15.2.4's anim-ID trio. v0.15.2.4 BAT in domt4_1 proved the {+0x150, +0x23F, +0x241}=0x21 pin held perfectly across two consecutive captures (FINAL SUMMARY confirmed those three bytes never changed) BUT kani still woke up and triggered battles #2 and #3 in rapid succession. Diagnosis: the anim-ID trio is purely a RENDERING pin (controls which pose the model displays). The actual wakeup is driven by the +0x154 dword LSB (and its +0x1FA byte mirror), which the engine decrements from 0x14 over ~5 seconds; once it crosses some threshold, the AI/movement logic starts moving kani toward Squall and collision detection triggers a new battle. Capture #2 INITIAL +0x154=0x0C — the engine PERSISTED the post-wakeup sub-state value across the battle/field reload (only +0x150 was reset to 0x21 by the engine's normal init), so each successive battle woke up faster than the last because the countdown never got a chance to reset. v0.15.2.5 fix: ApplyFreezePin() now writes 0x14 to bytes +0x154 and +0x1FA every frame in addition to the existing 0x21 writes to +0x150/+0x23F/+0x241. Byte-level writes are correct here — the +0x154 dword's upper three bytes were already 0 in INITIAL and the FIRST CHANGE log only ever showed the LSB changing. Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~25 lines: header comment trail, ApplyFreezePin two new byte writes + comment, FREEZE ACTIVATED log line, Initialize log line). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.2.4: Add per-frame FREEZE on top of the v0.15.2.3.1 diagnostic capture. v0.15.2.3.1 BAT in domt4_1 (capture started 18:00:11 on mode 4->1, ran the full 10s window) revealed a clean three-phase pattern matching Aaron's UX description: Phase A (0-700ms) field re-init burst as kani spawns; Phase B (700-5300ms, ~4.6s) QUIET PERIOD with kani visibly on the ground; Phase C (5300ms+) wakeup burst with position fields starting to update rapidly. Battle #2 at 18:00:18 (7s after capture start) confirmed kani fully woke up and caught Squall inside our window. Five bytes flipped in a tight 50ms window at Phase C onset and stayed at the new value through end-of-capture: +0x150 dword 0x00000021->0x00000011 (animation ID, 'down'->'running'), +0x154 dword 0x00000014->0x0000000C (paired sub-state), +0x23F byte 0x21->0x11 (anim shadow A), +0x241 byte 0x21->0x11 (anim shadow B), +0x1FA byte 0x14->0x0C (sub-state mirror). Position bytes confirm wakeup is real: +0x140 (X) went 0xFFFFFC1D=-995 -> 0xFFFFFDDC=-548, +0x144 (Y) went 0x00000DEA=3562 -> 0x00000A37=2615 -- kani moved ~947 units toward Squall during Phase C. v0.15.2.4 changes (chase_kani_freeze.cpp ~50 lines): (1) New state vars s_freezeActive, s_freezeFieldName[64]. (2) StartCapture sets s_freezeActive=true and saves ChaseDetector::GetDebouncedFieldName() into s_freezeFieldName as the freeze-anchor. (3) New helper ApplyFreezePin() called every Update() (after the existing capture loop trigger, before the early-return-if-not-capturing). It checks (a) whether s_freezeActive, (b) whether the current debounced field name is non-empty AND different from s_freezeFieldName -> deactivate freeze and log; otherwise (c) re-resolve kani via ChaseDetector::GetKaniEntityPtr() each frame and SEH-write 0x21 to bytes at +0x150, +0x23F, +0x241. Empty-string field name during the 2s name-debounce after fieldId flip does NOT deactivate (we don't yet know the destination). (4) Initialize/Shutdown clear new state. CONSERVATIVE FIRST ATTEMPT: only the three animation-ID bytes are pinned; +0x154 dword and +0x1FA byte (the paired sub-state) are NOT pinned in this build. If the next BAT shows kani still wakes up, v0.15.2.5 layers on +0x154 and +0x1FA. The diagnostic capture continues to run alongside the freeze so the next BAT's FINAL SUMMARY can verify pin compliance: +0x150, +0x23F, +0x241 should be ABSENT from the changed-bytes list if the freeze is working. If kani still moves and triggers a second battle despite the pin, the diagnostic will show what other bytes the engine wrote at the wakeup moment to drive it. Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~50 lines: header comment, freeze state vars, StartCapture activation, ApplyFreezePin helper, Update integration, Initialize/Shutdown reset). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.  // v0.15.2.3.1: Fix v0.15.2.3 capture trigger — fired during the wrong game-mode window. v0.15.2.3 BAT (domt4_1, count=1 chase-mode kani battle ending at 17:38:31) showed the capture started cleanly on the mode 3->5 transition but logged ZERO byte changes across the entire 5-second window. Root cause: the post-battle sequence is mode 3 (battle) -> mode 5 (fade-to-field transition, engine pauses ALL entity updates) -> mode 1 (active field, entity state machine resumes). v0.15.2.3 triggered on prev==3 && cur!=3, which lands in mode 5 where nothing changes. The actual field re-init in the BAT log fired at 17:38:37 (1 second AFTER our capture window closed), and the second kani battle started at 17:38:44 — meaning the wakeup completed within ~7 seconds of mode 1 beginning, entirely OUTSIDE our v0.15.2.3 capture window. v0.15.2.3.1 changes: (1) New MODE_FIELD_VAL=1 constant. (2) New s_battleSeenRecently flag, set true when mode reads MODE_BATTLE_VAL=3, cleared after a capture starts. (3) Trigger condition rewritten: capture now fires on the first frame of MODE_FIELD_VAL with s_battleSeenRecently true, i.e., the moment the engine returns to active field mode following any battle. Catches 3->5->1, 3->1, 3->4->1 etc. uniformly. Re-arms after each capture so subsequent kani battles in the same chase session each get their own capture. (4) CAPTURE_DURATION_MS bumped 5000 -> 10000ms. v0.15.2.3 BAT showed wakeup completes within ~7s of mode 1 starting, so 10s comfortably covers the on-ground period plus the standing transition with a couple seconds of headroom. (5) MID_SUMMARY_AT_MS bumped 2500 -> 5000ms (halfway through the new window). NO other behavior changes. Same diagnostic-only scope as v0.15.2.3 — v0.15.2.4 still installs the actual freeze hook based on the v0.15.2.3.1 BAT data. Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.cpp (~40 lines: new MODE_FIELD_VAL constant block, new s_battleSeenRecently state, rewritten trigger logic in Update, Initialize reset, log version string), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.3: DIAGNOSTIC build for chase robot freeze redesign. v0.15.2.2 BAT proved the v0.15.1 ChaseBattleFreeze approach (hook opcode_battle, NO-OP the second+ kani battle in each chase field) is not viable in domt2_1/domt5_1: ChaseDetector saw both battles via game-mode 1→3 polling (count=1 then count=2) but ZERO ChaseBattleFreeze NO-OP/pass-through log lines. opcode_battle is dead code for these fields — they enter battle via a different code path (random-encounter step counter or scripted encounter). The v0.15.2.1 robot fix worked in domt4_1 only because that's the one chase field that actually goes through opcode_battle. Aaron clarified the desired UX: post-battle, when the field re-appears, kani is visible on the ground for several seconds before the engine's wakeup animation/AI-state runs and the robot stands back up to resume chasing. The cleaner design is to hook that wakeup transition (which is the SAME state machine across every chase field) and pin kani at "incapacitated" until the field changes, instead of trying to intercept battles at all. v0.15.2.3 ships a DIAGNOSTIC build that captures the data needed to identify the wakeup byte. v0.15.2.4 will install the actual hook based on this BAT's output. Changes: (1) New module src/chase_kani_freeze.{h,cpp} (~330 lines). On every game-mode 3→non-3 transition (battle exit) while ChaseDetector reports we're in a chase field with kani's entity address resolved, snapshot kani's full entity block (stride 0x264 for Others, 0x1B4 for Backgrounds) and continuously diff for 5 seconds. Logs: INITIAL hex dump (full entity block, ~32 lines), every byte's FIRST CHANGE event with timing (e.g. 'KaniFreeze: t=1234ms tick=42 +0x07A: FIRST CHANGE 0x00 -> 0x01'), MID-WINDOW heartbeat at t=2.5s for visibility, and FINAL SUMMARY listing every byte that changed with initial-vs-final values and signed delta. SEH-guarded reads. Cap of MAX_STRIDE=0x264 byte buffer. (2) src/chase_battle_freeze.cpp removed from build (deploy.bat compile line replaced with chase_kani_freeze.cpp). The .h and .cpp source files remain on disk as orphans — v0.15.2.4 cleanup may delete them after the new approach is BAT-validated. (3) src/dinput8.cpp: include swapped chase_battle_freeze.h → chase_kani_freeze.h, Initialize/Shutdown calls updated, new Update() call added to per-tick chain (after ChaseAskOverlay::Update() so ChaseDetector's kani address has been refreshed). The expected BAT output pattern: kani is on the ground for several seconds = mostly-quiet log with maybe one byte (an animation tick or AI-state counter) incrementing slowly, then a sharp inflection where ONE byte (or small word/dword) flips at the moment kani stands up, then a flood of position-byte changes per tick as kani begins moving. v0.15.2.4 will pin the inflection byte at its initial value, freezing kani in the on-ground state until field exit. v0.15.1.2 timing fix preserved (3-second deferred OpenAsk so Squall's chase-trigger line plays first). v0.15.2.1 MODE_BATTLE_VAL=3 fix in chase_detector.cpp preserved (unrelated to wakeup; still needed for ChaseDetector's per-field battle counter and for ChaseKaniFreeze's battle-exit edge detection). v0.15.2.2 chase_ask_overlay TTS+keyboard-only path preserved unchanged. Files changed: src/chase_kani_freeze.{h,cpp} (NEW, ~330 lines). src/dinput8.cpp (~10 lines: include + Initialize/Update/Shutdown wiring). src/deploy.bat (1 line: filename swap). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md.
  // v0.15.2.2: Drop engine-rendered chase ASK proxy slot. v0.15.2.1 BAT shipped two diagnostic findings that close the engine-ASK chapter: (1) the SLOT-SNAP polling logged our proxy slot's state every second for 13 seconds after a fully-correct PopulateProxySlot, and EVERY snapshot showed our writes preserved EXACTLY (state=0x0D, geom=[0x50 0x0A 0xCC 0x5D], t1=t2=non-NULL, mode1=0x1000, trans=0x1000, firstQ=1 lastQ=2 curQ tracking, cb1=cb2=NULL). The engine never modified our slot — it just IGNORED slot 1 in its render pass for the entire 13 seconds the ASK was 'open' (until the kani-collision battle wiped the slot via field-to-battle transition cleanup). This rules out every 'we're missing a field' hypothesis and proves the engine's render loop doesn't iterate pWindowsArray looking for state=0x0D dialogs. Render is bound to script-VM context: only slots that an active script has parked on via opcode_ask/opcode_aask get rendered. Replicating that binding from a DLL hook would require massive disassembly work to find the script-VM 'current dialog slot' reference. (2) The robot-fix MODE_BATTLE_VAL=3 from v0.15.2.1 WORKS in domt4_1: log shows 'NO-OP kani BATTLE in domt4_1 (battleCount=1, freeze#1) returning 3' and freeze#2 — these are the 'interruption / delay' Aaron heard. The kani's bounce-back animation runs on its own AI timeline independent of opcode_battle, so the entity still 'gets up' visually — but Aaron is blind and the audio impact (no battle screen) is what matters. Note: ChaseBattleFreeze didn't fire AT ALL for the two domt5_1 battles at 22:32:43 and 22:34:17 — only ChaseDetector saw them via mode-flip detection. opcode_battle isn't being called for those battles; they use a different code path (random-encounter step counter or scripted encounter). Deferred to v0.15.3 investigation. v0.15.2.2 ships ONE small change in chase_ask_overlay.cpp::OpenAsk: replace the FindFreeWindowSlot+PopulateProxySlot block with 's_proxySlotIdx = -1' plus a one-line log and a 30-line comment explaining the rationale and pointing future maintainers at the v0.15.0–0.15.2.1 history in CHANGELOG.md. v0.15.2.x ships the chase ASK as TTS+keyboard only; the engine-rendered visual remains an open feature gap deferred to v0.15.3+ pending discovery of the script-VM binding mechanism. Helper functions (FindFreeWindowSlot, PopulateProxySlot, ReleaseProxySlot, LogProxySlotSnapshot, SyncEngineCursor) remain in source as unreferenced statics (MSVC C4505 off at default /W3, compiles clean) so a future version can re-enable proxy slot rendering if the script-VM binding mechanism is ever discovered. SLOT-SNAP polling in Update() and SyncEngineCursor in PollKeys are now inert (s_proxySlotIdx<0 early-returns), no spam. ReleaseProxySlot in CloseAsk is also inert (early-return). Risk: zero — only removes the broken engine-ASK attempt, leaves TTS+keyboard ASK path unchanged. v0.15.1.2 timing fix preserved (3-second deferred OpenAsk so Squall's 'Forget it!  Let's go!' plays first). v0.15.2.1 robot fix preserved (MODE_BATTLE_VAL=3). Files changed: src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (~30 lines, OpenAsk proxy block replaced with comment+disable). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Two-fix BAT response. (1) ROBOT FIX: chase_detector.cpp had MODE_BATTLE_VAL=999 — wrong. v0.15.2 BAT showed mode=3 dominant during chase battle (95s straight, ent0 frozen = Squall in battle screen). Battle mode in this Steam 2013 build is 3 at field-mode polling resolution. Edge detection 'prev != 999 && cur == 999' never fired so per-field battle counter stayed at 0 across kani contacts, ChaseBattleFreeze passed through every kani battle (count<1 condition). Result: robot kept getting up after each battle. Setting MODE_BATTLE_VAL=3 makes edge detection fire on first kani battle, freeze gate's count>=1 triggers on second. Field log will now show 'ChaseDetector: battle entered (game-mode 0x0001 -> 0x0003); field=domt4_1 chaseActive=1 count=1' on first contact, then 'ChaseBattleFreeze: NO-OP kani BATTLE' on second. (2) ASK RENDERING DIAGNOSTIC + GEOMETRY: v0.15.2 BAT showed proxy slot 1 was successfully populated (PopulateProxySlot returned true, fields written) but the engine didn't render the dialog box visually. Two changes: (a) Added geometry writes to PopulateProxySlot using captured values 50 00 0A 00 CC 00 5D 00 (4 × uint16 = 0x0050, 0x000A, 0x00CC, 0x005D) at offset 0x00. v0.15.2 left these as zero which may have caused the engine to cull the slot from the render pass. New TEMPLATE_GEOM[4] constant and WIN_OBJ_GEOM0_OFFSET. (b) Added new LogProxySlotSnapshot() diagnostic helper that dumps full slot state (geometry, text pointers, winId, mode1, trans, state, firstQ/lastQ/curQ, callbacks). Called once right after PopulateProxySlot ('post-populate' label) and then at 1Hz while the ASK is open ('while-open' label). This will tell us whether the engine modifies our writes (state going to 0 = engine touches the slot but invalidates it) or ignores them entirely (state stays at 0x0D = our slot lacks something needed to be a render target). New tick counter s_lastProxySnapshotTick. Risk: if geometry alone fixes rendering, great. If not, the snapshot data will guide v0.15.2.2. The snapshot logging may also reveal that slot 0 is special (engine renders only slot 0 for ASK) — if so, v0.15.2.2 would prefer/wait for slot 0. v0.15.1.2 timing fix and all v0.15.2 changes preserved. Files changed: src/chase_detector.cpp (~10 lines, MODE_BATTLE_VAL constant + comment), src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (~80 lines: WIN_OBJ_GEOM0_OFFSET constant, TEMPLATE_GEOM[4] constant, geometry writes in PopulateProxySlot, log line update with geom values, LogProxySlotSnapshot helper function, post-populate snapshot call in OpenAsk, while-open snapshot call in Update, s_lastProxySnapshotTick state). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Re-enable engine-rendered ASK dialog using captured template values. v0.15.1.2 BAT included a quick chase-diag ASK snapshot capture (Aaron triggered an in-game 3-option ASK, OnAskOpcodeFired dumped slot[0] full state). Captured values: state=0x0000000D (was guessing 0x07), mode1=0x1000 (was 0x0005), open_close_transition=0x1000 (was 0), callbacks at +0x34/+0x38 are 0x00000000 NULL (was function pointers — ROOT CAUSE of v0.15.1's 180Hz dispatch lockup). Real engine ASKs have NULL callbacks; the engine apparently reads +0x34/+0x38 for some other dispatch purpose (per-frame render tick, perhaps), not ASK confirm/cancel handlers. Setting them to non-NULL function pointers in v0.15.1 caused the engine to invoke them every frame. v0.15.2 changes in src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp: TEMPLATE_MODE1=0x1000, TEMPLATE_STATE=0x0000000D, new TEMPLATE_TRANS=0x1000 constant. PopulateProxySlot writes captured values, sets t1=t2=encoded buffer (was t2=nullptr), sets callbacks to 0. OpenAsk re-enables FindFreeWindowSlot+PopulateProxySlot calls (undoes v0.15.1.1's `s_proxySlotIdx=-1` hardcode). Hybrid input design: engine renders the dialog window with visual cursor; our PollKeys still owns input. New SyncEngineCursor() helper writes (s_currentHighlight+1) to engine's curQ field after each Up/Down keypress so the rendered cursor visually tracks our state for sighted spectators. CommitChoice still calls CloseAsk which calls ReleaseProxySlot which sets state=0 and clears callbacks/text pointers. v0.15.1.2 timing fix preserved unchanged — OnDialogText still defers OpenAsk by 3 seconds via s_triggerPending/s_triggerTimestamp so Squall's "Forget it!  Let's go!" plays first. ConfirmCallback/CancelCallback functions remain in source as unreferenced statics (MSVC C4505 off at default /W3, compiles clean). One known minor issue (defer to v0.15.3): chase_diag's OnAskOpcodeFired hex dump's `+10:` row only shows "000" instead of full 16 bytes — snprintf size-tracking bug in chase_diag.cpp where p2/p3 accumulators go negative when buffer overflows, causing UB in subsequent calls. High-level fields all captured cleanly so this didn't block v0.15.2. v0.15.2 BAT will verify: (1) Aaron hears Squall's chase-trigger line first, (2) chase-mode ASK appears as engine-rendered window AFTER 3-second delay, (3) Up/Down/Enter and 1/2 still work correctly with TTS announcements, (4) engine cursor visually tracks selection (verified via screenshot if possible; not strictly necessary since Aaron is blind), (5) chase_battle_freeze still caps battles correctly, (6) Lapin Beach FMV ends chase cleanly. RISK: if the engine ALSO consumes our keypress events (because it sees state=0x0D as 'active ASK awaiting input'), our PollKeys handler may double-fire — which would be visible as up/down counting twice per press in the field log. If observed, v0.15.2.1 would suppress engine input on our slot or detect-and-ignore. Files changed: src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (~50 lines: constants update, PopulateProxySlot rewrite, OpenAsk re-enable, new SyncEngineCursor + PollKeys integration), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md (top entry), DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. — defer prompt by 3 seconds so Squall's chase-trigger line plays first. v0.15.1.1 BAT showed the lockup was gone (proxy slot disabled) but the timing was wrong: ChaseAskOverlay::OnDialogText was called synchronously inside field_dialog's Hook_show_dialog, BEFORE field_dialog's own NVDA speak path got to announce 'Forget it!  Let's go!'. Our SpeakInitialPrompt(interrupt=true) clobbered the SAPI queue and Aaron never heard Squall's line. Fix: OnDialogText now sets s_triggerPending + s_triggerTimestamp = GetTickCount() + 3000 instead of calling OpenAsk synchronously. Update() polls per-tick and calls OpenAsk when the delay elapses, with a re-check of IsInChaseField() and !s_askFiredThisChase in case Aaron exited the chase during the delay window (extremely unlikely but cheap). 3 seconds covers a 5-word line at any reasonable TTS rate. New state vars in chase_ask_overlay.cpp: s_triggerPending, s_triggerTimestamp. New constant TRIGGER_DELAY_MS = 3000. New cleanup in chase-end branch of Update() to cancel pending if chase exited mid-delay. Initialize zeroes the new state. Aaron's other observation — 'didn't seem to trigger an in-game ASK dialog' — is a known limitation: v0.15.1.1 explicitly disabled the proxy-slot population in OpenAsk() to prevent the v0.15.1 callback feedback loop. Re-enabling the engine-rendered window requires real engine-set values for mode1 (currently 0x05 educated guess), state (currently 0x07 educated guess), and the callback ABI at +0x34/+0x38. chase_diag::OnAskOpcodeFired (added in v0.15.1) is already wired and dumps full snapshots of all 8 ff8_win_obj slots whenever opcode_ask or opcode_aask fires AND chase-diag is enabled (F12). To unlock v0.15.2 engine ASK: turn chase-diag ON before any natural in-game ASK (vendor 'Buy/Cancel', NPC yes/no question, save-point save-yes-no, Cid's 'Wanna become a SeeD?' — anything that fires opcode_ask), then v0.15.2 hardcodes captured values into TEMPLATE_MODE1/TEMPLATE_STATE and replaces the stub Confirm/CancelCallback with engine-aware no-ops or an ABI we've observed. Files changed for v0.15.1.2: src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (~75 lines: state vars, constant, OnDialogText defer logic, Update deferred-open + chase-end-cancel logic, Initialize reset), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version), CHANGELOG.md (top entry), DEVNOTES.md, NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md. Disable the engine-rendered proxy window in chase_ask_overlay.cpp::OpenAsk(). v0.15.1 BAT showed populating a free pWindowsArray slot with state=0x07 + our callbacks at +0x34/+0x38 put the engine into a feedback loop — ConfirmCallback/CancelCallback fired ~180 times/sec for 2+ seconds and the game appeared frozen. Aaron eventually completed the choice (committed Auto-drive via the TTS+keyboard path which kept working amid the spam), but the engine-window experiment was the lock cause. Pure TTS+keyboard now: SpeakInitialPrompt() reads the prompt, PollKeys() handles Up/Down/Enter and 1/2 number-key shortcuts. Aaron is blind so the engine-rendered window was sighted-player polish, not a functional requirement. Proxy code (FindFreeWindowSlot, PopulateProxySlot, Confirm/CancelCallback, ReleaseProxySlot) is preserved verbatim — v0.15.2 will re-enable once chase_diag's ASK snapshot logging captures real engine-set mode1/state values from a natural in-game ASK and we understand the callback ABI. ReleaseProxySlot early-returns on s_proxySlotIdx<0 so it stays a no-op without modification. Unused-static-function warnings on FindFreeWindowSlot/PopulateProxySlot/Confirm/CancelCallback compile clean — MSVC's C4505 is level 4 (off at default /W3). One file changed: src/chase_ask_overlay.cpp (~24 lines: replaced the proxy-allocation block in OpenAsk with a -1 assignment plus an explanatory log line and big comment block explaining the rationale and v0.15.2 plan). src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). CHANGELOG.md (top entry). DEVNOTES + NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT updated for ready-to-BAT state. New modules src/chase_detector.{h,cpp}, src/chase_ask_overlay.{h,cpp}, src/chase_battle_freeze.{h,cpp}. ChaseDetector is the single source of truth for chase-scene state: hard-coded chase-field name set (domt4_1, domt5_1, domt3_2, domt2_1, doopen2a, dotown_3, dotown_2, dotown_1) confirmed by v0.15.0 BAT, with a 2-second debounce on pCurrentFieldName per Finding 3 of that BAT (the name buffer lags pCurrentFieldId by 2-5 seconds). Tracks per-field battle count via game-mode-edge detection (prev != MODE_BATTLE, cur == MODE_BATTLE -> +1). Resolves kani's runtime slot per field via FieldArchive::LoadJSMCounts: doorsLines = doors+lines, bgStart = doorsLines, othersStart = doorsLines+backgrounds; kaniSymIdx >= othersStart -> Others slot, kaniSymIdx >= bgStart -> Backgrounds slot, else Doors/Lines (unexpected, bail). v0.15.0 BAT Finding 1 confirmed kani is always in pFieldStateBackgrounds across every chase field, so the new FindKaniLocation in chase_diag.cpp also routes through the same JSMCounts logic (the prior 'others-only' computation othersStart = symNameCount - otherCount was wrong and is removed). PollKani in chase_diag now picks the correct stride (0x1B4 Backgrounds vs 0x264 Others) via s_kaniArrayKind. ChaseAskOverlay fires on Squall's exact chase-trigger MES 'Forget it!  Let's go!' (with the double-space, confirmed by v0.15.0 dialog log capture; NOT 'Run!' as the deep research suggested). Trigger plumbing: field_dialog.cpp::Hook_show_dialog forwards every MODE_FIELD decoded text to ChaseAskOverlay::OnDialogText, which does a cheap strstr filter; matches in any chase field with chaseActive open the ASK exactly once per chase session (flag clears when ChaseDetector::IsChaseActive() flips back to false on transition out of the chase set). Two-path hybrid: PRIMARY engine-rendered window via the existing pWindowsArray slot allocation pattern (find first slot with state==0, populate text_data1 + win_id + mode1 + state + first/last/cur question + callbacks at +0x34/+0x38). Template values for v0.15.1 are educated-guess defaults (mode1=0x05, state=0x07) since chase_diag's new ASK snapshot logging (added in this build) hasn't yet captured real engine-set values; if the engine doesn't render the slot correctly on first BAT, the BELT-AND-SUSPENDERS TTS+keyboard path still works because it owns the actual choice capture. TTS speaks 'Chase mode. Auto-drive or Manual. Use up and down to choose. Press Enter to confirm. Currently selected: Manual.' with up/down arrow cycling (TTS announces new highlight on each cycle), Enter to commit, AND number-key shortcuts (1=Auto-drive, 2=Manual) as a courtesy. All keys Alt-gated per v0.14.105 convention. Selection persists to ff8_accessibility.ini under a new [Chase] section, key chase_mode = manual|auto, via direct WritePrivateProfileStringA (Config:: stays single-section). Auto-drive isn't shipped in v0.15.1 - selecting it announces 'Auto-drive is not yet implemented. Falling back to manual.' and stores manual; the slot is wired so v0.15.2+ auto-drive ships as a behavior change with no overlay rework. ChaseBattleFreeze hooks pExecuteOpcodeTable[0x69] (opcode_battle) via MinHook. Gates: mode==manual && IsInChaseField() && IsKaniEntityPtr(entityPtr) && GetCurrentFieldBattleCount()>=1 -> return JSM-VM advance code 3 without calling original (caps chase to 1 battle per field, sidesteps post-knockdown WAIT timer). All other cases pass through to s_origBattle. The 'calling entity is kani' check is pointer equality between the hook's entityPtr parameter and ChaseDetector::GetKaniEntityPtr() which returns backgroundsBase+0x1B4*kaniSlot OR othersBase+0x264*kaniSlot depending on s_kaniArrayKind. Risk noted: if BATTLE pushes a return value to its script-VM stack (we haven't disassembled it to confirm), post-BATTLE script paths might misbehave; v0.15.1 BAT will surface if so. Chase-diag instrumentation extended: new ChaseDiag::OnAskOpcodeFired() called from field_dialog's Hook_opcode_ask + Hook_opcode_aask. When chase-diag is enabled (F12), dumps full state of all 8 ff8_win_obj slots (state, mode1, open_close_transition, win_id, firstQ/lastQ/curQ, field30, callbacks, text pointers) plus a 60-byte hex dump per slot. Aaron's intended workflow: turn chase-diag ON before the chase, trigger any natural in-game ASK (NPC question, vendor 'buy/cancel', etc.) to capture template values, then enter the chase; v0.15.2 hardcodes the captured values into chase_ask_overlay's TEMPLATE_MODE1 and TEMPLATE_STATE for guaranteed engine-rendered window display. NO-OP when chase-diag is disabled (cheap early-return; doesn't spam the field log on normal NPC dialogs). Module wiring in dinput8.cpp: ChaseDetector::Initialize must be FIRST (the other modules query it), then ChaseDiag, ChaseAskOverlay, ChaseBattleFreeze. ChaseDetector::Update + ChaseAskOverlay::Update polled per-tick alongside the existing module updates; ChaseBattleFreeze has no per-tick driver since it operates entirely via its detour. Shutdown order reversed. F12 still owned by ChaseDiag::Toggle from v0.15.0; F12 rule honored - searched all source files for VK_F12/0x7B before adding the new chase_ask_overlay's keyboard handler, no F12 references in the new keyboard handler. FILES: src/chase_detector.{h,cpp} (new, ~80 + ~250 lines), src/chase_ask_overlay.{h,cpp} (new, ~30 + ~330 lines), src/chase_battle_freeze.{h,cpp} (new, ~30 + ~120 lines), src/chase_diag.h (added OnAskOpcodeFired forward decl, ~7 lines), src/chase_diag.cpp (replaced FindKaniSlot with FindKaniLocation using JSMCounts, added KANI_BACKGROUNDS/KANI_OTHERS routing in PollKani, added s_kaniArrayKind state+resets, added OnAskOpcodeFired implementation with hex dump per slot, ~150 lines net), src/field_dialog.cpp (added forward decls for ChaseAskOverlay/ChaseDiag, OnDialogText call in Hook_show_dialog, OnAskOpcodeFired calls in Hook_opcode_ask/Hook_opcode_aask, ~25 lines), src/dinput8.cpp (~20 lines: includes for 3 new modules, Initialize/Update/Shutdown wiring), src/deploy.bat (3 new compile lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).
  // v0.14.108: Party-member filter, take 2

  // v0.14.107: Filter party members from field entity catalog.

  // v0.14.106: Cleanup + INI template. Pairs with v0.14.105's Alt+F4 fix (root cause: every Alt+F4 close fired IncreaseRate() because the F4 handler wasn't gated on Alt; speech_rate crept up by 1 per session). v0.14.105 BAT confirmed the fix end-to-end: Aaron set rate=7, closed via Alt+F4, restarted, log showed `Config loaded rate=7` and welcome was at the saved rate. F4 then went to 8 (correct increment). All persisted settings round-trip correctly: speech_rate, speech_volume, speech_voice_id (DAVID), game_volume (40%), sfx_volume (80%), tts_duck_enabled (ON), ewm_enabled. v0.14.106 strips the v0.14.105 LogActualSAPIState diagnostic harness (helper + 5 instrumentation points: after InitSAPI, after ApplySavedVoiceId, after defensive re-apply, end of Initialize, before/after every IncreaseRate/DecreaseRate/IncreaseVolume/DecreaseVolume). The diagnostic proved persistence is sound and SAPI's SetVoice() does preserve rate/volume across voice changes — making the v0.14.105 defensive re-apply also unnecessary. Both removed in v0.14.106. Adds: human-readable INI template. config.cpp now writes a commented template (settings explained, ranges documented, in-game shortcuts noted) when (a) the INI file doesn't exist yet, or (b) the file exists but lacks our marker comment (legacy upgrade path — preserves all existing values). New helpers: HasTemplateMarker() scans file for our marker; EnsureTemplate() reads existing values via GetPrivateProfileInt/String, then rewrites the file with the commented template overlaid with current values. Called from Load() on first run and once per existing pre-comment install. Subsequent SetInt/SetString calls hit WritePrivateProfileStringA which preserves comments line-by-line. Aaron's existing INI will be auto-upgraded on next launch. NO behavior change for users beyond the friendlier file. FILES: src/dinput8.cpp (unchanged from v0.14.105 — retains the Alt-gate fix), src/screen_reader.cpp (strip LogActualSAPIState helper, 5 instrumentation points, defensive re-apply block), src/config.cpp (add INI_TEMPLATE constant, EnsureTemplate(), HasTemplateMarker(), call from Load()), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103.7: Elegant per-destination foot-friendliness check via Section 8 trigger programs. Aaron's question after v0.14.103.6 BAT: "didn't you say you identified a way to tell which locations support entry by car and which do not? If so, can't we just have AD automatically stop when it is right next to locations that do not let cars enter, and drive in to those that do?" Answer: YES — the data is parsed and ready. Section 8 trigger programs (s_triggerPrograms[], 38 entries) tell us per-region whether foot entry is supported. Cars enter foot-friendly regions via engine auto-dismount on the foot trigger; non-foot-friendly regions (vehicle-only landing pads like Balamb Garden's region 0x0C, Garden-only) reject cars permanently. Implementation: new IsLocationFootFriendly(catX, catY) helper walks s_triggerPrograms[] looking for any program with top_vehicle ∈ {ANY, FOOT, FOOT_ALT} AND a clause with vehicle ∈ {ANY, FOOT, FOOT_ALT} targeting the destination's region byte. New file-scope static s_destFootFriendly captured at StartAutoDrive after s_driveTargetX/Y are assigned. Sweep-abort hook now uses dynamic threshold: foot-friendly destinations keep DRIVE_BOUNCE_ABORT_THRESHOLD=2 retries (~27s, allows engine to find the gate); non-foot-friendly destinations get threshold=1 (immediate bounce-arrived on first sweep-abort, ~13s). Cross-references against the actual BAT data in v0.14.103.5 BAT log: Balamb Town catalog (12896,-26711) → seg(17,20) → region 0x07 → program 9 has foot clauses → foot-friendly ✓ (engine auto-dismounted, MODE_FIELD on cycle 1). Balamb Garden catalog (24576,-29406) → seg(19,20) → region 0x0C → ONLY program 20 (loc=0x0172, top_vehicle=Garden) targets 0x0C, NO foot clause → not foot-friendly ✓ (cars bounce). v0.14.103.6 BAT timing: Garden bounced 2 cycles = ~27s. With v0.14.103.7: Garden bounce-arrives on cycle 1 abort = ~13s (52% reduction vs .6, 67% reduction vs .5). Balamb car AD UNCHANGED (0 aborts, MODE_FIELD on cycle 1 entry). Defensive fallback: if region map fails to load OR is_segmentRegionLoaded=false at AD start, s_destFootFriendly defaults to TRUE (use the longer threshold) to preserve existing behavior. Same fallback if destination's segment is out-of-range or region byte = 0xFF. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.103.6 retry counter mechanism UNCHANGED (just dynamic threshold). v0.14.103.5 retry counter mechanism UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.4 wording UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.7 BAT plan: same two tests as v0.14.103.5/.6 (rent car → AD to Balamb Town, then AD to Balamb Garden), confirm Balamb still arrives normally (foot-friendly path), Garden bounce-arrives at ~13s instead of 27s. The [DRIVE] Start log will now include 'foot-friendly=YES/NO' for each drive so we can verify the heuristic ran correctly. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~80 lines: new IsLocationFootFriendly helper after WorldYToSegRow ~1757, new s_destFootFriendly state next to s_sweepAbortCount ~1395, IsLocationFootFriendly call in StartAutoDrive after Start log ~3568, dynamic threshold in sweep-abort hook ~3750, resets in StopAutoDrive and Initialize), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103.6: Lower DRIVE_BOUNCE_ABORT_THRESHOLD from 3 to 2 to reduce Garden bounce-arrived latency from ~40s to ~27s. v0.14.103.5 BAT (Wed 2026-05-06 23:19-23:21) confirmed both targets work with threshold=3, but Aaron noted Garden bounce took 'a long time of bouncing around' before announcing. BAT timing analysis: Balamb car AD arrived in 0 sweep-aborts (MODE_FIELD fires on cycle 1 entry, ~1s after entering final approach). Balamb foot AD in earlier v0.14.103 BAT arrived in 1 sweep-abort (cycle 1 abort → cycle 2 MODE_FIELD). Both arrival paths complete BEFORE threshold=2 trips. Garden car AD bounces all 3 cycles in v0.14.103.5 BAT — dropping threshold to 2 trips bounce-arrived at cycle 2 abort (~27s) instead of cycle 3 (~40s). 33% latency reduction with no risk to existing arrival paths, since neither Balamb foot nor Balamb car needs 2+ aborts to arrive. Aaron also asked about a 'more elegant solution' for the Garden case. The architecturally-correct answer is parsing wmsetus Section 8 vehicle-mask data at module init to determine per-location car-friendliness directly: Balamb Garden's location entry would be flagged 'cars rejected' and bounce-arrived would fire on the FIRST abort (~13s, faster still). That's a v0.15.x project (requires understanding the wmsetus data format more thoroughly than the deep research provides). For now, threshold=2 is the simplest empirically-validated improvement. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.103.5 retry counter mechanism UNCHANGED (just the constant value). v0.14.103.4 wording UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.3 sweep-abort hook architecture UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.6 BAT plan: same two tests as v0.14.103.5 (rent car → AD to Balamb Town, then AD to Balamb Garden), confirm Balamb still arrives normally and Garden bounce-arrives at ~27s instead of 40s. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (1 line: DRIVE_BOUNCE_ABORT_THRESHOLD constant from 3 to 2 + comment update), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103.5: Add retry counter to sweep-abort bounce-arrived hook to fix car entry into car-friendly towns. v0.14.103.4 BAT (Wed 2026-05-06 ~22:30+) confirmed: bounce announcement at Balamb Garden works correctly with new wording ("You may need to enter on foot."). BUT regression discovered: AD no longer drives the car INTO car-friendly towns like Balamb. Aaron's report: 'the bounce detection and arrival announcement at B-Garden worked as expected. However, it no longer drives the car into towns that support entry by car such as Balamb.' Root cause: v0.14.103.3's sweep-abort hook fires bounce-arrived on the FIRST sweep abort. But for car-friendly towns the engine fires MODE_FIELD on the SECOND or LATER pass through the trigger area. Foot AD to Balamb in the v0.14.103 BAT log showed exactly this pattern: cycle 1 sweep-abort → normal steering resumes → cycle 2 re-enters zone → MODE_FIELD fires within 2 seconds. By stopping AD on the first abort, v0.14.103.3 killed that retry pattern. Pre-v0.14.103.3 behavior (which worked for Balamb): 'returning to normal steering' allowed unlimited retries until either MODE_FIELD fires (car-friendly town) or stuck-detection bails out (which itself was unhelpful for Garden-style locations — hence the bounce hook). v0.14.103.5 ships ONE targeted fix: make the bounce-arrived hook count consecutive sweep-aborts and only fire after DRIVE_BOUNCE_ABORT_THRESHOLD (=3) cycles. (1) New static const DRIVE_BOUNCE_ABORT_THRESHOLD = 3 (~40 seconds of retries). (2) New file-scope static int s_sweepAbortCount, reset in StartAutoDrive. (3) Sweep-abort hook in UpdateAutoDrive increments counter, checks threshold: if reached → announce bounce-arrived + StopAutoDrive (v0.14.103.3 behavior). If below → reset sweep state and resume normal steering with [DRIVE-SWEEP] Aborting log including retry N/M (v0.14.99 behavior). For Balamb (car-friendly): cycle 1-2 abort + retry, cycle 3 MODE_FIELD fires (typically) before threshold trip. For Balamb Garden (non-car-friendly): cycles repeat indefinitely until threshold trips at cycle 3, then bounce-arrived fires. Net effect: car-friendly towns get up to 2 retry attempts (sufficient per BAT data); car-unfriendly locations bounce-arrive after ~40s. Tunable if BAT shows otherwise. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.103.4 wording change UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.3 sweep-abort hook architecture UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.3 WM_SAVEMAP_TO_DWORD_SCALE=1 fix UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.1 [VEH-VERIFY] UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.5 BAT plan: rent car in Balamb. (Test 1) AD to Balamb Town — expect successful arrival via MODE_FIELD within 2-3 sweep-abort cycles, normal '[DRIVE] Arrival via game-mode' log. (Test 2) AD to Balamb Garden — expect 3 sweep-abort retries, then bounce-arrived announce 'Arrived near Balamb Garden. You may need to enter on foot.' If Test 1 fails (Balamb still bounces): threshold may need to be higher. If Test 2 fails (Garden enters): probably won't happen but would mean wmsetus interpretation needs review. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~25 lines: new constant + state at ~1380, retry counter logic at sweep-abort site ~3724, reset in StartAutoDrive ~3417), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103.4: Wording tweak per Aaron's BAT feedback. v0.14.103.3 BAT (Wed 2026-05-06 ~22:30) confirmed sweep-abort bounce-detection works end-to-end — car drove to Balamb Garden, sweep-abort fired, TTS announced and AD stopped silently. Aaron's only feedback: 'manual approach' is unclear; should say you may have to enter the location ON FOOT (since you're in a car and need to dismount). Single-line text change in the bounce-arrived announcement: 'Arrived near [Location]. The entrance may require a manual approach.' → 'Arrived near [Location]. You may need to enter on foot.' Foot AD users see the same text but it still makes sense (they're already on foot but may need to approach a different angle). NO behavior change otherwise. v0.14.103.3 sweep-abort bounce-arrived path UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.3 WM_SAVEMAP_TO_DWORD_SCALE=1 fix UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.1 [VEH-VERIFY] dump UNCHANGED. v0.14.103 forest avoidance UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.4 BAT plan: same as v0.14.103.3 — rent car, drive AD to Balamb Garden, expect sweep-abort + TTS announcing 'Arrived near Balamb Garden. You may need to enter on foot.' If announcement reads correctly, v0.14.103.4 is the final v0.14.103.x build. v0.14.104 cleanup: strip [VEH-VERIFY] diagnostic dump and one-shot flag, remove dead per-frame bounce-detection state (s_carBounceFrames, s_carBouncePrevX/Y, s_carBounceLastTick) and constants (DRIVE_BOUNCE_*) since the sweep-abort hook covers the same scenario more reliably, then consolidated commit and push to GitHub. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (1 line: snprintf format string in the bounce-arrived announce at the sweep-abort site ~3705), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103.3: Hook bounce-detection into the sweep-abort site instead of trying to detect frozen position. v0.14.103.2 BAT (22:11:38) confirmed: build clean, [VEH-VERIFY] dump fired correctly at first drive start, AD drove the car to Balamb Garden (distance dropped from 8892 to 970 in 7 seconds), but bounce-detection STILL didn't fire. Aaron's report: 'AD drove to B-Garden, but never stopped and announced arrival.' The 4-frame frozen-position bounce-detection criterion doesn't match real-world car-vs-wall behavior. The car bounces and oscillates rather than freezing. Distance bounces 970-1554 continuously. Gas is also intermittent during sweep (held during walk phases, off during turn phases). The strict per-sample frozen criterion never accumulates 4 consecutive frames. KEY INSIGHT FROM BAT: the [DRIVE-SWEEP] Aborting log line IS the ground-truth bounce signal. Sweep activates after final-approach timeout (6 seconds within 1000 units with no field-entry trigger). Sweep walks the player in different directions to find the gate. For cars, sweep walks always drift out fast (cars are too fast for the 1500-unit grace band). The abort fires within 1-3 seconds of sweep start. So 'sweep aborted on drift' literally means 'we got within 1000 units of target, the engine never accepted entry, and our recovery sweep drifted us back outside.' That IS the stuck-near-target signal. v0.14.103.3 ships TWO targeted fixes plus ONE empirically-validated cleanup: (1) Replace the v0.14.103 4-frame frozen-position bounce-detection in UpdateAutoDrive with a hook at the sweep-abort site (line ~3705). When sweep aborts on drift AND we're within DRIVE_NEAR_LOCATION_DIST, announce 'Arrived near [Location]. The entrance may require a manual approach.' and StopAutoDrive(nullptr). (2) Drop the per-frame bounce-detection block entirely — it never fires in practice, and the sweep-abort hook covers the same scenario more reliably. The bounce-detection state variables (s_carBounceFrames, s_carBouncePrevX/Y, s_carBounceLastTick) and constants (DRIVE_BOUNCE_*) become dead code; left in place for v0.14.104 cleanup. (3) Fix WM_SAVEMAP_TO_DWORD_SCALE from 4096 to 1. BAT empirically proved that savemap WORLDMAP struct stores positions at the SAME scale as foot DWORDs — not 20.12 fixed-point. The [VEH-VERIFY] dump showed: foot DWORDs X=16031, Y=-26948 versus car_pos[0]=16031, car_pos[2]=-26948 (exact match at 1:1 scale). The 4096x multiplication would have broken car position reads if the vehicle path ever fired (e.g. for Garden/Ragnarok). Fixing now while we have the empirical evidence in hand. v0.14.103.3 BAT plan: rent car in Balamb (or use save state from previous BATs), drive AD to Balamb Garden. Expected sequence: car drives in (dist 8000 → 970 over ~7s), final-approach timeout fires after 6s, sweep starts with phase 1 turning, walk begins and drifts to dist=1500-something, sweep abort triggers, bounce-arrived announcement fires: 'Arrived near Balamb Garden. The entrance may require a manual approach.' AD stops silently. Total time from drive start to bounce announce: ~14-16 seconds. Aaron then manually walks the remaining 1500 units to find the gate. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.103 forest avoidance + 3-state terrain + GetWorldMapPosition_Active helper + [VEH-VERIFY] dump UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.1 [VEH-VERIFY] timing UNCHANGED (still fires at first drive start). v0.14.103.2 vehicle-agnostic announce text UNCHANGED. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~25 lines: replace sweep-abort block ~3705 with bounce-arrived path, remove v0.14.103 4-frame bounce block ~3580, fix WM_SAVEMAP_TO_DWORD_SCALE constant), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103.2: Drop inCar gate from bounce-detection. v0.14.103 BAT correction — Aaron clarified he DID test car AD to Balamb Garden in the v0.14.103 BAT. The car drove (distance dropped from 8892 to 970 over 7 seconds, then oscillated 970-1500 as it bounced off Balamb Garden's walls), but bounce-detection never fired. Root cause: the inCar gate I added (`GetVehicleType(s_lastVehicle) == VEH_CAR`) requires the locomotion byte to read 32-40 (Car range), but per the v0.14.101 BAT (recorded in v0.14.102's changelog), the locomotion byte at 0x02040A5E STAYS AT 6 (Selphie foot) when in a rental car — it never reads a car value. The v0.14.103 deep research called this 'transient' but empirical testing shows it's the steady state. So s_lastVehicle stayed at 6 → inCar was always false → bounce-detection never sampled. Secondary observation from the v0.14.103 BAT: foot DWORDs WERE updating during the rental car drive (distance changed continuously). The deep research's claim that 'foot DWORDs freeze in vehicles' may only apply to Garden/Ragnarok (where the engine has dedicated vehicle modes); rental cars appear to track position via foot DWORDs. So GetWorldMapPosition's foot fallback was giving us valid position data the whole time — Piece 2's vehicle-aware path wasn't needed for rentals. v0.14.103.2 ships ONE targeted fix: drop the inCar check from bounce-detection. The bounce-detection condition becomes simply (s_keyGasHeld && dist < DRIVE_NEAR_LOCATION_DIST), so it fires whenever any drive is stuck near target with throttle held. Risks: foot AD near a non-enterable location could trigger bounce-detection too. Mitigated because foot AD typically reaches the engine's MODE_FIELD trigger before 4 consecutive frozen samples accumulate (the v0.14.96 deferred-arrival flow announces normal arrival first). For genuine 'foot is stuck and gives up' scenarios, the existing 6-stuck-check 'Stuck. Cannot reach destination' path already handles it; bounce-detection firing first with 'Arrived near X' is informationally similar and harmless. ALSO: announcement message changed from 'Dismount and walk to enter.' (assumed car) to 'The entrance may require a manual approach.' (vehicle-agnostic). v0.14.103.1 [VEH-VERIFY] dump at first drive start UNCHANGED — still dumps both candidate offsets so we get address validation data this BAT too. v0.14.103.2 BAT plan: rent a car in Balamb (or load same save state as v0.14.103 BAT), drive AD to Balamb Garden. Expected: car drives in, bounces off walls, throttle held, position frozen for ~1s → [DRIVE-BOUNCE] log line and 'Arrived near Balamb Garden. The entrance may require a manual approach.' announce + StopAutoDrive(silent). ALSO upload Logs/ff8_world.log so we can read the [VEH-VERIFY] dump from the first drive start. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.103 forest avoidance + 3-state terrain + GetWorldMapPosition_Active helper UNCHANGED. v0.14.103.1 [VEH-VERIFY] dump UNCHANGED. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~10 lines: drop inCar gate, rewrite condition, update announcement message), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103.1: Fix v0.14.103 [VEH-VERIFY] timing and offset uncertainty. v0.14.103 BAT (21:41-21:43) confirmed: build clean, terrain 3-state working (185 land/10 forest/573 ocean), foot AD to Balamb Town succeeded at 21:43:53 with arrival via MODE_FIELD, GetWorldMapPosition_Active foot-fallback path works. BUT two issues surfaced: (1) [VEH-VERIFY] dump fired at module init when savemap was uninitialized — all reads were 0, even foot DWORDs were 0. The save loaded ~20s AFTER the dump. So the diagnostic told us nothing. (2) Tracing arithmetic against the deep research's stated target addresses revealed my offset is off by 0x1000: deep research says char_pos at 0x01CFFEB8, which means struct offset = 0x01CFFEB8 - 0x01CFDC5C = 0x225C, not the 0x125C I implemented. The deep research summary stated '+0x1270 minus 0x14' but the resulting +0x125C doesn't reach the stated target — appears to be a deep-research typo (should have been +0x2270 minus 0x14 = 0x225C). v0.14.103.1 ships TWO targeted fixes: (a) Move [VEH-VERIFY] dump to fire ONCE at first drive start (after save is loaded), so the addresses being dumped have real data to read. (b) Dump BOTH candidate WORLDMAP struct offsets (+0x125C and +0x225C) at the dump site so we can see empirically which one contains live position data. The actual GetWorldMapPosition_Active code keeps using +0x125C for now; once BAT confirms which offset is correct, v0.14.103.2 patches WM_WORLDMAP_OFFSET if needed. Bounce-detection NOT yet exercised because Aaron didn't test car AD this BAT. Forest avoidance IS active (terrain grid is 3-state) but only blocks forest segments for cars, which weren't tested either. v0.14.103.1 BAT plan: rent a car in Balamb, drive AD to ANY destination. The first car-AD start fires the [VEH-VERIFY] dump showing both candidate offsets populated with whatever values the engine has stored. Compare the two — the one that looks like reasonable signed int16 coordinates (~-1000 to ~+1000 range, since these are world-map block-relative coords) is the right offset. The other will look like noise or zeros. After offset confirmation: bounce-detection should also have a chance to fire if the car-AD destination is a Garden-style location. NO new addresses (still using existing constants), NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.103 forest avoidance + 3-state terrain + GetWorldMapPosition_Active helper + bounce-detection state/logic all UNCHANGED. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~30 lines: remove [VEH-VERIFY] from Initialize, add to StartAutoDrive guarded by s_vehVerifyFired one-shot flag, dump both candidate offsets), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.103: Car AD robustness chapter — five integrated pieces in one build per Aaron's 'one bigger build' call. (Piece 1) Vehicle detection refinement: replace the v0.14.94 'early-return when s_driveActive' hotfix with a 3-sample low-pass filter on locomotion byte at 0x02040A5E gated by scene flag 0x0203ED2C == 0. Per the v0.14.103 deep research (saved at Plan & Research Documents/Vehicle state and car position deep research results.md), the locomotion byte IS the right address; the 'reads 6 in rental car' transient is a sampling rate issue (the world-map input handler runs faster than the locomotion state machine). FFNx canary reads the same byte; no lower-level pointer chain exists. Sample only when scene flag == 0; commit state change after 3 consecutive identical samples. (Piece 2) Car position read: new addresses for the savemap WORLDMAP struct, which the deep research confirmed IS maintained live in RAM during driving (refuting the prior 'PC version may omit positions' caveat — hyne's 26-byte WORLDMAP_PC describes only what gets serialised to disk; the runtime engine keeps the full PSX-format struct in memory because the driving code was ported expecting it). With 76-byte-header correction subtracting 0x14 from the 0x1270 offset: car_pos[6] (rental) at savemap+0x125C+0x30 = 0x01CFFEE8, ragnarok_pos[6] at 0x01CFFED0, bgu_pos[6] (mobile Garden) at 0x01CFFEDC, char_pos[6] (foot mirror) at 0x01CFFEB8. Layout uint16 X, Z, Y, unk, unk, rotation. Foot DWORDs at 0x0203EE80/84/88 freeze in vehicles because sub_53FAC0 switches the per-frame integrator from updating foot DWORDs to updating vehicle position arrays at mount; foot DWORDs are 'last known foot position' frozen until dismount. New helper GetWorldMapPosition_Active(x, y, z) returns vehicle position when in vehicle, foot DWORDs otherwise. UpdateAutoDrive's distance/bearing/stuck-detection calls active getter; PollKeys catalog-distance keeps using foot for catalog-from-player perspective. (Piece 3) Forest avoidance for cars: extend s_terrainGrid[24][32] from binary (0=land, 1=ocean) to 3-state enum SegTerrainClass {SEG_LAND=0, SEG_FOREST=1, SEG_OCEAN=2}. LoadTerrainGrid counts forest polygons (terrain values 0-5 per Plan & Research Documents/World Map Terrain and Locomotion Reference.md) and ocean polygons (32-34) per segment; majority-of-polygons rule classifies the segment. IsSegmentTraversable(row, col, veh) rejects SEG_FOREST when veh == VEH_CAR; all other vehicles unchanged. (Piece 4) Arrive-near-location for non-car-friendly destinations. Reuses v0.14.96 deferred-arrival mechanism for car-friendly towns (engine auto-dismounts when car drives onto town entry trigger -> MODE_FIELD fires -> existing arrival path runs unchanged — no per-location car-entry table needed). New bounce-detection state for non-car-friendly locations (Garden-style — research clarified the engine determines this via wmsetus Section 8 vehicle-mask + per-polygon terrain byte 0x0D type 29; we sidestep parsing by relying on observable behavior): when AD active in car AND throttle held (s_keyGasHeld) AND car position delta < 50 units across 4 consecutive ~250ms samples (~1 second of zero velocity with throttle held) AND distance to target < DRIVE_NEAR_LOCATION_DIST (1500), announce 'Arrived near [Location]. Dismount and walk to enter.' and StopAutoDrive(silent). Reset bounce state on AD start, AD stop, world-map exit. (Piece 5) Verification logs (diagnostic, strippable in v0.14.104). New [VEH-VERIFY] block at module init dumps the candidate WORLDMAP struct addresses with initial reads (car_pos, ragnarok_pos, bgu_pos, char_pos, car_rent flag); also fires once at first drive start for cross-validation. BAT decision tree: char_pos X coord ~ foot DWORD X / 4096 at game start confirms +0x125C arithmetic; car drive showing car_pos updating confirms address; values looking like ASCII or pointer-shaped (0x004xxxxx) means arithmetic is wrong and v0.14.103.1 patches with empirical offsets. Aaron is blind so Cheat Engine isn't accessible; the diagnostic log block is the verification mechanism. v0.14.104 strips this once verified. NO new hooks. NO build script changes. v0.14.102 A=gas key injection unchanged. v0.14.96 deferred-arrival flow unchanged. v0.14.94 vehicle-noise hotfix REPLACED by Piece 1's 3-sample low-pass + scene flag gate. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~300 lines: new constants block for scene flag + WORLDMAP struct offsets, CheckVehicleChange rewrite for Piece 1, new GetWorldMapPosition_Active helper for Piece 2, UpdateAutoDrive distance/bearing/stuck switched to active getter, SegTerrainClass enum + extended s_terrainGrid + LoadTerrainGrid 3-state classifier + IsSegmentTraversable car/forest gate for Piece 3, bounce-detection state and logic in UpdateAutoDrive for Piece 4, [VEH-VERIFY] diagnostic block for Piece 5, file-header CURRENT STATE block updated), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.102: Restore A=gas key injection for car AD. v0.14.101 BAT confirmed diagnosis (c) from the previous analysis: Aaron in the rental car with engine running, AD's distance announcements never updated (so position genuinely frozen from AD's POV) but the car was actually driving on screen — wait, actually re-examining: locomotion byte was 6 (foot) throughout, position frozen at (16031,-26948). User reminded that 'car AD was working before the whole Sonnet regression' — critical context. Searched past Claude conversations and found the missing piece: in v0.11.13/v0.11.14 the previous Claude session empirically confirmed via F12 key-state diagnostic that the rental car uses A key (VK=0x41, scan=0x1E) as the GAS PEDAL that must be held continuously, and W key (VK=0x57, scan=0x11) for reverse. Arrow keys steer ONLY; they don't accelerate the car. The v0.11.14 design always injected A whenever wantUp=true — harmless on foot (A is not bound to any walking action) and essential for car. The v0.14.x rewrite during the 'Sonnet regression' lost this, leaving SetDriveKeys with only UP/LEFT/RIGHT arrows. Cars receive arrow keys and steer correctly but never accelerate because no gas pedal is held. Foot AD works because foot uses arrow keys directly. CRUCIAL DETAIL: A and W are NOT extended keys (per v0.11.14: 'NOT extended keys (no KEYEVENTF_EXTENDEDKEY)'); arrow keys ARE extended. So the key-injection helpers need to handle both flag types. FIX: (1) Add 'extended' parameter to PressKey/ReleaseKey (default true preserves arrow-key behavior). (2) Add s_keyGasHeld state. (3) SetDriveKeys: when up=true, also press A (scan 0x1E, not extended); when up=false, release A. (4) ReleaseAllDriveKeys: also release A. NOT shipping W (reverse) yet — forward-only AD doesn't need reverse, and v0.11.14's forest-stuck recovery (reverse-turn-forward) is a separate feature deferred to v0.14.x+. Also REMOVED the v0.14.101 diagnostic '[DIAG-LOCO]' logging now that we know the answer; keeping the code clean. v0.14.100 hardcoded refined-coord baseline UNCHANGED. v0.14.100 bearing-based final-approach steering UNCHANGED. v0.14.99 sweep-abort-on-drift UNCHANGED. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.102 BAT plan: Aaron loads same save (foot, next to car), enters car, AD to Balamb Garden. Car should now actually move. Distance announcements should decrease over time. Drive should arrive or fail with normal stuck-detection (not the previous 'car never moves' failure). v0.14.103 if needed: add W key for reverse + forest-stuck recovery (reverse, turn, retry forward) per v0.11.14 design. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~25 lines: PressKey/ReleaseKey extended param ~6 lines, s_keyGasHeld state +1 line, SetDriveKeys A injection ~4 lines, ReleaseAllDriveKeys A release ~1 line, removed v0.14.101 [DIAG-LOCO] calls -10 lines, file-header CURRENT STATE block prepended ~50 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.101: DIAGNOSTIC build for the v0.14.100 BAT car-AD-no-movement issue. v0.14.100 BAT confirmed Balamb Town arrival working: drive started at dist=221, took 3 random encounters and several segments of bearing-based + sweep steering, eventually crossed the trigger at 18:25:04 with [DRIVE] Arrival via game-mode (mode=1 MODE_FIELD, fieldId=0x006A, fieldName='bcgate_1', dist=65). Refined coord auto-updated from hardcoded (12896,-26711) baseline to actual entry (12886,-26775). v0.14.99 sweep-abort-on-drift also fired correctly mid-drive when a battle drifted the player to dist=1509. SECOND BAT scenario (separate failure to diagnose): Aaron rented a car in Balamb, exited to world map (player at 12873,-26747), pressed plus to select Balamb Garden (11km away), pressed \\ to start drive. Drive started cleanly but the car NEVER MOVED — 6 stuck checks all reading 'moved 0 units in 3000ms window', then 'Stuck. Cannot reach destination'. KEY CLUE in log: '[WM-ENTRY-DEBOUNCE] Window expired with non-canonical locomotion=21; keeping s_lastVehicle=0'. So the locomotion byte at WM_LOCOMOTION (0x02040A5E) read 0x15 (21 decimal) when Aaron was in the rental car, and the canonical-value debounce didn't recognize it, leaving s_lastVehicle=0 (foot). BFS then treated him as foot too. Two possible causes for the no-movement: (a) byte 21 is a real car-driver locomotion value we haven't catalogued (rental car may have its own value distinct from story-car / Galbadia car / Edea's car); the engine sees a car driver and might respond to keystrokes differently than foot. (b) byte 21 represents a transitional state (e.g., 'in vehicle, not yet driving', 'rental shop just exited but car not engaged') in which the engine ignores movement keys until the player is fully in driving mode. To distinguish (a) from (b), need to see what byte values are active throughout the failed drive. This build adds locomotion-byte logging at TWO sites: (1) at drive start, immediately after the existing '[DRIVE] Start' log line, emit '[DIAG-LOCO] At drive start: locomotion=0xXX (decimal NN), s_lastVehicle=V'; (2) at every stuck check, immediately after the existing '[DRIVE] Stuck check' log line, emit '[DIAG-LOCO] At stuck check: locomotion=0xXX (decimal NN), s_lastVehicle=V, player=(X,Y)'. The position log on stuck check distinguishes 'truly not moving' (position unchanged across checks) from 'moving but stuck-detection threshold mismatch' (position changing slightly). NO behavior change — purely additive diagnostic. NO new addresses (WM_LOCOMOTION already in ff8_addresses.h since v0.14.50-ish; GetLocomotionMode helper already exists in world_map.cpp). NO new hooks. NO build script changes. v0.14.100 hardcoded refined-coord baseline UNCHANGED. v0.14.100 bearing-based final-approach steering UNCHANGED. v0.14.99 sweep-abort-on-drift UNCHANGED. v0.14.101 BAT plan: rent a car in Balamb (or use the same save state from this BAT), exit to world map, attempt AD to Balamb Garden. Upload Logs/ff8_world.log. Look for: (a) [DIAG-LOCO] At drive start line showing the byte value when AD starts, (b) sequence of [DIAG-LOCO] At stuck check lines tracking byte+position over the 18-second stuck-detection window. If position is unchanged across checks: the engine is genuinely not responding to AD's keystrokes when in this vehicle state — we'd then explore whether byte 21 is a 'pre-driving' transitional state and either (i) wait it out, or (ii) inject a different key sequence to engage the car. If position IS changing slightly (under DRIVE_STUCK_THRESHOLD=100 units): stuck-detection is over-sensitive for slow-moving vehicles — we'd raise the threshold or add per-vehicle thresholds. v0.14.102 ships the actual fix once we know which case it is. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~10 lines: 2 [DIAG-LOCO] log calls, file-header CURRENT STATE block prepended ~50 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.100: Balamb Town refined-coord baseline + bearing-based final-approach steering. v0.14.99 BAT (on foot, save loads at exact catalog (13249,-26779) for Balamb Town): drive started with dist=0, planner correctly says 'Player already in goal segment empty path', AD enters final approach and emits wantUp=true (walk forward). Player barely moves (62 units in 3000ms, then 0). Sweep activates after 2 stuck checks, runs all 6 phases without progress, declares 'Could not find entrance'. Drive 2 (started 2.4km away after Aaron repositioned) used bearing-based out-of-approach steering, got stuck for 6 checks, cleanly stopped with 'Stuck. Cannot reach destination'. The v0.14.99 sweep-abort-on-drift fix WORKED for the second drive (no multi-km wandering this time — stuck detection terminated cleanly). The first drive exposed the deeper architectural issue I'd noted as deferred: Balamb Town's catalog (13249,-26779) is the world-map ICON CENTER, NOT the actual gate trigger position. Prior BATs captured refined entry coords that show the actual gate is ~350 units WEST and ~70 units NORTH at (12896,-26711). When AD targets the catalog and the player is sitting AT the catalog with arbitrary save-induced heading, walk-forward goes some random direction — not necessarily toward the gate. AND refined coords aren't persistent across sessions (queued for v0.15.x), so the empirical (12896,-26711) value captured in v0.14.96/v0.14.98 is gone on this fresh session. TWO INTEGRATED CHANGES: (1) HARDCODE Balamb Town's refined-coord baseline at (12896, -26711) in Initialize. Sets s_refinedX/Y/Has[1] to the empirical entry coord captured during v0.14.98 BAT. Bootstraps the refined-coord system on fresh sessions until persistence ships (v0.15.x). Single location for now — once v0.14.100 BAT confirms the approach works for Balamb Town, expand to Balamb Garden (24647,-29675 from v0.14.90.2 or 25368,-30226 from prior data) and Fire Cavern (refined coord from prior BATs not currently documented). For now, only locations with multiple-confirmed refined captures get a baseline. (2) BEARING-BASED FINAL-APPROACH STEERING when dist > 200. Replaces the v0.11-era 'wantUp = true sweeps through the trigger band' design with proper bearing logic for the 200..1000 unit range. Below 200 units, KEEP walk-forward (avoids near-target oscillation that the original walk-forward design protected against). The original design was correct for 'player walking toward target from 500+ units offset' — the sweep-through scenario — but breaks for 'player at catalog center with arbitrary heading'. Bearing-based steering auto-corrects from any starting orientation. NEW threshold constant FINAL_APPROACH_FORWARD_DIST = 200 governs the transition. Together these mean: AD targets refined (12896,-26711) from player (13249,-26779), dist=360, bearing-based logic turns the player west-northwest, walks toward refined, reaches dist<200, walk-forward sweeps through the actual gate trigger, [DRIVE] Arrival via game-mode fires. v0.14.99 sweep-abort-on-drift UNCHANGED (still in place; will fire if a battle ever drifts the player far). v0.14.97 PLAN-DEBUG logging UNCHANGED. v0.14.98 program 9 fix UNCHANGED. v0.14.96 deferred-arrival flow UNCHANGED. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.100 BAT plan: from the same save Aaron used for v0.14.99 (loads at exact catalog for Balamb Town, on foot, story 205), press \\ to drive. Verify in Logs/ff8_world.log: (a) [INIT] Refined entry default: Balamb Town (12896,-26711) line at module init, (b) [DRIVE] Using refined entry for Balamb Town: (12896,-26711) at drive start, (c) drive starts with dist~360 instead of dist=0, (d) bearing-based steering active (no immediate stuck checks; player should move toward refined coord), (e) drive completes via deferred-arrival OR fails cleanly via 6-stuck-check 'Stuck. Cannot reach destination'. NO more 'Could not find entrance' from sweep exhausting near the catalog. RISK: bearing-based steering in 200-1000 range could cause oscillation around target if the engine's turning is twitchy. Mitigated by walk-forward below 200 units. If oscillation manifests in the BAT (player turning back-and-forth without crossing trigger), v0.14.101 would tighten the threshold or add hysteresis. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~30 lines: hardcoded refined defaults in Initialize ~10 lines, FINAL_APPROACH_FORWARD_DIST constant + final-approach steering rewrite ~15 lines, file-header CURRENT STATE block prepended ~70 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.99: Fix unreachable v0.14.97 sweep-abort-on-drift. v0.14.98 second BAT (Aaron testing car AD from save outside Balamb Town): drive started near target with dist oscillating around the FINAL_APPROACH_DIST=1000 boundary. Stuck-in-final-approach detection fired correctly, sweep activated. First sweep walk drove the car 4000+ units west in 3 seconds (cars are fast; locomotion byte read 0 due to v0.14.94 hotfix blocking vehicle updates during AD, so isOnFoot=true and 'wantUp=true for 3 seconds' translated to 4km of car travel). Random encounter at dist=4217. Re-entry replanned correctly (player still in region 0x07 multi-segment area, empty-path) BUT sweep state persisted across re-entry. More sweep walks, more drift, more battles — eventually exhausted at phase 6 declaring 'Could not find entrance' at dist=6800 from target. ROOT CAUSE: the v0.14.97 sweep-abort-on-drift check was placed in the else branch of 'if (isOnFoot && dist < DRIVE_FINAL_APPROACH_DIST)'. But the sweep state machine has an early-return at the top of UpdateAutoDrive that fires BEFORE the final-approach branch is ever evaluated. So when sweep was active (the only time the abort would matter), the abort code was unreachable. v0.14.98 first BAT didn't catch this because sweep didn't activate during that drive (Aaron started 596 units from target and never got stuck long enough). FIX: move the sweep-abort check to BEFORE the sweep state machine in UpdateAutoDrive. Same logic, reachable placement. When player drifts to dist > FINAL_APPROACH_DIST * 1.5 (1500 units) while sweep is active, clear sweep state and let normal steering take over. For cars this means a single sweep walk will trigger abort on the next tick (since one car walk easily covers 1500 units), preventing the cascade. For foot, single walks stay within grace (~200-300 units) so the abort only fires when a battle has drifted the player. Also REMOVED the now-redundant abort check in the final-approach else branch. v0.14.97 PLAN-DEBUG logging UNCHANGED. v0.14.98 program 9 fix UNCHANGED. v0.14.96 deferred-arrival flow UNCHANGED. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.99 BAT plan: from a save in a car near Balamb Town, press \\ to drive there. Verify the log shows: drive starts, possibly enters final approach, possibly sweep activates if stuck, but if sweep walks drift the car far from target, [DRIVE-SWEEP] Aborting (drifted out of final approach: dist=..., threshold=1500.0) line fires within 1-2 ticks, sweep clears, normal steering resumes, drive either arrives or hits 'Stuck. Cannot reach destination' from the regular stuck-detection path. NOTE: deeper issue surfaced — region 0x07 spans multiple segments (seg(17,20) AND seg(16,21) both in region 0x07), so 'Player already in goal segment' empty-path detection is too coarse for narrow-gate locations like Balamb Town. The actual gate trigger is a sub-segment area within seg(17,20). Drives that drift to seg(16,21) are in the goal region but not at the gate; normal steering should aim for refined entry coords in that case. Future improvement (v0.15.x): when planner says 'empty path' AND refined coord exists, steer toward refined coord rather than catalog/segment center. Also future: cosmetic 'Entered final approach zone' log spam from dist oscillating around the 1000 boundary — add edge-detection so the message fires once per crossing. Both deferred to keep v0.14.99 minimal. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~25 lines: new sweep-abort block at top of UpdateAutoDrive ~17 lines, removed dead-code abort block in else branch ~10 lines, file-header CURRENT STATE block prepended ~50 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.98: Planner-decline fix for Balamb Town based on v0.14.97 BAT diagnostic. The PLAN-DEBUG trace at 17:07:01 confirmed the v0.14.96 hypothesis exactly: program 9 (loc_id=0x010B) was rejected with 'SKIP top_story=[290,0) story=205 out of window', and region 0x07 (Balamb Town's actual entry trigger segment-region byte) was absent from the active region set after walking all 38 programs. Active set was 16 regions {0x21,0x22,0x24,0x09,0x03,0x01,0x00,0x02,0x16,0x17,0x04,0x19,0x27,0x10,0x26,0x25} with 0x07 missing. Closest-active-region search picked region 0x09 at seg(17,1) which is on the wrong side of the map; planner declined; drive fell back to v0.14.86 catalog-center steering. ROOT CAUSE INSIGHT from the trace: program 9's clauses have story windows [0..490) for region 0x06 and [0..3900) for region 0x07 — BOTH including story values below the supposed top-level gate of 290. If the gate were genuinely top-level, those pre-290 windows would be unreachable code. The Python disassembler in the v0.14.92 BAT put the 0xFF02 0x0122 (≥290) operand in the wrong scope; it almost certainly belongs to clause 0 (giving region 0x06 effective window [290,490) for disc-2 Balamb-during-Garden-departure). Clause 1 (foot, region 0x07, story [0,3900)) is the canonical Balamb Town foot-entry gate that the engine fires at story 205 (independently confirmed by the v0.14.96 BAT log: Aaron entered Balamb Town at story 205 with fieldName='bcgate_1'). FIX: change s_triggerPrograms[9].top_story_gte from 290 to 0. Region 0x07 now joins the active set at any story, the closest-active-region search picks it at segDist=0 from catalog seg(17,20), and PlanPath produces an actual route (or empty path if player already in goal segment). v0.14.97 PLAN-DEBUG trace logging UNCHANGED — still emits per-program/per-clause lines so we can confirm the fix worked and audit other locations. v0.14.97 sweep-abort-on-drift UNCHANGED. v0.14.96 deferred-arrival flow UNCHANGED. NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. The fix is a single value change in src/world_map.cpp's s_triggerPrograms[9] table entry plus comment expansion explaining the diagnosis. v0.14.98 BAT plan: drive from current save (story=205, on foot, near western Balamb coast) toward Balamb Town. Verify in Logs/ff8_world.log: (a) [PLAN-DEBUG] [09] loc=0x010B clause 0/1 lines now show PASS for region 0x07, (b) Active region set after walk now includes 0x07 (17 regions instead of 16), (c) [PLAN] line shows 'closest active region 0x07 at seg(17,20) segDist=0' (or similar in-segment match), (d) PlanPath either succeeds with a path or recognizes player already in goal segment, (e) drive completes via deferred-arrival as in the v0.14.96 BAT. If this fix works, audit other story-gated programs (4, 9, 10, 13, 14 are the candidates with clause-local story windows; programs 0, 3, 4, 8, 16, 21, 23-30, 32, 34 with high top-level gates and no clause-local windows are correctly gated). FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~20 lines: s_triggerPrograms[9] table entry top_story_gte 290 → 0, comment explaining diagnosis), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

  // v0.14.97: Planner-decline diagnostic + sweep gating + refined-coord goal cell. Post-v0.14.96 push BAT showed every drive declines the planner with [PLAN] No path from seg(X,Y) to any of N goal cells. Root cause hypothesized: Balamb Town's actual trigger region is 0x07, referenced only by program 9 which requires top_story_gte=290; Aaron's story flag is 205, so program 9 is filtered out, region 0x07 falls out of the active set, closest-active-region search picks an unrelated region at an unrelated segment, planner declines, drive falls back to v0.14.86 catalog-center steering with no terrain awareness. Side effect: sweep search activates from stuck detection while the player is kilometers from the target (not in final-approach zone), wasting all 6 sweep phases at the wrong location. THREE INTEGRATED CHANGES: (1) [PLAN-DEBUG] clause-rejection trace logging in MatchProgramForCatalog. When a drive starts, walk every TriggerProgram and emit one log line per program: 'program [NN] locID=0xXXXX top_vehicle=0xVV top_story=[A,B) num_clauses=K' followed by per-clause lines 'clause N: vehicle=0xVV region=0xRR story=[A,B) unk_flags=0xFF' with explicit pass/fail reason for each clause (vehicle-mismatch / story-out-of-window / region-not-in-segment-map / OK). Then a summary line: 'active regions for veh=V story=S: {list of region bytes}'. This will reveal whether (a) our 0xFF02/0xFF03 operand decode is wrong (operands swapped, sign-extension, etc), (b) we're missing a program that handles pre-mobile-Garden Balamb entry, or (c) program 9's bounds need correction. (2) Tighten sweep gating in UpdateAutoDrive. Sweep only activates if s_finalApproachEnterTick != 0 (player crossed into final-approach zone in this drive AND we tracked it) AND the current distance is still below FINAL_APPROACH_DIST*1.5 (a small grace band; if the player drifted out of final approach due to a battle they can re-enter and sweep can re-arm). Stuck-far-from-target falls through: announce 'Cannot reach destination' once and stop, instead of wandering in sweep patterns kilometers away. (3) Use refined coords for the planner goal cell. If s_refinedHas[locIdx] is true, MatchProgramForCatalog receives the refined (X,Y) instead of the catalog (X,Y), so the closest-active-region search runs from the empirical entry segment. For Balamb Town this doesn't directly help (catalog and refined land in the same segment), but is the right architecture going forward and helps any location where the catalog and refined coords land in different segments (e.g. wide-entrance locations where the icon center is far from the entry trigger). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.96 deferred-arrival flow unchanged. v0.14.95 closest-active-region search still in place; v0.14.97 only adds visibility into why it's declining. v0.14.94 vehicle-noise hotfix and Section 2 loader unchanged. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~150-180 lines: PLAN-DEBUG trace block in MatchProgramForCatalog/MatchProgramForCatalogEx ~80 lines, sweep gating tighten in UpdateAutoDrive ~15 lines, refined-coord wiring in StartAutoDrive ~10 lines, file-header CURRENT STATE block update ~30 lines, plus a new failure-stop path for stuck-far-from-target ~15 lines), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). v0.14.97 BAT plan: drive from current save toward Balamb Town, cancel after a few seconds. Upload Logs/ff8_world.log. We expect [PLAN-DEBUG] lines to fire at drive start with full clause-rejection trace; that diagnostic dump tells us exactly which clauses are getting filtered out and why. v0.14.98 ships the actual fix once the trace is analyzed.

  // v0.14.96: Fix v0.14.95 BAT false-positive arrivals — drives to Balamb and Balamb Garden announced 'Arrived' when a random encounter fired within 1500 units of the destination, because the v0.14.95 distance fallback can't distinguish 'world map exited because trigger fired' from 'world map exited because random encounter started'. Both look the same at the moment of exit (scene flag flips, dist-to-target unchanged). FIX per Aaron's suggestion: use the game's settled game mode after the world-map exit to disambiguate. The mode register at FF8Addresses::pGameMode (already resolved at startup, used by IsOnField etc.) takes 1-3 polls to transition from MODE_WORLDMAP (2) to its destination mode after IsOnWorldMap flips false. New deferred-arrival state machine: when world map exits while a drive is active, capture the exit tick, release drive keys, set s_driveAwaitingArrivalDecision=true (drive stays active so cancel still works). Subsequent Poll() ticks call new ResolveDeferredArrival() which reads pGameMode and decides: MODE_FIELD (1) = real arrival → announce + capture refined coord + StopAutoDrive; MODE_SWIRL (3) / MODE_BATTLE (999) / MODE_AFTER_BATTLE (4) = encounter → log paused, drive resumes on world-map re-entry; anything else (incl. lingering MODE_WORLDMAP=2) = keep waiting up to 2 seconds. On timeout, falls back to the v0.14.95 segment-membership / distance heuristic as safety net (logged with 'timeout-fallback' suffix). Required Poll() restructuring: the early-return on !s_onWorldMap had to allow ResolveDeferredArrival to run for the duration of the wait; new path calls ResolveDeferredArrival BEFORE the early return when s_driveAwaitingArrivalDecision is true. Logs the field name from FF8Addresses::pCurrentFieldName on arrival for diagnostic clarity (e.g. 'Arrival via game-mode (mode=1 MODE_FIELD, fieldId=0x002F, fieldName=balamb_town_entrance, ...)'). v0.14.95 closest-active-region planner unchanged; v0.14.94 vehicle-noise hotfix and Section 2 loader unchanged. NO new addresses (pGameMode, pCurrentFieldId, pCurrentFieldName all already exposed by ff8_addresses.h since v04.00 / v01.13). NO new hooks. NO build script changes. v0.14.95 BAT log diagnosis: 12:43:36 declared 'Arrival via exit-distance (fallback)' for Balamb Garden at lastPos=(25405,-30324) dist=1215 — but the player had drifted 1100 units southward in 3 seconds which is encounter behavior, not field-entry. Same pattern at 12:51:26 for Balamb Town. Both false positives are gone in v0.14.96 because pGameMode reads MODE_BATTLE/SWIRL during those exits, not MODE_FIELD. ALSO from v0.14.95 BAT: Sections 9 and 19 are NOT region maps — they look like other data (still 16 active regions in MatchProgramForCatalog after their dump); the multi-section region-map hypothesis is wrong and v0.14.96 doesn't extend the dump list further. Resolving the true source of the missing 26 region IDs is deferred to v0.14.97+; the deferred-arrival fix in v0.14.96 makes the existing v0.14.95 closest-active-region planner correctly identify arrivals at Balamb Garden / Balamb Town anyway via the segment-membership branch when pGameMode says MODE_FIELD. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~150 net lines: ResolveDeferredArrival function ~120 lines, state variables ~5 lines, Poll() restructuring ~15 lines, StopAutoDrive reset ~3 lines, Initialize reset ~3 lines, file-header CURRENT STATE block rewritten), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).  // v0.14.95: Fix v0.14.94 BAT failure where MatchProgramForCatalog NEVER matched any program for any catalog target. Root cause: catalog (X, Y) for each location is the icon center on the world map, which sits on OPEN LAND (e.g. Balamb Garden's catalog seg(19,20) maps to region 0x0C, Balamb Town's seg(17,20) to region 0x07). Region 0x0C has only one program (prog20 Garden-vehicle); region 0x07 has only prog9 (story ≥290). Neither program is satisfiable from foot+story-205 (Aaron's BAT state), so the v0.14.94 'match the catalog segment's region directly' algorithm returned -1 for every drive — every drive fell back to v0.14.93's distance-based arrival. Drives to Balamb Garden and Balamb Town succeeded via the 1500-unit fallback because their refined-coords land within range; Fire Cavern (catalog 6800 units off the actual trigger zone) couldn't possibly succeed via that fallback. NEW algorithm: build the active region SET first — every region byte referenced by ANY clause currently satisfiable from veh + story — then walk the 32x24 grid looking for the segment closest to (catRow, catCol) whose region is in that active set. 5-segment distance cap avoids accidentally routing to some other location's trigger when nothing nearby matches; in that case decline and fall back to catalog-center steering. ALSO: extends WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX[] from {2,7,8} to {2,7,8,9,19} for v0.14.96+ investigation. v0.14.94 BAT log shows Section 2 alone has only 19 unique region IDs (300 of 768 cells populated) but s_triggerPrograms[] references 45 unique regions — hypothesis: the world map's trigger regions are split across multiple 32x24 region maps in different wmsetus sections (Sections 9 and 19 are also 772 bytes each, the same shape as Section 2). v0.14.96 will load these and merge into a multi-section region map if the dumps confirm the hypothesis. ALSO: enhances the [DRIVE] Paused log to include the player's exit segment + region byte (alongside the existing lastPos field) so post-pause analysis can confirm whether the trigger fired in a known-active region or not. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~110 net lines: MatchProgramForCatalog rewritten ~80 lines, dump list extension 1 line, Paused log enhancement ~10 lines, plus unchanged comment blocks updated), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.94 vehicle-noise hotfix and Section 2 loader unchanged. v0.14.93 [TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] init logging unchanged. Three integrated changes addressing v0.14.93 BAT findings: (1) VEHICLE-NOISE HOTFIX in CheckVehicleChange — early-return when s_driveActive prevents AD's keybd_event arrow-key injection from polluting s_lastVehicle via the locomotion byte at 0x02040A5E (which cycles through canonical Car/Garden/Ragnarok values during AD operation, each held >64ms past the v0.14.90 4-poll debounce). Resolves Issues 1+2 from the BAT: 'Car' announcements during drives + sweep search not firing when stuck in final approach (s_lastVehicle stays at its pre-drive foot value, so isOnFoot stays true, so the sweep guard fires correctly). (2) SECTION 2 LOADER captures the 32×24 segment-region byte map from wmsetus.obj into s_segmentRegionMap[24][32] at module init. Loader extends the existing LoadTriggerZones (which already reads the same archive for diagnostic dumping) — no duplicate I/O. Indexing: byte at offset row*32+col with no header (the 4-byte trailer at offset 768 was previously misdocumented as a header in v0.14.93's comment; corrected). (3) A* PATH PLANNER replaces v0.14.86's linear-direction steering. New PlanPath(start_seg, vehicle, goal_seg_set) runs A* on the 32×24 grid with 4-neighbor edges (foot/Chocobo/Car: land-only via existing s_terrainGrid; Ship/Garden: any segment; Ragnarok: skips the planner entirely as before), wrap-aware Manhattan heuristic (east-west torus shortcut for Esthar approaches), uniform edge cost. New goal-set construction MatchProgramForCatalog(catalog_x, catalog_y, vehicle, story) walks s_triggerPrograms[] looking for any clause satisfiable from current state (vehicle predicate matches AND story window contains the savemap word at [0x2036bde/0x2036bdf]); collects every cell in s_segmentRegionMap whose byte matches that clause's region operand. Multi-target A* terminates at the first goal segment popped from the priority queue, which is provably the closest reachable goal. StartAutoDrive runs the planner once and stores the path in s_drivePath[]; UpdateAutoDrive steers toward s_drivePath[s_drivePathIdx]'s segment center and advances when the player crosses into it. ARRIVAL: replaces v0.14.90.2's distance heuristic — when the world map exits AND the player's last segment was in the goal set, AD declares arrival. This fixes Issue 3 from BAT (Fire Cavern entry at seg(19,20) was 6807 units from catalog seg(20,20) and was misclassified as 'Paused' by the 1500-unit threshold; now any goal segment in the trigger zone counts). REPLAN on world-map re-entry after battle pause because random encounters drift the player off the planned path. Sweep search and stuck detection STAY as fallbacks for sub-segment failures (planner says traversable but a sub-segment obstacle blocks). UNCHANGED: keybd_event injection, camera-axis projection (still per-field calibrated), pause/resume, BFS catalog reachability filter, refined-coord capture (still records the player's actual entry position for future sessions). NO new addresses (savemap word [0x2036bde/0x2036bdf] is documented in s_triggerPrograms[] schema; this build adds the read), NO new hooks, NO build script changes (all in src/world_map.cpp). FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~600 new lines: s_segmentRegionMap state + Section 2 extraction inside LoadTriggerZones, GetCurrentStoryFlag helper + savemap address, ClauseMatches/MatchProgramForCatalog/CollectGoalSegments helpers, A* PlanPath core, s_drivePath state, StartAutoDrive integration, UpdateAutoDrive waypoint follower, segment-membership arrival check, CheckVehicleChange early-return, file-header CURRENT STATE block rewritten), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). v0.14.93 trigger-data embedding unchanged — this build wires it into AD targeting. v0.14.93 [TRIGGER-DUMP]/[TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] init logging unchanged. // v0.14.93: Chapter 3 Stage 4 deliverable — embed the 38 decoded field-entry trigger programs from the v0.14.92 BAT (Section 8 of wmsetus.obj) as a static s_triggerPrograms[] C++ array in src/world_map.cpp, and extend the section dump list to {2, 7, 8} so Section 2 (the 32×24 segment-region byte map referenced by 0xFF08 region operands) gets captured. Schema: TriggerClause = {vehicle, region, story_gte, story_lt, unk_flags}; TriggerProgram = {loc_id, top_story_gte, top_story_lt, top_vehicle, num_clauses, *clauses}. Vehicle operands per the artifact's encoding table: 0x80 = Squall foot (most common), 0x84 = alt-leader foot (treated as foot for AD), 0x30 = Garden (mobile B-Garden), 0x31 = Chocobo, 0x32 = Ragnarok, 0 = no restriction (used in clauses for top-level-vehicle programs). Region operands are bytes referenced into Section 2's segment map. story_gte=0 means no lower bound, story_lt=0 means +∞ upper bound. unk_flags is a bitmask recording which 0xFF0F/10/11/12/13/20/21 opcodes appeared in the original clause; operand values themselves preserved verbatim in `Plan & Research Documents/wmsetus Section 8 decoded.md` for future reference. NEW [TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] log block at module Initialize walks s_triggerPrograms[] and emits one line per program (38 lines total) for runtime sanity-check that the embedded data compiled correctly — gives Aaron a way to confirm the data is in the binary as expected without running an AD test. NO game behavior change — s_triggerPrograms[] is defined but not yet wired into StartAutoDrive's targeting; v0.14.94 wires it in and demotes sweep-search to fallback-only for locations whose programs use unmodeled UNK opcodes or have no clauses (program 18, the late-game Chocobo-only mobile destination). AD's existing catalog-center steering + sweep-search fallback continues to work unchanged. BAT plan: build, launch the game once, upload Logs/ff8_world.log. Verify (a) [TRIGGER-DUMP] sect02 begin (size=772) appears with the 4-byte header + 768 segment-region bytes (range 0..0x45 per the decoded artifact's region usage summary), (b) [TRIGGER-DUMP] sect07 and sect08 unchanged from v0.14.92, (c) [TRIGGER-PROGRAMS] block shows 38 programs with location IDs in the decoded artifact's order (0x0031, 0x0051, 0x0091, ..., 0x02C1). FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~280 lines: TriggerClause + TriggerProgram structs + UNK flag constants + vehicle alias constants in the constants block, 38 clause arrays + 38-entry program table, LogTriggerPrograms function, WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX[] extended to {2,7,8}, file-header CURRENT STATE block updated for v0.14.93 scope, Initialize call site for the new dumper), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO schema changes, NO build script changes. v0.14.92 dump infrastructure unchanged. // v0.14.92: Chapter 3 Stage 4 diagnostic build — hex-dump wmsetus.obj Sections 7 and 8 to ff8_world.log to surface the FIELD ENTRY trigger bytecode. v0.14.91 BAT proved the deep research's leading hypothesis wrong: Sections 17/18 are wm2field-style destination data (32-byte records with field-walkmesh coords ±10000 keyed on wmField IDs 0x07-0x10), not world-map trigger geometry. Zero (X,Y) catalog matches at any encoding/stride with proximity ≤2000. Subsequent disassembly hunt of FF8_EN.exe (using the Game Files/disassembly/ tree) identified the real architecture: the world-map tick at sub_53FAC0 calls sub_545EA0 each frame on foot ([0x2036b70]==0). sub_545EA0 reads from Section 8 of wmsetus.obj (resolved at runtime as [0x2040070], set up by sub_542DA0's 8th iteration — confirmed by reading sub_542DA0's body which iterates through the 48-entry section header storing each section's absolute pointer to a fixed .data slot). Section 8 (2652 bytes) is a BYTECODE PROGRAM with ~56 different opcodes (range 0xFF02..0xFF38) dispatched via a jump table at sub_546100. Opcodes include rectangle-bounds checks (compare player coords against entry's u16 literals), savemap story-flag comparisons (e.g. byte at [0x2036bde/0x2036bdf]), AND/OR combinators, multi-stage matching states (modes 1-6 maintained in sub_545F10's parser), and a follow-link instruction (0xFF0E) that lets locations share sub-programs. When a program path evaluates to 'match', the wmField ID it carries gets written and the field-entry transition fires via sub_544630. (sub_541C80 is the separate ENCOUNTER trigger using Section 1's terrain+vehicle key table indexed by Section 2's 32x24 region map; that's a step-counter accumulator system not relevant for AD steering.) Why this matters for AD: B-Garden's program is a wide-rectangle bounds check (entrable from any direction). Balamb Town's program is a tight rectangle just covering the gate (entrable only from the south). Catalog-center steering misses Balamb Town because the catalog X/Y is the town center, not the gate. Once Section 8 is decoded, we know each location's exact entry rectangle and AD targets the rectangle center directly. THIS BUILD: src/world_map.cpp's existing LoadTriggerZones() function (renamed-in-comment to 'configurable section dumper'; function name kept stable to minimize diff) gets a new WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX[] array driving which sections to dump. v0.14.91 dumped {17,18}; v0.14.92 dumps {7,8}. Section 7 (56 bytes) is the small adjacent section possibly serving as auxiliary metadata; Section 8 (2652 bytes) is the bytecode — ~170 log rows total, trivial cost. The 48-entry section table is still logged in full as a layout sanity-check. NO game behavior change — still purely diagnostic at module init. Aaron BATs by launching the game once and uploading ff8_world.log; Claude then writes a Python disassembler in the bash sandbox using the opcode dispatch table mapped from sub_546100 + sub_545F10, runs it against the dumped bytes, and emits per-location entry geometry. v0.14.93 hardcodes the decoded data as a static s_triggerData[] array; v0.14.94 wires it into StartAutoDrive's targeting (use rectangle center over catalog center) + arrival check (use rectangle bounds), demoting sweep-search to fallback-only for locations whose programs use opcodes not yet decoded. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~80 lines: WMSETUS_DUMP_SECTIONS_1IDX + WMSETUS_DUMP_COUNT constants, removed WMSETUS_TRIGGER_SECT_A/B_IDX, file-header CURRENT STATE block fully rewritten for the corrected architecture, constants block expanded with the section→runtime-pointer table from sub_542DA0, LoadTriggerZones header comment updated, dump loop replaces the two hardcoded safeDump calls), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO schema changes, NO build script changes. v0.14.91 logic unchanged in every other respect. //

// v0.14.90.3: Suppress locomotion-byte noise during the world-map re-entry animation. v0.14.90.2 BAT (clean save outside B-Garden, drives Garden→Garden short test + Garden→Balamb-Town long drive) confirmed AD works end-to-end: distance-based arrival decision fires correctly on entry to both targets, refined-coord capture works (Balamb Garden refined to (24647,-29675), Balamb Town to (12889,-26835)), persist-through-battle works across three random encounters with three pause/resume cycles. Chapter 2 functionally complete. BUT BAT log surfaces a residual issue: every world-map re-entry post-battle fires THREE consecutive `Vehicle change:` announcements over ~3 seconds (e.g. at 14:38:06–14:38:08: 'On foot (mode 0)', 'Ship (mode 3)', 'On foot (mode 6)'), each followed by a BFS catalog rebuild. These are NOT real vehicle changes — Aaron is on foot the whole time. The locomotion byte at WM_LOCOMOTION cycles through canonical values during the camera zoom-in animation, each held for ~1 second (well past v0.14.90's 4-poll/~64ms debounce). Result for the user: three jarring spurious announcements per battle exit + spurious catalog rebuilds (foot→ocean-allowed→foot). Worse than just audio noise — if Aaron pressed `=` during the 1-second 'Ship' window, he'd cycle through the 38-entry ocean catalog he can't actually reach. THIS BUILD: time-gate CheckVehicleChange for the first WM_ENTRY_DEBOUNCE_MS (3000ms) after every world-map entry. The animation residue gets silently dropped during the window. At window expiry, snapshot whatever the byte reads as the new s_lastVehicle baseline (silently — no announce, no rebuild). Real vehicle actions outside the window (boarding Ship at a dock, mounting Ragnarok, dismounting Garden) still announce normally because they happen during steady gameplay, not during animation transitions. Edge case: scripted story events that mount/dismount during the 3-second post-battle window would have their announce dropped — accepted because such events are rare and the next legitimate transition restores the chain. Initial catalog build on world-map entry still uses GetLocomotionMode() directly (not gated by debounce) — in the BAT log this read 0 (foot) immediately on entry which was correct; if the byte happens to read transient noise at the exact first poll, the catalog would be wrong but a subsequent stable read would re-trigger via class-change rebuild after the debounce window. Files: src/world_map.cpp (~30 lines: WM_ENTRY_DEBOUNCE_MS constant + s_wmEntryTick state + suppression block at top of CheckVehicleChange + entry-tick capture in Poll() world-map entry handler + reset in Initialize), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO schema changes, NO build script changes. v0.14.90.2 distance-based arrival decision unchanged. v0.14.84: World Map nav post-BAT follow-up. v0.14.83 fixed the two reported regressions (vehicle spam + dead -/=/ Backspace nav keys), confirmed by BAT log showing exactly one 'Ship (mode 3)' announce on boarding and [KEY] log entries on every nav keypress. BAT also surfaced two FURTHER missing pieces Aaron remembers from before the v0.14.24 build damage: (1) entity catalog used to filter/sort differently than the current flat 37-entry distance sort, (2) the `\` key used to trigger auto-drive. Investigation: GitHub `main` at `7c7afdf3` (v0.14.82) and the v0.13.46-0.13.61 catch-up commit `f23c3eb` both contain the SAME minimal v0.11.16-baseline world_map.cpp we have locally — neither BFS terrain filtering nor auto-drive code has ever been pushed to GitHub. Those features were either lost in Aaron's local working tree during the v0.14.24 build damage and never restored, or were designed-but-never-implemented. The research is rich and present on disk: `Plan & Research Documents/World Map Accessibility deep research results.md`, `World Map Terrain and Locomotion Reference.md`, `wmx.obj polygon format deep research findings.md`, `Auto-Drive Architecture Review.md`, `World Map Location Coordinates Research Findings.md`. Full restoration is multi-session work and is now Priority 1 in NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT. THIS BUILD scope is intentionally narrow: (a) add `\` key handler (`VK_OEM_5`) to PollKeys that speaks 'World map auto-drive is not yet implemented in this build.' and logs `[KEY] backslash placeholder` — gives Aaron audible feedback that the keypress is being received instead of silent failure; (b) tighten the WM_LOCOMOTION whitelist comment to record the research-doc finding that legitimate locomotion values include 0/6/10/31/32 (Squall foot, Selphie foot, train, Chocobo, invisible car) AND the 1/2/3/4 our GetVehicleName uses for Car/Chocobo/Ship/Ragnarok — there is a known disagreement (research says Chocobo=31, our code says Chocobo=2) that needs empirical verification against actual gameplay; for now the v0.14.83 `vehicle > 4` whitelist is preserved because it correctly catches Squall foot (0), Ship (3), and Ragnarok (4) which are Aaron's immediate test surface, while suppressing the multi-byte-skid noise that motivated the fix. NO scope change to catalog logic or new auto-drive infrastructure in this build — those are deliberately deferred. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (~10 lines: VK_OEM_5 handler + Selphie/etc. annotation in vehicle whitelist comment), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO new hooks, NO build script changes. v0.14.83 fix unchanged and on top of v0.14.82 GitHub state, so v0.14.83+v0.14.84 will go to GitHub as a single combined commit once the world map restoration roadmap is settled. //

// v0.14.83: World Map navigation regression fix. Two-bug repair stemming from the v0.14.24 build damage / v0.14.31 partial recovery (the 'Sonnet fiasco'). BUG 1 (dead nav keys): WorldMap::HandleKeyPress was defined inside namespace WorldMap in src/world_map.cpp but never declared in src/world_map.h and never called from anywhere — orphaned during the v0.14.31 recovery, which restored Update() and Shutdown() but missed the keyboard dispatch. World map nav keys (- = Backspace) have been silently dead since then. FIX: replaced the orphaned HandleKeyPress with a private static PollKeys() invoked at the end of Poll(), modeled on FieldNavigation::HandleKeys. Edge-detected GetAsyncKeyState for VK_OEM_MINUS (cycle previous location), VK_OEM_PLUS (cycle next), VK_BACK (announce bearing). Implicitly gated on s_onWorldMap because Poll() early-returns when off world map. No collision with FieldNavigation's identical key set: FieldNavigation::HandleKeys gates on FF8Addresses::IsOnField() which is mutually exclusive with WM scene flag (0x0203ED2C == 0). New [KEY] log lines per handler so future regressions are obvious in ff8_world.log. BUG 2 (vehicle-change spam): the byte at WM_LOCOMOTION = 0x02040A5E reads canonical vehicle modes (0/1/2/3/4) only at the instant of mounting/dismounting, then drifts through non-canonical residue (animation phase or unrelated counter) during steady locomotion. v0.14.82 ff8_world.log showed the Ragnarok session producing locomotion-byte values 0 → 4 → 8 → 12 → 16 → 20 → ... → 60 over ~15 s, triggering 'Vehicle change: Unknown vehicle' on every poll. Same pattern in the Ship sessions: 3 → 6 → 9 → 12 → 16 → 19. FIX: whitelist guard in CheckVehicleChange — values outside the known mode set {0=On foot, 1=Car, 2=Chocobo, 3=Ship, 4=Ragnarok} are treated as 'no change' (s_lastVehicle not updated, nothing spoken), so transient residue is silently dropped while legitimate transitions still announce because the byte does pass through a canonical value at the moment of mount/dismount. Per the NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT guardrail, this is a root-cause fix for broken change-detection rather than a blanket announce throttle. Also fixed Initialize() log to use FF8OPC_VERSION via %s instead of the hard-coded 'v0.11.16' per the one-version-source convention from v0.13.45. FILES: src/world_map.cpp (PollKeys added, orphaned HandleKeyPress removed, CheckVehicleChange whitelisted, Initialize log uses FF8OPC_VERSION), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. The deeper question of where the canonical current-vehicle byte lives if 0x02040A5E is genuinely a state-machine field is deferred — the whitelist captures every legitimate transition because the byte always reads a canonical value at the mount/dismount moment, so the address question is academic for the announce path and can wait for a future investigation if the announce ever feels wrong. BAT plan: walk on world map, press - and = to cycle locations — each press should produce one location announce; press Backspace and confirm bearing announce; mount Ragnarok and confirm exactly one 'Ragnarok.' announce; fly for 30+ seconds and confirm complete silence between mount and dismount; dismount and confirm exactly one 'On foot.' announce. Check ff8_world.log for [KEY] entries on each keypress. v0.14.82 Scan TTS 50% Vulnerable cutoff unchanged. //

// v0.14.82: Vulnerable threshold relaxed from 60% to 50% to capture canon vulnerabilities that landed just below the conservative cutoff. v0.14.81 BAT validated five scans (Fastitocalon, Glacial Eye, Caterchipillar, two Bite Bugs) with all predictions matching log output exactly, but cross-reference against nightsolo canon revealed three vulnerabilities being dropped that should have announced: Bite Bug Sleep (chance ~56%), Caterchipillar Sleep (~53%), Fastitocalon Darkness (~56%). All three are listed as nightsolo canon vulnerabilities and would land on average more than half the time. Lowering threshold to 50% re-includes them while still correctly dropping Fastitocalon Sleep (12%) and Glacial Eye Sleep (32%) — the high-Spirit cases that motivated the chance-based model. THIS BUILD: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusWeaknesses, single-line threshold change `chance >= 60` -> `chance >= 50`, plus comment block updated to record the 60->50 lesson. PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENT CHANGES from v0.14.81 BAT: Bite Bug "...and Zombie. Vulnerable to Sleep." (Sleep added in second tier). Caterchipillar same: "Highly vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie. Vulnerable to Sleep." Fastitocalon: "Vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie." (Darkness added). Glacial Eye unchanged (Sleep at 32% still silent). FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp (~1 line code + ~25 line comment update), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.80 FormatStatusResistances unchanged. v0.14.79 popup-hook fix unchanged.  // v0.14.81: Status weakness tiering switched from byte-only to chance-based, accounting for target Spirit. v0.14.80 BAT exposed a real gap: Fastitocalon was announced as 'Vulnerable to Sleep' (byte=50) but Sleep landed 0/12 casts in actual play because Fastitocalon's Spirit=177 makes the actual direct-magic-cast inflict chance only ~13%. Per the FF Wiki Magic page, direct status spell casts use the formula `Inflict% = Magic/4 - Spirit/4 + 100 - byte` (StatusAttack fixed at 200 for direct casts). v0.14.81 implements that formula with assumed player Magic = 30 (representative low-mid game caster), then tiers on the resulting estimated chance: chance >= 95% -> 'Highly vulnerable'; chance 60-94% -> 'Vulnerable'; chance < 60% -> silent (drops out of weakness announce). Conservative thresholds chosen so that 'Vulnerable' really does mean 'will land most casts' rather than 'might land sometimes'. Statuses with byte >= 100 still flow to FormatStatusResistances on key 9 (Resists/Immune to tiers from v0.14.80), unchanged. PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENT CHANGES per the v0.14.79 BAT bytes: Fastitocalon key 0 (Spirit=177) - Sleep drops out (chance ~13%); other low-byte statuses still announced because byte=0..1 with Spirit=177 still gives chance ~50-57% -- wait, that means Death/Poison/Petrify/Silence/Berserk/Zombie may also drop out for Fastitocalon. Let me recheck: Death=1 chance = 7.5 - 44.25 + 99 = 62.25% -> 'Vulnerable' (not Highly vulnerable anymore). Poison=1 same, ~62%. Petrify=1 same. Silence=0 chance = 7.5 - 44.25 + 100 = 63.25% -> 'Vulnerable'. Berserk=0 same, 63%. Zombie=1 same, 62%. Darkness=6 chance = 7.5 - 44.25 + 94 = 57.25% -> silent (just below 60% threshold). So Fastitocalon key 0 v0.14.81 announce: 'Vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie.' (no Highly vulnerable tier; Darkness and Sleep both drop out). Bite Bug key 0 (Spirit=3): all byte 0-3 statuses chance = 100 (capped) -> 'Highly vulnerable'; Darkness byte=6 chance = 100.75 -> capped 100 -> 'Highly vulnerable'; Sleep byte=50 chance = 56.75% -> silent. So Bite Bug v0.14.81 announce: 'Highly vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie.' (no Vulnerable tier; Sleep drops out at 57% which is below 60%). Glacial Eye key 0 (Spirit=100): byte 0-1 statuses chance = 7.5 - 25 + 99-100 = 81.5-82.5% -> 'Vulnerable'; Darkness byte=6 chance = 76.5% -> 'Vulnerable'; Sleep byte=50 chance = 32.5% -> silent. So Glacial Eye v0.14.81 announce: 'Vulnerable to Death, Poison, Silence, Berserk, Zombie, and Darkness.' (Sleep drops out, no Highly vulnerable tier). FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusWeaknesses body + comment block replaced; new ComputeMagicCastChance helper added (~80 lines net), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.80 FormatStatusResistances unchanged. v0.14.79 popup-hook fix unchanged.  // v0.14.80: Status resistances tiered — 'Resists' (byte 100-199) + 'Immune to' (byte 200+) on key 9, mirroring v0.14.78 weakness announce structure (symmetry). Driven by Aaron's design discussion after v0.14.79 BAT: weaknesses use 'Highly vulnerable / Vulnerable' two-tier; resistances should match that structure for predictable mental model. Casting-focused use case settled the threshold choice: direct spell cast inflict % ≈ 100 - byte, so byte=100 is the hard boundary where direct casts stop landing (current 'Resists' tier handles that correctly), and byte=200 is the hard boundary where even max junction stacking fails (new 'Immune' tier handles that). Bytes 100-199 are 'Resists' (defeatable with junction); bytes 200+ are 'Immune' (cannot inflict at all). PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENTS per v0.14.79 BAT bytes: Bite Bug key 9 — 'Resists Slow, Stop, Doom, Slow Petrify, and Confuse.' (unchanged from v0.14.79; no 200+ bytes). Fastitocalon key 9 — 'Resists Slow, Stop, Doom, and Slow Petrify. Immune to Confuse.' (Confuse=255 splits into the new tier). Glacial Eye key 9 — 'Resists Slow, Stop, Doom, Slow Petrify, and Confuse.' (unchanged). FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusResistances body + comment block replaced (~50 lines net), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.78 FormatStatusWeaknesses logic unchanged. v0.14.79 popup-hook fix unchanged.  // v0.14.79: Repeat-scan detection bug fix — popup hook condition relaxed to match BOTH text_id 0x02 AND 0x06 with value=0x32. v0.14.78 BAT revealed v0.14.76 was based on a misread: my v0.14.76 changelog claimed "every Scan-cast popup arrives with text_id=0x02, NOT 0x06; text_id=0x06 never fires for any popup" — the v0.14.78 BAT log directly contradicts this, showing the Bite Bug Scan-cast popup is exactly text_id=0x06 value=0x32 at retaddr=0x00485938 ([POPUP] entry at 22:01:38). With v0.14.78's `tid == 0x02` condition, NO scan was caught by the popup hook in this BAT — zero `[NOEFFECT-WATCH] Scan detected` entries across the entire log. Only the FIRST scan in a battle got announced because PollBattleMagicId's 0->39 transition fallback caught it; subsequent scans in the same battle had magic_id stuck at 39 and no detection mechanism fired. The screen-close detection in screenshot.inl had the same bug, leaving s_scanScreenActiveSlot stuck after every Scan UI close (zero `OnScanPopupDespawn` entries in log). THIS BUILD: src/battle_tts_sprite.inl popup-hook condition changed from `tid == 0x02` to `(tid == 0x02 || tid == 0x06)`; src/battle_tts_screenshot.inl screen-close condition changed from `prev.text_id == 0x02` to `(prev.text_id == 0x02 || prev.text_id == 0x06)`. Defensive OR rather than just reverting to 0x06 because the v0.14.77 BAT (Grat + T-Rex) DID produce `[NOEFFECT-WATCH] Scan detected` entries, which means at least one of those casts had text_id=0x02 — different cast contexts (Magic-cast vs Draw-cast, full vs compacted view, different monsters, different battle states) may use different text_ids and we should be robust to all of them. value=0x32 (decimal 50, the spell-name display duration) plus the surrounding context (sub_48E830 fires within milliseconds in NoEffect_RecordSnapshot's window) provides enough specificity that false-positive Scan detection from non-Scan popups is unlikely. BAT plan: scan ANY monster, then scan a SECOND monster in the SAME battle (or scan the same monster twice if multi-target unavailable). Both should produce a `[NOEFFECT-WATCH] Scan detected` log line and proper `[SCAN-CACHE]` capture and proper auto-announce when each Scan UI window opens. Press key 0 in each Scan window; tiered weakness announce from v0.14.78 should fire correctly for both. After Scan UI close, number keys 1/2/3 should revert to default ally HP behavior (proves OnScanPopupDespawn fired). FILES: src/battle_tts_sprite.inl (~25 lines: condition + comment block), src/battle_tts_screenshot.inl (~15 lines: condition + comment block), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. v0.14.78 weakness-tiering logic in scan_tts.cpp is unchanged — it was correct in v0.14.78, but never got exercised on the second scan because that scan was never detected.  // v0.14.78: Status weaknesses tiered — byte <=5 'Highly vulnerable to ...', byte 6..50 'Vulnerable to ...', byte 51..99 silent, byte >=100 announced via FormatStatusResistances on key 9. v0.14.77 BAT confirmed BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = +0x80 is correct (Grat/T-Rex bytes match canon FF8 design including Sleep=50 'tutorial midpoint'), but the flat byte == 0 weakness threshold missed canonical FF8 vulnerabilities: T-Rex's wiki-documented Sleep / Darkness / Death weaknesses sit at bytes 50 / 2 / 1 respectively, not 0, so v0.14.77 announced only the byte==0 statuses (Petrify / Silence / Berserk for both Grat and T-Rex). Investigation of the FF8 Scan UI rendering chain in disassembly (sub_84F860 -> sub_84F8D0 -> sub_84FD90 -> sub_49F0A0) confirmed the vanilla Scan UI does NOT display status weakness/resistance at all — only stats and elemental affinities. The 20 status bytes exist solely for the inflict probability formula `% chance to inflict before junction = 100 - byte`; there is no game-side threshold to mirror because the game never surfaces this info. We're exposing tactical data vanilla FF8 hides, so the threshold is a deliberate design choice. THIS BUILD: src/scan_tts.cpp::FormatStatusWeaknesses split into two tiers. byte <= 5 -> 'Highly vulnerable' (near-100% inflict before junction — designer filler-low for guaranteed-land ailments). byte 6..50 -> 'Vulnerable' (junction-amplifiable; 100 spells stacked to ST-Atk-J brings StatusAttack to 200, beating StatusDefense 100+50=150 for byte=50 ailments by 50% per swing). Wording uses two sentences when both tiers populated: 'Highly vulnerable to A, B, and C. Vulnerable to D.' JoinNameList helper unchanged. PREDICTED ANNOUNCEMENTS per v0.14.77 BAT bytes: Grat key 0 — 'Highly vulnerable to Death, Poison, Petrify, Darkness, Silence, Berserk, and Zombie. Vulnerable to Sleep.' T-Rexaur key 0 — same shape (Petrify byte differs Grat=1 vs T-Rex=0 but both <=5). Key 9 unchanged from v0.14.77. FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp (FormatStatusWeaknesses body + comment block replaced; ~50 lines net), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. Older changelogs archived to DEVNOTES history. v0.14.74 shipped BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = 0x4C per the deep research's 'best-supported hypothesis' and v0.14.74 BAT immediately invalidated it (Grat showed alternating 169/251 byte pattern across the entire 20-byte run, contradicting the canonical Sleep-vulnerable Grat). v0.14.74.1 added the [SCAN-STRUCT] 121-byte hex dump to find the real offset; v0.14.74.x..v0.14.76 fought through three intervening cross-battle / popup-detection bugs before v0.14.76 unblocked clean two-enemy [SCAN-STRUCT] capture. v0.14.76 BAT delivered the diagnostic data: Grat (Lv15, monsterId 0x1F) and T-Rexaur (Lv21, monsterId 0x43) BOTH show +0x4C..+0x6B as identical garbage 'A9 FB A9 FB ...' (uninitialized memory or shared scratch buffer), but +0x80..+0x93 differs per-monster AND matches FF Wiki / Quistis tutorial canon for every validated status across both samples (Sleep=0x32 (50%) at idx 7 in BOTH — structural smoking gun matching Quistis 'put Grats to sleep' tutorial; Grat Death=0x01 + T-Rex Death=0x00 both highly vulnerable; Grat Berserk=0x00 vulnerable per canon; positive statuses idx 8-19 all show high values (immune) per canonical FF8 enemy resist profile). The deep-research-predicted disasm threshold `cmp al, 0x64` does NOT exist anywhere in `0x00801000.asm` — that model is wrong, but the +0x80 hypothesis is independent and BAT-validated. THIS BUILD: (1) `BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE` already updated to 0x80 in src/battle_tts.h with full BAT-validation comment block. (2) `src/scan_tts.cpp::SpeakField` case 9 re-enabled to call `FormatStatusResistances` (announces ailments with statusRes byte >= 100, the deep research's 'Strong vs' threshold). (3) `src/scan_tts.cpp::SpeakField` case 0 re-enabled to call `FormatStatusWeaknesses` (announces ailments with statusRes byte == 0, the conservative 'fully vulnerable' threshold from FF Wiki StatusDefense formula). Both formatters were already present from v0.14.74 and are unchanged — only the v0.14.74.1 stub-revert that wired them to 'Not implemented yet.' is reverted. (4) `[SCAN-STRUCT]` 121-byte diagnostic dump stripped from CaptureSnapshot (purpose served — actual offset found). DumpRow and LogStructDump helpers also removed since they're now unreferenced. The `[SCAN-CACHE]`, `[SCAN-ELEM]`, and `[SCAN-STAT]` log lines remain in CaptureSnapshot — those are useful for ongoing validation of element + status reads against canon. BAT plan: (a) scan Grat — key 9 should announce 'Resists' with 12 entries (the positive statuses that show high values; we filter to offensive ailments, so this announce should be much shorter — likely 'No status resistances' since Grat's offensive-ailment indices [Death/Poison/Petrify/Darkness/Silence/Berserk/Zombie/Sleep/Slow/Stop/Doom/SlowPetrify/Confuse] are all < 100 in the +0x80 sample). Key 0 should announce 'Weak to Berserk' (Grat's idx 5 = 0x00 at +0x85) and possibly other ailments with byte==0; the [SCAN-STAT] log will reveal exactly which. (b) scan T-Rexaur — key 0 should announce something close to 'Weak to Death' (idx 0 = 0x00 at +0x80). Other T-Rex bytes from the v0.14.76 BAT: +0x80..+0x87 = '00 01 00 02 00 03 01 32' — Death idx 0 = 0, Petrify idx 2 = 0, Silence idx 4 = 0; idx 1 = 1 (Poison), idx 3 = 2 (Darkness), idx 5 = 3 (Berserk) all small but non-zero so they won't trigger the byte==0 weakness announce — calibration data, may want to relax to byte < N for some N in v0.14.78 if the announce is too sparse. FILES: src/scan_tts.cpp (~30 lines: 2 case bodies replaced + LogStructDump call removed + DumpRow + LogStructDump function bodies removed; ~120 lines net deletion), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new addresses, NO schema changes, NO new hooks. The +0x80 confirmation gives us a BAT-validated foundation to build on — if BAT shows announcement weirdness, the [SCAN-STAT] log lines tell us exactly which bytes need different thresholds, and we adjust formula constants in v0.14.78+ without touching the offset. v0.14.55's popup-hook condition `tid == 0x06 && value == 0x32` in HookedPopupSprite (battle_tts_sprite.inl) AND the matching Scan-window-close condition `prev.text_id == 0x06 && prev.value == 50` in PollPopupRecords (battle_tts_screenshot.inl) both used the WRONG text_id constant. Empirical evidence from v0.14.75 BAT log (Grat scan + T-Rexaur scan attempt): every Scan-cast popup arrives with text_id=0x02, NOT 0x06. text_id=0x06 never fires for any popup type — zero matches across the full 4796-line log. The bug was completely silent because PollBattleMagicId's transition-based fallback in battle_tts_ewm.inl caught FIRST scans in a battle (battle_magic_id transitions 0→39) and that produced apparently-working Grat scans with proper [SCAN-CACHE]/[SCAN-STRUCT] dumps. But REPEAT scans in the same battle (battle_magic_id stays at 39 from the first cast onward) had no transition for the polling fallback to detect, AND the popup-hook primary path was dead, so the action-layer ScanTTS detection never fired. Result: the second+ Scan in any battle produced no Scan announcement, no [SCAN-CACHE] capture, and a spurious 'No effect on <target>' from the watchdog 10 seconds later. Aaron experienced this as 'T-Rexaur scan triggered in compacted view despite the config setting being changed' — a misdiagnosis: it was NOT compacted view (the log shows sub_B687C0 fire #1 firing for the T-Rexaur scan, which compacted view skips entirely), it was the broken popup-hook detection. The Config 'Scan: Always' setting is working correctly; this is a separate code bug introduced in v0.14.55 and undetected for ~21 builds because the magic_id polling fallback masked it for first-of-battle scans (the only kind we'd been informally testing). FIX: change `0x06` → `0x02` in both locations (one each in sprite.inl and screenshot.inl). With the correct text_id, the popup hook reliably catches every Scan cast (first or repeat) via its existing s_lastScanCastTick mechanism; NoEffect_RecordSnapshot consumes the tick within the same frame and calls ScanTTS::OnScanCast directly with the correct slot. PollBattleMagicId's Scan branch becomes a redundant fallback that may produce duplicate [SCAN-CACHE]/[SCAN-STRUCT] log lines on first-of-battle scans; harmless cosmetic noise that we can clean up later if it bothers anyone (OnScanCast's per-slot 30 s lock from v0.14.57 prevents duplicate user-facing announcements). Side benefit: the matching screen-close detection fix (`prev.text_id == 0x02 && prev.value == 50` in screenshot.inl::PollPopupRecords) means OnScanPopupDespawn now actually fires when the Scan UI closes, properly clearing s_scanScreenActiveSlot so number keys 1..0 revert to default ally-HP behavior. That was a latent bug never reported because Aaron hadn't yet pressed number keys after closing the Scan UI in any past BAT. THIS FIX UNBLOCKS THE PRIMARY NEXT-SESSION PRIORITY (BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE three-enemy diagnostic per NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT). With repeat scans now working, Aaron can scan Grat → T-Rexaur → Tonberry in any sequence and we'll get clean [SCAN-STRUCT] dumps for all three to diff-find the correct status resist offset. FILES: src/battle_tts_sprite.inl (~5 lines: condition flipped + comment block extended explaining the v0.14.55 transcription error), src/battle_tts_screenshot.inl (~5 lines: condition flipped + comment block extended), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO new behavior beyond the fix; no version bump in scan_tts.cpp; no schema changes; no new addresses. BAT plan: enter Balamb Garden Training Center, force a multi-Grat encounter or a Grat→T-Rexaur sequence, cast Scan twice (or scan two different enemies in the same battle), confirm both produce proper [SCAN-CACHE]+[SCAN-STRUCT] log output AND a proper auto-announce when each Scan UI window opens AND no spurious [SPELL-NOEFFECT] / [NOEFFECT-FLUSH] entries. After BAT pass, this directly unblocks v0.14.74.1's [SCAN-STRUCT] diagnostic which now has reliable repeat-scan capture, so the BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE three-enemy diff can proceed in v0.14.77+. The v0.14.74.1 stub-revert of keys 9/0 (FormatStatusResistances/FormatStatusWeaknesses unwired, 'Not implemented yet.') is unchanged in this build and ships as-is."

// v0.14.75: Permanent screenshot trigger relocated from F12 to F11 + dead F11 diagnostics removed + AnnounceMenuSummary moved to M.

// v0.14.74.4-diag: F12 = on-demand screenshot capture (diagnostic build). Aaron needs to verify whether FF8's in-game Config menu has a 'Scan compacted-view' toggle before we invest in compacted-view fallback code for the Scan UI auto-announce architecture. The mod can already capture FF8's OpenGL framebuffer (via the SwapBuffers hook + glReadPixels installed in BattleTTS::Initialize), but only at hard-coded trigger points (kind4 sprite spawns, scan UI fire #1, etc.). Standard Windows screenshot tools (PrintScreen, snipping tool, GDI BitBlt) return solid black for FF8 because the OpenGL framebuffer isn't in the Windows compositor pipeline, so Aaron can't capture the Config menu manually. ARCHITECTURE: in src/dinput8.cpp's AccessibilityThread main hotkey block — alongside the existing F1/F2/F3-F8/V handlers — added an F12 edge-detector. On press, builds an absolute path 'Logs/screenshots/f12_<HHMMSS>_<MS>' (no extension) under BattleTTS::GetScreenshotDir(), calls BattleTTS::RequestScreenshotAsync(path) with default 0 frame delay, logs '[F12-SCREENSHOT] Capture requested: <path>.png' to ff8_mod.log, and speaks 'Screenshot captured.' via ScreenReader::Speak so Aaron knows the keypress registered. RequestScreenshotAsync sets a flag on the global SwapBuffers hook; the next swap (any game state — menus, field, battle, all share the same hook) writes <path>.bmp + <path>.png to disk. F12 RULE COMPLIANCE per userMemories: searched all source files for VK_F12, F12, and 0x7B keycode references before adding the new handler. Found one orphaned ScanTTS::PollDiagnosticKey forward decl in src/battle_tts.cpp's ScanTTS namespace block (left over from v0.14.66-diag's scan-type-label dump that was already removed in some earlier cleanup but never fully cleaned) — REMOVED. The 'F12 reserved' comment in src/field_nav_handlekeys.inl is just a comment, no code, left as-is. The 'F12 is handled by FieldNavigation::HandleKeys()' comment in dinput8.cpp updated to reflect the new ownership. Confirmed scan_tts.cpp / scan_tts.h / battle_tts_hp.inl have no PollDiagnosticKey or F12 keycode reference. F12 has exactly one consumer now — the screenshot trigger in dinput8.cpp. CONTEXT: blocked on the v0.14.74.3 BAT result — Aaron tested twice. Test 1 (success, world map outside Garden): Bite Bug -> escape -> Fastitocalon. v0.14.74.3 fingerprint fix worked perfectly, Fastitocalon properly identified. Test 2 (compacted-view issue, Balamb Garden Training Center): Grat -> escape -> T-Rexaur. T-Rexaur correctly identified (fingerprint fix worked!) but Scan only spoke the description without prompting for number keys and without waiting for confirm — auto-advanced. HYPOTHESIS: T-Rexaur Scan rendered in FF8's compacted view (shown when scanning a monster ID that's already been scanned in the current save/session). Action-layer (sub_48D200 popup hook) fires for both compacted and full views, captures snapshot to s_scanCache[slot]. But sub_B687C0 only fires for the FULL Scan UI render — compacted view skips it. The auto-announce trigger (s_scanScreenActiveSlot activation) is gated on sub_B687C0 first-fire (v0.14.60 architecture). So in compacted view: data captured silently, no auto-announce, number keys don't activate. v0.14.54 BAT proved both sub_B687C0 and sub_84F860 skip compacted view; v0.14.55 moved to action-layer, then v0.14.59+ UX redesign re-coupled the user-facing announce to sub_B687C0. NEXT STEP after this BAT: if Config menu has no Scan toggle (highly likely — vanilla FF8 PC config menu is just Cursor / ATB / Battle Speed / Battle Message Speed / Sound / Music / Magic Order / Window Color), v0.14.74.5+ adds compacted-view fallback in ScanTTS::OnScanCast(_, true) — ~800 ms timeout-based fallback: if sub_B687C0 doesn't fire within the window, treat as compacted view, speak the auto-announce now, set s_scanScreenActiveSlot for ~15-20 s so number keys work without the despawn signal. FILES: src/dinput8.cpp (~30 lines added in main hotkey block), src/battle_tts.cpp (~5 lines removed: orphaned PollDiagnosticKey forward decl + comment), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). NO behavior change for normal Aaron play except the new F12 hotkey.

// v0.14.74.3: Cross-battle stale ENEMY NAME bug fix — second stale-data vector exposed by v0.14.74.2 BAT. ROOT CAUSE: in src/battle_tts.cpp::AnnounceBattleStart, the readiness gate is just 'allyMaxHP > 0 AND BATTLE_INIT_MIN_DELAY_MS elapsed'. When the player escapes battle 1 and immediately enters battle 2 with a different formation, the engine does NOT zero enemy slot memory on exit and does NOT immediately repopulate it on the next entry — it leaves the previous battle's enemy data (HP=587/MaxHP=587/Lv=21 status=0x00 for Grats in the BAT log) in place for ~8-10 seconds before writing the new formation. Squall's allyMaxHP=901 survives the transition, so the existing readiness gate fires immediately at the 2-second min delay; CountActiveEnemies() returns true (stale Grat HP > 0); BuildEnemyNameString reads stale memory and announces the previous battle's names. Worse, s_enemyAnnounceDone is set true, disabling the second-pass safety net that would have caught the late population. Aaron's BAT 19:47:48: entered T-Rexaur battle, mod announced 'Battle! 2 Grats.' against the audible T-Rexaur roar; engine wrote real T-Rexaur HP at 19:47:58 (+10 s) but cache was already locked. FIX: snapshot enemy slot fingerprint (HP/MaxHP/Lv/status for slots 3-6) at OnBattleExit; in AnnounceBattleStart and the Update() second-pass loop, if enemyCount > 0 but the live fingerprint matches the snapshot bit-for-bit AND we're inside BATTLE_INIT_TIMEOUT_MS, treat enemies as 'not ready' and defer to second-pass. New helper EnemySlotsMatchLastBattleSnap() does the comparison under SEH guard. New struct EnemySlotSnap; new state s_lastBattleEnemySnap[BATTLE_TOTAL_SLOTS - BATTLE_ALLY_SLOTS] + s_lastBattleEnemySnapValid. BATTLE_INIT_TIMEOUT_MS bumped from 10000 to 15000 ms because the engine took ~10 s to repopulate T-Rexaur data in the BAT (right at the old timeout boundary). FIRST-BATTLE-OF-SESSION SAFE: snapValid=false makes the helper return false, preserving existing behavior. VICTORY EXIT SAFE: ending HP=0/status=0x01 differs from a fresh battle's HP>0/status=0x00 — fingerprints differ — no false delay. ESCAPE EXIT (THE BUG CASE): slot data persists in memory, fresh battle reuses same memory, fingerprints match, defer to second-pass which retries every frame until engine writes new data. SAME-FORMATION RE-ENCOUNTER: fingerprints match for the full 15-second timeout, then we fall through and announce — worst case is a 15 s announce delay on legitimate same-formation re-encounters, an acceptable cost for not lying about what enemies are present. NEW DIAGNOSTIC LOGS: [EXIT-SNAP] in OnBattleExit dumps the captured fingerprint per battle exit; [STALE-ENEMY] in AnnounceBattleStart fires whenever the stale-fingerprint path defers the first-pass announce. FILES: src/battle_tts.cpp (~5 edits totaling ~140 lines, all comment-heavy), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The v0.14.74.2 magicId fix is preserved exactly; this build runs alongside it. Test plan: cast Scan on enemies in battle 1, escape, walk into battle 2 with a DIFFERENT formation. Expected: 'Battle!' generic announce at the 2-second mark, then the correct enemy name announced ~8-10 s later via second-pass when engine populates fresh data. Pre-fix observed (v0.14.74.2 BAT): announces previous battle's enemy names in new battle. NoEffect-on-Scan suppression failure (Aaron heard 'No effect on Grat 1' for a T-Rexaur scan) is a downstream symptom of this same root cause and should also be fixed by this build, since the slot-3 entity wasn't actually a Grat — the action-layer Scan detection was operating on whatever slot data the engine had at the time of the cast (real T-Rexaur), but the cached enemy name was Grat from stale memory. Once enemy data is properly fresh by the time Scan fires, the detection path's name resolution will be correct and the watchdog cancel can fire on the right (slot, name) pair. v0.14.74.1 [SCAN-STRUCT] diagnostic still pending BAT — unchanged in this build.

// v0.14.74.2: Critical correctness fix for stale Scan / GF announces leaking across battle escape. PRIMARY FIX in src/battle_tts.cpp::OnBattleEnter: replaced 's_prevBattleMagicId = -1' with a SEH-guarded read of *s_battleMagicIdAddr to cache the engine's current battle_magic_id value at battle entry. ROOT CAUSE: Setting prev to -1 made every battle's first PollBattleMagicId call see the residual value left over from the previous battle (39 = Scan after a Scan cast, 200 = Ifrit after a summon, etc.) as a fresh -1 -> stale_value transition, which fired ScanTTS::OnScanCast or GfAudioDesc::OnGFAnimationStart against whatever target bitmask was also still in memory at 0x01D76884. Most visible failure mode: escape after casting Scan, enter next battle, hear a stale Scan announce against whatever slot the previous battle's bitmask resolved to (or a no-op SCAN-TTS log line if the bitmask resolved to invalid). Same class of bug applied to GF effect IDs: escape after summoning Ifrit, enter next battle, hear Ifrit's audio description replayed at battle entry. The fix caches the live value so PollBattleMagicId sees prev == cur and detects no transition; once the engine eventually clears battle_magic_id to a fresh value (typically 0), that transition is non-actionable (neither GF effect ID nor 39). v0.14.74.2 BAT result: PRIMARY FIX VERIFIED — [SCAN-TTS] Battle entry: cached current magicId=39 as prev fired correctly on the post-escape battle 2 entry, suppressing the stale Scan that would otherwise have fired. But BAT exposed a SECOND stale-data vector (enemy name cache reading stale slot memory) addressed in v0.14.74.3 above. DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH FIX in src/battle_tts_sprite.inl::ResetSpriteSpawnState: added InterlockedExchange clearing s_lastScanCastTick to 0 per battle. Without this, escaping within SCAN_CAST_RECENT_MS (1 sec) of casting Scan could leak the popup-hook tick across the battle boundary and trigger a bogus action-layer Scan announce on the very first sub_48E830 fire of the next battle (NoEffect_RecordSnapshot's InterlockedExchange normally consumes the tick within the same frame the popup hook sets it, but the fix is one line and the race window is real). FILES: src/battle_tts.cpp (~30 lines added at the s_prevBattleMagicId reset site, comment-heavy because the bug is subtle), src/battle_tts_sprite.inl (1 line of code + ~10 lines of comment in ResetSpriteSpawnState), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

// v0.14.74.1: Diagnostic build for the broken keys 9/0 from v0.14.74. Two changes scoped to src/scan_tts.cpp. (1) NEW [SCAN-STRUCT] diagnostic log in CaptureSnapshot dumps 121 bytes of the entity struct from +0x3C through +0xB4 every scan, split across 8 log lines for greppability. Range covers the elemental block anchor (+0x3C..+0x4B, 16 bytes — should match [SCAN-ELEM] u16 values, validates dump alignment), the unknown gap (+0x4C..+0xB3, 104 bytes — contains the 20-byte status resistance block somewhere), and the level anchor (+0xB4, 1 byte — should equal snap.level, confirms alignment held all the way down). New helpers DumpRow (formats one 16-byte hex row) and LogStructDump (calls DumpRow 8 times under SEH guard). v0.14.74 BAT decisively proved BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = 0x4C is wrong: Grat scanned with alternating 169/251 byte pattern across all 20 indices, contradicting canon (Grat is the in-game source of Sleep magic but the v0.14.74 announcement said Grat 'Resists Sleep'). The deep research itself flagged that offset as a 'best-supported hypothesis' and explicitly recommended runtime validation; this dump provides the validation. Plan A diagnostic-driven path chosen over Plan B disassembly hunt because Plan B's specific formulation (cmp al, 0x64 in sub_84F860 phase handlers) didn't pan out — those phase handlers don't read entity offsets in the suspect range at all; the threshold check lives in the status APPLICATION path, not the scan UI rendering path, so finding it via disasm would require a much wider hunt. (2) Keys 9 / 0 in SpeakField switch reverted to 'Not implemented yet.' so misleading status info doesn't ship to Aaron during play — FormatStatusResistances and FormatStatusWeaknesses still exist and are unchanged but no longer wired. Keys 1-8 stay exactly as v0.14.74 (offensive/defensive stats, elemental resistances + weaknesses all working). Test plan for next BAT: scan three enemies with known-different status profiles and diff the [SCAN-STRUCT] sections in ff8_battle.log. Grat (vulnerable to Sleep/Silence/Berserk per Quistis Training Center tutorial canon) should show 0x00 at the indices for those statuses. T-Rexaur (vulnerable to Sleep/Darkness/Death/Poison per FF8 wiki) should show 0x00 at those indices and high values at others. Tonberry (no documented status weaknesses) should show high values across the entire 20-byte block. The 20-byte run where these patterns line up consistently is the actual BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE; v0.14.74.2 will update battle_tts.h with the corrected offset and re-enable keys 9/0. Files: src/scan_tts.cpp (DumpRow + LogStructDump helpers, LogStructDump call in CaptureSnapshot, SpeakField cases 9/0 reverted to 'Not implemented yet.' stubs), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). All other v0.14.74 mechanisms (split stats, combined elemental resistances on key 7, FormatWeak on key 8) preserved exactly.

// v0.14.74: Scan UI keyboard layout overhaul. Splits the 8-stat block into offensive (key 5: STR/MAG/SPD/HIT) and defensive (key 6: VIT/SPR/EVA/LCK) readouts — hearing all 8 numbers in one announcement was overwhelming. Consolidates v0.14.73.1's separate Absorbs (key 7) and Nullifies (key 8) into a single combined Resistances readout on key 7 that announces Halves / Strongly resists / Nullifies / Absorbs as four sentence-separated buckets (e.g. 'Halves Fire and Earth. Strongly resists Ice. Nullifies Thunder. Absorbs Water and Holy.'). The 901..999 'Strongly resists' range — left ambiguous and silent in v0.14.73.1 — is now folded into key 7 alongside the other resistance categories. Elemental Weaknesses (FormatWeak) moves from key 6 to key 8 (logic unchanged, threshold still v < 800). NEW key 9: Status Resistances — reads 20 u8 bytes from entity+0x4C (BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE) and announces ailments where statusRes byte >= 100 (the deep research's 'Strong vs' threshold). NEW key 0: Status Weaknesses — announces ailments where statusRes byte == 0 (the conservative 'fully vulnerable' threshold inferred from the FF Wiki StatusDefense formula; T-Rexaur's BAT is the canonical anchor for calibration). Both keys 9 and 0 filter to 13 offensive ailments only (Death/Poison/Petrify/Darkness/Silence/Berserk/Zombie/Sleep/Slow/Stop/Doom/Slow Petrify/Confuse) — buffs (Haste/Regen/Reflect/Float) and non-offensive effects (Drain/Expulsion/???) excluded since they're not actionable for the player. The active-statuses readout that used to live on key 0 in v0.14.73 is removed because the same info is announced via the target-cursor flow in BuildStatusString already — net win for the layout (10 useful keys instead of 9 useful + 1 redundant). NEW HEADER CONSTANT: BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE = 0x4C in src/battle_tts.h with full encoding documentation. NEW HELPER: ReadSlotStatusRes() in src/scan_tts.cpp mirrors ReadSlotElements (SEH-guarded memcpy of 20 bytes from entity_base + BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE). NEW LOG LINE: [SCAN-STAT] in CaptureSnapshot dumps all 20 raw status bytes per scan in deep-research order (Death/Poison/Petrify/Darkness/Silence/Berserk/Zombie/Sleep/Haste/Slow/Stop/Regen/Reflect/Doom/SlowPetrify/Float/Confuse/Drain/Expulsion/Unknown) so the byte == 0 weakness threshold can be data-driven if T-Rexaur's BAT shows different values. NEW FORMATTERS: FormatOffensiveStats + FormatDefensiveStats (replace FormatStats), FormatResistances (replaces FormatAbsorb + FormatNullify; folds in Halves and Strongly resists), FormatStatusResistances (key 9), FormatStatusWeaknesses (key 0). REFACTORED HELPER: JoinElementList -> JoinNameList parameterized over a name table (now used by both element formatters via ELEMENT_NAMES and status formatters via STATUS_NAMES_20). NEW DATA: STATUS_NAMES_20 (all 20 status names in deep-research order, used by [SCAN-STAT] log + status formatters), OFFENSIVE_AILMENT_INDICES (13-element filter table), OFFENSIVE_AILMENT_COUNT. SpeakField switch updated for new bindings. BuildAutoAnnounce comment block updated to reference the v0.14.74 key map (spoken phrase unchanged — still 'Press numbers 0 through 9 for details.'). Files: src/battle_tts.h (BENT_STATUS_RESIST_BASE constant), src/scan_tts.cpp (~280 lines: ReadSlotStatusRes, [SCAN-STAT] log, FormatOffensiveStats/FormatDefensiveStats, JoinNameList refactor, FormatResistances, FormatStatusResistances, FormatStatusWeaknesses, STATUS_NAMES_20, OFFENSIVE_AILMENT_INDICES, SpeakField switch, comment blocks), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). Expected v0.14.74 BAT outcomes: (1) Fastitocalon should announce on key 7: 'Halves Fire. Absorbs Water.' (820 in halves bucket, 1000 in absorb bucket); on key 8: 'Weak against Thunder and Earth.' (700 and 650). (2) T-Rexaur should announce on key 0: something like 'Weak to Death, Poison, Darkness, and Sleep.' if standard FF8 data has those bytes at 0 — this confirms or calibrates the threshold; if T-Rexaur's Sleep byte is non-zero but small (50, 30, etc.), the [SCAN-STAT] log reveals it and we adjust to byte < N. (3) Tonberry (no documented status weaknesses) should announce 'No status weaknesses.' as a null-test. (4) The 8-stat readout no longer happens on key 5 — instead key 5 says 'Strength X. Magic X. Speed X. Hit X.' and key 6 says 'Vitality X. Spirit X. Evasion X. Luck X.' — each ~half the cognitive load. (5) Active-statuses readout removed from key 0 — if Aaron misses it later, status info still arrives via target-cursor announcements during normal battle navigation."

// v0.14.72: Resolve sub_47EC70 hook conflict between SCAN-TTS and victory TTS by removing the standalone scan_tts.cpp hook installer (v0.14.68-diag through v0.14.71) and routing through the victory module's existing canonical hook (HookedBtCandidate1 in battle_tts_victory.inl, installed since v0.13.14 via InstallBattleTextHooks). v0.14.71 BAT log decisively exposed the conflict at module init: '[BT-HOOK] sub_47EC70 @ 0x0047EC70: E9 EB F4 A0 71 8B 04 45' (the E9 JMP rel32 opcode confirmed scan_tts.cpp's hook installed first) followed by '[BT-HOOK] sub_47EC70 MH_CreateHook FAILED: 3' (MH_ERROR_ALREADY_CREATED for the victory installer). MinHook silently lost the race for whichever installer ran second, so victory phase detection (EXP/Items/GF/Ability announcements via text_id 22/23/21/28/109/121/127 routing in HookedBtCandidate1) has been broken since v0.14.68-diag. Aaron confirmed in this session that he had noticed victory TTS not announcing post-Scan but hadn't reported it because we were focused on Scan itself. ARCHITECTURE FIX: scan_tts.cpp's HookedGetBattleText converted to public ScanTTS::HandleBattleText(int textId, const char* result); the BATTLE_GET_TEXT_ADDR / GetBattleTextFn typedef / s_originalGetBattleText / s_getBattleTextHookInstalled / InstallGetBattleTextHook all removed; the InstallGetBattleTextHook call in Initialize replaced with a no-op comment explaining the new ownership. battle_tts_victory.inl's HookedBtCandidate1 gains a one-line forward call '::ScanTTS::HandleBattleText((int)a1, (const char*)result)' immediately after s_origBt1 returns, before the victory-mode-only logic block (mode==4/5/100). HandleBattleText is internally gated on GetScanFlightSlot() so it's a near-no-op outside an active scan event — critical because the victory hook fires on every sub_47EC70 invocation (~hundreds per battle frame). All v0.14.71 SCAN-TTS behavior preserved: Fly-types still capture 'Fly Monster' on key 3, type-less monsters still correctly fall back to plain 'Level N.', wider gate via GetScanFlightSlot still active, per-scan reset in OnScanCast still defensive. Forward declaration of HandleBattleText added to scan_tts.h public API and to the existing ScanTTS namespace forward-decl block in battle_tts.cpp (matches the existing v0.14.66-diag pattern for PollDiagnosticKey). Files: src/scan_tts.h (public decl), src/scan_tts.cpp (~30 lines removed: hook machinery deleted, Initialize updated), src/battle_tts.cpp (1 forward decl added to ScanTTS namespace block), src/battle_tts_victory.inl (1 forward call added in HookedBtCandidate1), src/ff8_accessibility.h (this version). v0.14.72 BAT result: PASS. First 8 bytes of sub_47EC70 now read '8B 44 24 04 66 8B 04 45' (the original prologue, NOT E9 ...); '[BT-HOOK] sub_47EC70 hooked OK'; '[BT-HOOK] 8/8 hooks installed' (was 7/8 since v0.14.68-diag). Scan TTS still works perfectly; victory TTS phase announcements function again post-Scan for the first time in 4 sessions.

// v0.14.69: PRODUCTION build wiring the Scan UI's monster-type label into the Level announcement (key 3). v0.14.68-diag's hook on sub_47EC70 (get_battle_text) BAT decisively cracked the type-label architecture: the on-screen 'Fly Monster' / 'Earth Monster' / etc. label at the bottom-left is rendered by TWO consecutive sub_47EC70 calls during a Scan UI session — first the type prefix (text_id varies per monster type, e.g. text_id=99 returns 'Fly' for Glacial Eye), then text_id=36 which is the universal 'Monster' suffix. Decoded via FF8 text encoding (uppercase encoded = decoded + 4, lowercase encoded = decoded - 2): text_id=99 hex=4A 6A 77 00 → 'Fly', text_id=36 hex=51 6D 6C 71 72 63 70 00 → 'Monster'. Visually confirmed in scan_001352_348_slot3_Glacial_Eye.png — bottom-left renders 'Fly Monster' exactly. v0.14.69 BAT result: Bite Bug worked perfectly ('Level 8, Fly Monster.'); Fastitocalon failed to capture (Level key 3 said just 'Level 8.'); Caterchipillar wasn't actually scanned (Aaron encountered the battle but didn't cast Scan).  // v0.14.66-diag: F12-gated diagnostic to hunt down the memory location of the 'Fly Monster' / 'Earth Monster' / etc. monster-type label rendered at the bottom-left of the Scan UI. The Bite Bug screenshot from v0.14.65.3 BAT exposed this previously-unseen UI element — we don't yet announce it. FFNx canary's ff8.h documents only scan_text_positions / scan_text_data for the Scan UI (no separate type table named), and the strings 'Fly Monster' / 'Fly' / 'Monster' are NOT in FF8_EN.exe as plain ASCII (FF8_EN_strings.txt confirms), so the type text lives in some encoded data table we haven't located. APPROACH: when Aaron presses F12 while a Scan window is active, ScanTTS::PollDiagnosticKey() (new) dumps to ff8_battle.log under the [SCAN-TYPE-DIAG] tag: (1) the full 0xD0-byte entity struct for the active scan slot — the type might be a single byte stored among the unknown-meaning offsets, (2) 0x80 bytes BEFORE scan_text_positions (0x01887474) looking for a parallel positions table, (3) the full scan_text_positions table (0x140 bytes for ~160 monster_id entries) for cross-reference, (4) the first 0x100 bytes of scan_text_data (0x018875B4), (5) 16-bit reads at id*2 from 9 candidate base addresses around scan_text_positions ± 0x200, (6) 8-bit reads at id from 6 candidate base addresses around scan_text_data, all SEH-guarded and ASCII-gutter formatted. Aaron presses F12 for several enemies of varied types (Bite Bug = Fly Monster, plus Geezard or Caterchipillar of a different type) and we diff the dumps to find which bytes encode the type. Wired in via a new public ScanTTS::PollDiagnosticKey() (forward decl in scan_tts.h, impl at end of scan_tts.cpp before the namespace closer) called from PollHPCheckKeys() in battle_tts_hp.inl on every battle-frame poll — internally edge-detects F12 (s_diagF12WasDown) and gates on IsScreenActive() so it's a cheap no-op outside its narrow active window. F12 was already verified free across all source files (no existing VK_F12 / 0x7B handlers). DELIVERABLE: this build produces no behavior change for Aaron's normal play — just adds the F12 hotkey for the diagnostic dump. Once Aaron BATs with two or three enemies of varied types and uploads ff8_battle.log, we'll diff the [SCAN-TYPE-DIAG] sections and identify the type field. Files: scan_tts.h (forward decl), scan_tts.cpp (impl + DumpHexWindow helper, ~190 lines added at end of file), battle_tts.cpp (forward decl in ScanTTS namespace block), battle_tts_hp.inl (one-line call site at top of PollHPCheckKeys), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The v0.14.65.3 frame-delay screenshot mechanism is unchanged — still in production for the auto-capture during scan UI render. v0.14.65.2 BAT confirmed the path fix worked perfectly (PNG landed at 'Logs\\screenshots\\scan_234209_596_slot3_Fastitocalon.png' which Claude can read directly without manual copying), but the captured image showed the same too-early render as v0.14.65.1: stat labels visible (HP / STR DEF / INT SPI / DEX EVA) with NO numeric values rendered, plus typewriter description showing only 'Fast' and 'A fi' partial chars. Stats announce was perfect ('Strength 20. Vitality 132. Magic 56. Spirit 180. Speed 5. Luck 0. Evasion 6. Hit 0.' for Lv14 Fastitocalon). The capture was happening on the very next swap after fire #1, before FF8's typewriter could render values. FIX: extend the existing async screenshot machinery with a frame-delay counter. New static volatile int s_captureFrameDelay alongside s_captureRequested in battle_tts_screenshot.inl. HookedSwapBuffers decrements the counter each frame and only calls DoGLCapture() when it reaches 0. RequestScreenshotAsync now takes an optional frameDelay parameter (default 0 preserves v0.14.65 behavior; existing callers including the synchronous CaptureScreenshot path are unaffected). scan_tts.cpp passes 90 (≈1.5 s at 60 fps) which gives the engine ample time to draw the full description (~95 chars) plus all 6 numeric stat values. Description renders ~1.5 chars per frame = ~63 frames; stat values typically render in another ~10-15 frames, so 90 frames has comfortable margin. User won't notice the visual delay since Aaron is blind — only the diagnostic capture is being deferred. The scan UI stays open for ~6 s before any keypress (BAT log shows window opened at 23:42:09 and key 5 pressed at 23:42:15) so the delayed capture can't accidentally catch a different UI state. CaptureScreenshot (synchronous) explicitly resets s_captureFrameDelay to 0 so its 160 ms Sleep-and-poll contract is preserved — only the async path uses the delay. Files: battle_tts_screenshot.inl (s_captureFrameDelay declaration + HookedSwapBuffers counter check + CaptureScreenshot reset), battle_tts.h (RequestScreenshotAsync gets default frameDelay=0 parameter), battle_tts.cpp (RequestScreenshotAsync impl reads frameDelay and clamps negatives to 0), scan_tts.cpp (call site passes 90 + log message updated to mention the delay), ff8_accessibility.h (this version).

// v0.14.65.2: Fix the screenshot path so scan_*.png captures land in the project's Logs\\screenshots directory alongside kind4_*, poll_NEW_*, popup_time_* etc., instead of FF8.exe's CWD (Steam install dir's Screenshots folder, where Claude has no read access). BAT result: PASS. Path now lands at 'C:\\Users\\ampag\\OneDrive\\Documents\\FFVIII-Accessibility-Mod\\FF8_OriginalPC_mod\\Logs\\screenshots\\scan_234209_596_slot3_Fastitocalon.{bmp,png}'. Image content matches v0.14.65.1 (same too-early render pattern — labels visible, no values yet, typewriter partial), confirming the path fix didn't change capture timing. Same too-early issue addressed in v0.14.65.3 with the frame-delay counter. FIX: added BattleTTS::GetScreenshotDir() public accessor that returns KIND4_SCREENSHOT_DIR (file-static const char* in battle_tts_sprite.inl, only visible inside battle_tts.cpp's translation unit). scan_tts.cpp HookedScanGetText now composes the absolute path from this accessor instead of using a relative 'Screenshots\\' prefix.

// v0.14.65.1: Fix the auto-screenshot trigger that didn't fire in v0.14.65 BAT. ROOT CAUSE: I assumed sub_B687C0 fires per-frame while the scan UI is open. v0.14.65 BAT log proved otherwise — between fire #1 (window-open) and the user closing the scan window 38 seconds later, sub_B687C0 fired exactly ONCE. So my count==30 trigger was unreachable. FIX: move the screenshot trigger from the count==30 branch to inside the count==1 branch, right after OnScanPopupSpawn(). v0.14.65 stats wiring (Part A) is unchanged — BAT confirmed perfect: announced 'Strength 16. Vitality 129. Magic 50. Spirit 180. Speed 5. Luck 0. Evasion 6. Hit 0.' for Fastitocalon Lv10, matching [SCAN-CACHE] log values exactly.

// v0.14.65: Two complementary changes — Stats key 5 wired (BAT'd PASS) + auto-screenshot capture (BAT'd FAIL — trigger never fired, fixed in v0.14.65.1).

// v0.14.64: Strip SC1-PROBE and SC2-PROBE diagnostics. v0.14.63 BAT confirmed everything works correctly: 'Cast Fire'-type spell-cast banners announce again, scan announce works cleanly with no duplication, and the IsScreenActive()-based gate correctly suppresses only the rendered scan-window text. The probes were added in v0.14.60 (SC2-PROBE) and v0.14.61 (SC1-PROBE) to identify the source of the duplicate scan announcement; that work is complete. Probes were just adding log noise on every speech call, so this build removes them along with the now-unused <intrin.h> include for _ReturnAddress(). Single-channel SAPI mode from v0.14.61 STAYS in place (s_pVoice2 = nullptr in InitSAPI) — Aaron prefers it and it eliminates the simultaneous-overlap class of bugs entirely. The v0.14.60 sub_B687C0 first-fire announce trigger STAYS — it's the correct architecture. The v0.14.62/63 Hook_show_dialog gate (suppress only when ScanTTS::IsScreenActive() during battle) STAYS — it correctly suppresses scan duplicates while letting all other battle UI text through. Files: screen_reader.cpp (3 deletions — SC1-PROBE block in Speak, SC2-PROBE block in SpeakChannel2, intrin.h include), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The Scan TTS chapter (v0.14.50-64) is now closed; next priority returns to v0.14.65+ implementing remaining Scan field queries: stats (key 5), elemental affinity (keys 6/7/8), status resist (key 9), active statuses (key 0).

// v0.14.63: Narrow Hook_show_dialog battle-mode suppression to scan-active period only. v0.14.62 BAT confirmed Scan announce works perfectly with no duplications, but Aaron noticed an unintended regression: the 'Cast Fire'-type spell-cast banner that appears at the top of the screen when a character begins casting a spell stopped announcing. That banner rides on Hook_show_dialog (universal text renderer), and v0.14.62's blanket 'mode == 3 → suppress' gate silenced it along with the scan duplicate. Fix: narrow the suppression gate from 'currentMode == 3' to 'currentMode == 3 && ScanTTS::IsScreenActive()'. ScanTTS::IsScreenActive() returns true only between OnScanPopupSpawn (first sub_B687C0 fire — when the scan window actually renders) and OnScanPopupDespawn (when the player dismisses it). During that period — and only that period — the rendered scan text would duplicate the scan_tts.cpp auto-announce, so we suppress. Outside that period (including all other battle moments), 'Cast Fire' / 'Cast Cure' / 'Cast Scan' / mid-battle cutscene dialog / any other battle window text speaks normally through show_dialog. Sequencing safety: HookedScanGetText sets s_scanScreenActiveSlot via InterlockedExchange BEFORE returning the text to the engine, and Hook_show_dialog reads window text AFTER calling the original (which is what triggers sub_B687C0 internally). So by the time we check IsScreenActive() in show_dialog's speak path, the flag is already set on the first scan render — the very first show_dialog call for the scan window correctly suppresses. Files: field_dialog.cpp (added ScanTTS forward decl + replaced v0.14.62 gate with IsScreenActive() gate), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The v0.10.112 'Received <item>' draw-result rewrite ('Squall received 4 Blizzards') still applies and is now permitted always in battle since it never coincides with scan being active. Note that the v0.14.62 'isDrawReceived' bypass flag is no longer needed and was removed; the rewrite logic above the gate stays in place. SC1-PROBE and SC2-PROBE diagnostic instrumentation kept active for v0.14.63 BAT verification; will strip in v0.14.64 once Aaron confirms both scan AND 'Cast Fire' work cleanly. Single-channel SAPI mode also stays.

// v0.14.62: Gate Hook_show_dialog speak path to non-battle modes only. v0.14.61 BAT (single-channel SAPI + SC1-PROBE) proved Aaron heard the duplicate twice but serialized instead of simultaneous — single-channel worked as expected. The SC1-PROBE caught THREE Voice 1 calls at the exact moment of the Scan announce: caller=0x6DC65684 (the char* Speak wrapper), all queue-mode (interrupt=0), with content matching the three text sections of the FF8 Scan UI window: 'A fish that swims in the ground...' (description), 'Fastitocalon' (name), 'LEVEL 14 HP ?????/?????' (stats line with hidden-HP markers). Source identified: Hook_show_dialog in src/field_dialog.cpp. The hook intercepts the universal text renderer at FF8Addresses::show_dialog_addr (per the v04.17 comment, hooked for MODE_TUTO coverage) and decodes any new window text into ScreenReader::Speak via the queue-mode path. ScanTTS already owns scan-window announces via the v0.14.60 architecture (sub_B687C0 first-fire — the same engine call that triggers the rendered text fetch); this hook re-speaks those same text fetches as separate utterances. Fix: in the speak path of Hook_show_dialog, when currentMode == 3 (battle), suppress the speak unless decoded text matches the v0.10.112 'Received <item>' draw-result path (which is still valid because that's a battle-specific feature owned by field_dialog, not ScanTTS). All other battle window text is silently dropped (still logged as [SHOW_DIALOG-SUPPRESS] for diagnostic auditing). Trade-off: any other battle UI text that PREVIOUSLY relied on this hook (e.g. battle dialog windows from cutscenes that play during fights) will go silent until we explicitly route them through BattleTTS. Acceptable risk — we'll catch missing announces during regression and add explicit handling. Files: field_dialog.cpp (gate), ff8_accessibility.h (version). SC1-PROBE and SC2-PROBE diagnostic instrumentation kept active for v0.14.62 BAT verification; will strip in v0.14.63 once the duplicate is confirmed gone. v0.14.61 single-channel mode also kept — separate concern from the duplicate source, but Aaron has confirmed he prefers single-channel regardless.

// v0.14.61: Single-channel SAPI mode + SC1-PROBE diagnostic. v0.14.60 BAT showed Aaron still heard simultaneous duplicate speech during Scan announce, sounding 'disjointed and difficult to follow.' The [SC2-PROBE] caught only ONE SpeakChannel2 call (caller=0x6DC8E281, the scan_tts.cpp auto-announce path), so the second voice MUST be coming through ScreenReader::Speak() — which routes to s_pVoice (voice 1) and was NOT being probed. Aaron noted he'd thought we had disabled the dual-channel audio system, but s_pVoice2 was still being created unconditionally in InitSAPI. Two changes: (1) InitSAPI now leaves s_pVoice2 = nullptr and skips its SpMMAudioOut allocation. SpeakChannel2 falls back to s_pVoice (voice 1) so all speech goes through one voice and SAPI's per-voice queue serializes everything — no simultaneous overlap is mechanically possible. Trade-off: SpeakChannel2(text, interrupt=true) now purges voice 1's queue, potentially cutting off menu/command speech mid-utterance. Aaron has confirmed he prefers this over overlapping speech. (2) Added SC1-PROBE in ScreenReader::Speak(const wchar_t*, bool) mirroring the SC2-PROBE pattern: logs caller _ReturnAddress() and 75-char text preview as [SC1-PROBE] in ff8_mod.log every call. With single-channel mode active, simultaneous overlap can't occur, but the SC1-PROBE will tell us WHICH path was redundantly speaking the scan announce on voice 1 (and let us fix the duplicate at its source in v0.14.62). Files: screen_reader.cpp (skip voice 2 creation + SC1-PROBE in Speak), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). Strip both probes in v0.14.62+ once root cause is found. Note that v0.14.60's architectural fix (announce trigger on sub_B687C0 first-fire) IS still in place and working correctly (single SC2-PROBE confirmed the auto-announce content is correct); v0.14.61 just prevents whatever is calling Speak() with the same/related text from doubling up audibly.

// v0.14.60: Architectural fix for the v0.14.59 duplicate-announce bug + cosmetic + diagnostic. v0.14.59 BAT proved the architecture fired the auto-announce at action-commit time (when the kind=0x06 val=50 popup spawns), about 9 seconds before the actual Scan UI window opens visually. Aaron heard the announce TWICE: once at start of cast animation (full content), once when the window appeared (just name+description). The mod log AudioDucker showed two distinct duck windows (19:36:33-39 and 19:36:42-47); the second has no corresponding [SCAN-TTS] Auto-announce log line, so its source is unknown as of v0.14.59 BAT review. v0.14.60 architecture: (1) MOVED announce trigger from popup-spawn (SPRITE-POLL NEW kind=0x06 val=50 in screenshot.inl) to FIRST FIRE of sub_B687C0 hook in scan_tts.cpp — sub_B687C0 fires when the engine actually reads scan text for the UI render, matching what the player sees on screen. (2) Per-scan reset of s_scanHookFireCount in OnScanCast(_, true) so 'first fire' means 'first fire of THIS scan event,' not the cumulative count across all scans this battle. HookedScanGetText now calls OnScanPopupSpawn on count==1 (instead of being a vestigial no-op); subsequent fires are throttled diagnostic-only. (3) screenshot.inl SPRITE-POLL NEW path no longer calls OnScanPopupSpawn; the comment explains the move. The DESPAWN edge still calls OnScanPopupDespawn because the popup record stays alive for the full UI session and only despawns when the player dismisses the window. (4) Cosmetic: BuildAutoAnnounce now strips trailing periods (and trailing whitespace) from the description before appending its own period, fixing the 'may be a shark.. Press number keys' double-period in v0.14.59 BAT. (5) Diagnostic: ScreenReader::SpeakChannel2(const wchar_t*) logs every call with caller _ReturnAddress() and a 75-char text preview as [SC2-PROBE] in ff8_mod.log. If v0.14.60 BAT still shows two AudioDucker duck windows during a Scan, the SC2-PROBE log lines will identify the second caller's return address (which can be mapped back to source by subtracting the dinput8.dll load base). Strip the probe in v0.14.61+ once the second caller is identified or the duplicate disappears. Files: scan_tts.cpp (architecture + cosmetic), battle_tts_screenshot.inl (remove popup-spawn trigger), screen_reader.cpp (SC2-PROBE), ff8_accessibility.h (this version). The magicId==39 fallback in ewm.inl is kept as defense-in-depth — in the new architecture it just re-arms the snapshot/counter (idempotent if popup-hook path beat it) and the next sub_B687C0 fire announces. Doomtrain Scan-effect remains a known limitation if it doesn't trigger sub_48D200 popup hook AND doesn't trigger sub_B687C0 — not yet observed.

// v0.14.59: Scan UX redesign — silent action-layer + screen-open auto-announce + interactive number-key queries 1..0 (1..4 wired this build, 5..0 stub 'Not implemented yet.' for v0.14.60..63 chapter). The action-layer (popup hook in noeffect.inl, magicId==39 in ewm.inl) silently captures a per-slot ScanSnapshot {name, level, HP, monster_id, description} and sets s_pendingScanSlot — NO speech. One frame later, the [SPRITE-POLL] NEW emitter in screenshot.inl detects the kind=0x06 val=50 popup record and calls ScanTTS::OnScanPopupSpawn, which speaks '<Name>. <Description>. Press number keys 1 through 0 for details.' on Channel 2 and sets s_scanScreenActiveSlot. While the screen is active, PollHPCheckKeys in battle_tts_hp.inl routes 1..0 to ScanTTS::SpeakField for live querying. On popup despawn, OnScanPopupDespawn clears s_scanScreenActiveSlot. v0.14.60 supersedes the announce-trigger location (see above).

// v0.14.57: Suppress duplicate Scan announcements. v0.14.56 BAT confirmed the spurious 'No effect on <target>' is gone, but Aaron heard the Scan announcement TWICE per cast: once at action-commit (popup-based action-layer) and once 10 seconds later when the Scan UI opened and triggered the dispatcher hook. Fix: two-tier dedup. Added `bool fromActionLayer = false` parameter to OnScanCast. The two action-layer callers (popup path in noeffect.inl, magicId==39 path in ewm.inl) pass true. When OnScanCast announces with fromActionLayer=true, it sets a (slot, tick) lock for 30 seconds. Hook callers (HookedScanDispatch + HookedScanGetText, both default false) check the lock at function entry. Lock is per-slot; bypassed by action-layer. BAT PASS 2026-04-29 23:38: three Scans → three announcements. Five files: scan_tts.h, scan_tts.cpp, battle_tts.cpp, battle_tts_ewm.inl, battle_tts_noeffect.inl.

// v0.14.56: Fix v0.14.55 LNK2019 by removing the duplicate ScanTTS forward declaration from battle_tts_noeffect.inl. Cross-namespace forward decls inside .inl files create nested-namespace symbols (BattleTTS::ScanTTS::OnScanCast) different from the global ::ScanTTS::OnScanCast. Fix: removed the redundant forward decl. The global declaration already exists in battle_tts.cpp at file scope.

// v0.14.55: Action-layer Scan detection via the existing popup hook (HookedPopupSprite, sub_48D200). v0.14.54 BAT proved that BOTH sub_B687C0 and sub_84F860 only fire for the FULL Scan view (first scan of a target in a battle): the dispatcher counter reached only ~120-179 fires total despite Aaron casting Scan multiple times — i.e. only ONE Scan UI session triggered hooks. The compacted/reduced view shown on repeat Scans skips BOTH window-rendering paths entirely. Fix: detect Scan one level above the UI — at the action-commit popup. Every Scan cast (regardless of view path) spawns the spell-name popup with text_id=0x06 and value=0x32 (spell ID 50 = Scan) just before sub_48E830's snapshot recorder runs. battle_tts_sprite.inl now writes `s_lastScanCastTick` whenever that popup fires; battle_tts_noeffect.inl's NoEffect_RecordSnapshot reads it at the top: if a Scan-cast tick is within 1000 ms, the snapshot is skipped (no spurious 'No effect') and ScanTTS::OnScanCast(targetSlot) is called directly to announce. OnScanCast's existing (slot, ~2 s quiet window) dedup ensures the new path doesn't double-announce when the v0.14.54 dispatcher hook also fires on full views. The dispatcher and text-fetch hooks remain in place as defense-in-depth and to provide independent detection paths. Three files touched: battle_tts_sprite.inl (s_lastScanCastTick write site in HookedPopupSprite), battle_tts_noeffect.inl (forward decl + read + skip in NoEffect_RecordSnapshot), ff8_accessibility.h (version). v0.14.55 BUILD FAILED with LNK2019 — namespace mistake on the forward declaration. v0.14.56 fixes it.

// v0.14.54: Second Scan-detection hook on sub_84F860 (the Scan UI phase dispatcher at 0x0084F860) to catch the compacted view shown on repeat Scans of the same target in a battle. v0.14.53 BAT (with the diagnostic logging) proved the v0.14.52 sub_B687C0 hook only catches the FULL Scan view: `[SCAN-HOOK] fire #1 slot=3` appeared once at 21:38:14 for the first Bite Bug cast, then never again — the second Bite Bug Scan at ~21:38:38 (compacted view, same target) skipped sub_B687C0 entirely, leading to a spurious `[NOEFFECT-FLUSH] No effect on Bite Bug` at 21:38:48. Aaron's hint confirmed: FF8 shows a compacted/reduced-info view when scanning the same target a second+ time in a battle, and that view doesn't fetch the description text. Fix: hook sub_84F860 in addition to sub_B687C0. v0.14.55 BAT showed THIS hook also doesn't fire for compacted view, requiring the action-layer fix above. The dual hooks remain installed for defense-in-depth and full-view detection.

// v0.14.53: Diagnostic build to find why Magic-menu Scan still triggers spurious 'No effect on <target>' under v0.14.52. v0.14.52 BAT installed the sub_B687C0 hook cleanly (trampoline at 0x13160F60, log line confirms 'OK') but the Magic-menu Scan on Bite Bug 1 at 21:22:17 still produced 'No effect on Bite Bug 1' — no [SCAN-TTS] Cast line between the watchdog snapshot and the flush. Two possibilities: (a) hook never fires for Magic-menu Scan because that path uses a different rendering function, OR (b) hook fires but OnScanCast silently dedups. This build adds logging to disambiguate: HookedScanGetText logs the first call and every 60th thereafter as `[SCAN-HOOK] sub_B687C0 fire #N slot=N`, and OnScanCast logs `[SCAN-DEDUP] OnScanCast suppressed slot=N` when dedup blocks. After BAT, if [SCAN-HOOK] lines appear during Magic-menu Scan, we know the hook fires and the issue is dedup; if none appear, the hook never fires for that path and we need a different signal (likely hook a different function in the Scan UI rendering chain). One file: scan_tts.cpp (diagnostic instrumentation only — no behavior change beyond logging).

// v0.14.52: Path-agnostic Scan detection via MinHook on scan_get_text_sub_B687C0 (0x00B687C0). v0.14.51 BAT proved the magicId==39 polling detection in PollBattleMagicId only fires for Draw-Cast Scans because Magic-menu Scan goes through a different effect dispatcher that doesn't write to battle_magic_id at 0x01D99A68. Aaron cast Scan 6 times (3 enemy via Draw-Cast, 3 ally via Magic menu); only the Draw-Cast cases produced [SCAN-TTS] Cast lines and worked. The five Magic-menu Scans (one enemy at 19:54:53 + three party + one missed) each got the spurious 'No effect on <target>' watchdog announcement because OnScanCast (and therefore the watchdog cancel) never fired. Fix: hook scan_get_text_sub_B687C0 — the FFNx-named Scan-text-fetch function the engine calls every render frame the Scan UI is open. It fires for ALL Scan casts regardless of cast path because every Scan UI rendering goes through it. Slot is arg[0]. Added MinHook include + hook scaffold + InstallScanGetTextHook to scan_tts.cpp; called from ScanTTS::Initialize(). Added (slot, ~2 s quiet window) dedup to OnScanCast so the per-frame hook fires (60+ Hz during UI open) only announce once per cast: announces on slot change OR after a quiet gap; same-slot fires within the gap are suppressed. The magicId==39 path in PollBattleMagicId is retained as a parallel detection — dedup makes it a no-op when the hook fired first. Two files: scan_tts.cpp (hook + dedup), ff8_accessibility.h (version).

// v0.14.51: Scan TTS spurious 'No effect on <target>' suppression. v0.14.50 BAT proved Scan announcement worked correctly ('Glacial Eye. Level 9. HP 265 of 265.' at 19:14:37) but at 19:14:42 the no-effect watchdog from battle_tts_noeffect.inl queued 'No effect on Glacial Eye.' and flushed it 10 s later — same root cause as the v0.14.49 Draw-Stock bug: Scan goes through the player-magic action-announce path (sub_48E830 retaddr=0x0048594E, arg[1]=0x16) which records a watchdog snapshot, but Scan deals no HP / status / display change BY DESIGN (it just opens an info window). Fix: new public BattleTTS::CancelNoEffectWatchdogForSlot(int) wrapping a static NoEffect_CancelForSlot helper in battle_tts_noeffect.inl that clears both s_pendingSpellNoEffect (snapshot stage) and s_pendingNoEffectAnnounce (already-queued stage) for the given slot. ScanTTS::OnScanCast calls it after SpeakChannel2 so the genuine Scan announcement fires and the watchdog stays silent. Timing analysis: PollBattleMagicId (where ScanTTS detection runs) executes BEFORE PollPendingSpellNoEffect in the same Update tick, so the cancel is always applied before the watchdog could ever expire. The defensive queued-announcement clear covers the corner case where effect 39 is written several ticks after the snapshot. Three files touched: battle_tts_noeffect.inl (new helper), battle_tts.h + battle_tts.cpp (public wrapper), scan_tts.cpp (call site + Initialize log update).

// v0.14.50: Scan spell TTS — first slice. Detection in PollBattleMagicId (battle_tts_ewm.inl): when battle_magic_id transitions to 39 (FF8BattleEffect::Scan, confirmed via FFNx canary), read the active battle target bitmask at 0x01D76884, resolve to slot via BitmaskToSlot, and announce '<Name>. Level <L>. HP <cur> of <max>.' on Channel 2 via BattleSpeakEvent. Soft fallback: when maxHP > 99,999, announce 'HP unknown' instead — wiki guidance for hidden-HP enemies. Exact engine mechanism (whitelist vs numeric) deferred to v0.14.53; for now any max > 99,999 hides the value. No new files; ~30 lines added inside PollBattleMagicId reusing existing helpers (GetSlotName, BitmaskToSlot, BattleSpeakEvent) and entity-struct offset constants. Description, elemental affinities, status resists, and repeat-spam suppression are upcoming slices v0.14.51..v0.14.53. Deep research findings at Plan & Research Documents/Scan spell deep research results.md.

// v0.14.49: Draw-Stock no-effect suppression. The watchdog in battle_tts_noeffect.inl was firing 'No effect on <enemy>' ~6 s after every Draw > Target > Spell > Stock action because Stock genuinely doesn't change HP, status, or any of the watchdog's monitored signals. Draw > Cast must keep the watchdog active (a Fire-absorbing enemy castled with Fire is a genuine no-effect case that should announce). Fix: at the top of NoEffect_RecordSnapshot, gate on s_submenuCommandId == 0x16 (Draw command was selected during this turn's submenu nav) AND the live byte at 0x01D768D9 == 0 (Stock cursor, not Cast). Both conditions must be true to skip the snapshot. s_submenuCommandId persists across the turn-end -> action-execute transition because PollTurnAndCommands only resets it on the NEXT turn's start, so it's still valid when sub_48E830 fires for the Draw action. We don't add s_inSubmenu to the gate because that flag IS cleared on activeChar -> 0xFF before sub_48E830 fires. The live byte at 0x01D768D9 is read fresh every time rather than falling back on s_drawStockCastPrev (which could be 0xFF if the player confirmed Stock at default cursor without ever moving). One-file change in src/battle_tts_noeffect.inl plus this version bump.

// v0.14.48: Audio ducking tuning pass after v0.14.47 BAT. Three changes per Aaron's listening: (1) hold time 200ms -> 800ms on both buses to bridge gaps between menu-nav announcements (the v0.14.47 200ms hold was just short enough that volume bounced up between successive menu cursor moves). (2) BGM depth -15 dB -> -10 dB; v0.14.47 BGM duck was deeper than necessary now that ducking is working at all. (3) SFX depth -6 dB -> -15 dB; attack animation SFX still masked TTS at -6 dB, so escalated to match the depth that was previously on BGM. Pure constant changes in GameAudio::Initialize -- no architecture changes.

// v0.14.47: Audio ducking Phase 2 — reusable AudioDucker module with per-bus configuration (BGM and SFX), reference-counted BeginDuck/EndDuck trigger, smoothed one-pole envelope (separate attack/release time constants), hold timer to bridge SAPI chunk gaps, and dB-based config converted to linear gain internally. New files: src/audio_ducker.h + audio_ducker.cpp. Wired into game_audio.cpp via four function-pointer accessors (GetUserBgmVolume / ApplyBgmVolume / GetUserSfxVolume / ApplySfxVolume) so the ducker stays domain-agnostic. GameAudio::Update polls ScreenReader::IsSpeaking() once per tick on the rising/falling edge to drive BeginDuck / EndDuck; existing 500ms periodic Reapply gates on !AudioDucker::IsActive(); F2 toggle now actually switches ducking on/off via AudioDucker::SetEnabled. Default depths: BGM -15 dB (deep — GF summon music masks AD heavily), SFX -6 dB (light — most SFX short). Default times: 100 ms attack, 600 ms release, 200 ms hold. dB depths hardcoded for now because Config::GetInt uses GetPrivateProfileInt which doesn't handle negative-value INI keys cleanly — tunable in source. F5/F6/F7/F8 volume changes skip the immediate Reapply call when ducker is active so they don't briefly flash to user-level mid-duck (ducker picks up the new user vol on next Tick).

// v0.14.45: SFX volume control + audio-ducking toggle + full keyboard layout reshuffle. Resolved game-side sfx_set_master_volume via opcode_effectplay2 chain (pExecuteOpcodeTable[0x21] -> +0x5F sfx_play_to_current_playing_channel -> +0x35 play_sfx_on_channel -> +0xA1 sfx_set_volume; sfx_get_master_volume = sfx_set_volume - 0x10; sfx_set_master_volume = sfx_get_master_volume - 0xE0). FFNx replaces sfx_set_master_volume with a JMP to its own setSFXMasterVolume bridge in the same way it replaces set_midi_volume. Mirrored the v0.09.24 BGM hook architecture: scan the FFNx replacement bytes for nxAudioEngine ptr + setSFXMasterVolume method address, MinHook the FFNx function, periodically reapply via direct __fastcall thiscall shim. New persistent INI keys: sfx_volume (default 100), tts_duck_enabled (default 1), sfx_duck_ratio (default 30, percent for hand-editability). Auto-duck logic itself is deferred to v0.14.46 — F2 toggle persists the bool and announces state but is otherwise inert until Phase 2 wires ScreenReader::IsSpeaking() polling. Keyboard layout reshuffled per Aaron: F1 cycle voice, F2 ducking toggle, F3/F4 speech rate, Shift+F3/F4 speech volume, F5/F6 SFX volume, F7/F8 BGM volume. Removed v0.12.21 stale F2 'Director Varblock' diagnostic block from field_nav_handlekeys.inl (DEVNOTES called it unused but it was firing). Generalized 'Function key repurposing rule' added to DEVNOTES.

// v0.14.44: GF summon audio descriptions. New module gf_audio_desc.cpp/h mirrors fmv_audio_desc architecture. Per-GF VTT files embedded as RCDATA resources (IDR_VTT_GF_BASE = 6000). Trigger wired into PollBattleMagicId() in battle_tts_ewm.inl: when battle_magic_id changes to a GF effect ID, GfAudioDesc::OnGFAnimationStart fires and begins cue playback on Channel 2 (event voice). Stops playback when battle_magic_id reverts (covers natural end + R1+L1 skip). VTTs cover all 16 junctioned GFs (Quezacotl, Shiva, Ifrit, Siren, Brothers, Diablos, Carbuncle, Leviathan, Pandemona, Cerberus, Alexander, Doomtrain, Bahamut, Cactuar, Tonberry, Eden) plus Phoenix (item) and Odin (auto-summon). Each opens with GF name + attack name (replaces visual popup), then cues describe animation phases. Timing draws on community references; will need BAT tuning per GF since animation durations are approximate.

// v0.14.43: Diagnostic cleanup post-Bug-A resolution. Stripped DumpItemMenuState (the [ITEM-DUMP] block) from src/battle_tts_menu.inl — v0.14.42 BAT verified the disassembly model so the dump is no longer needed. Stripped [BATTLESPEAK-DIAG] log preamble/postamble from BattleSpeak in src/battle_tts.cpp — added in v0.14.37 to investigate items audio purge, no longer needed. Stripped [SPEAK-DIAG] log statements from ScreenReader::Speak in src/screen_reader.cpp — same provenance. Net: ~150 lines of diagnostic code removed; F12 stays free for next investigation. The [ITEM-LIST] block is retained (low overhead, one shot per submenu open, useful for future regression checks). No functional changes; behavior identical to v0.14.42 with cleaner logs.

// v0.14.40: Items submenu — visual page/slot fix + comprehensive diagnostic dump. v0.14.39 BAT showed (a) FindItemControllerNode() returns NULL on every cursor read (40/40) — the v0.10.104 handler signature 0x4F81F0 isn't where expected in this build, OR FFNx replaced it, OR the pool layout shifted; (b) Aaron's saved arrangement has gaps between items ("Elixir was page 1 slot 4 when really it was on page 3 following a bunch of empty slots"). Fix part 1 (visual position): added boIdx field to BattleItemEntry tracking the original battle_order position. BuildItemList now records boIdx for each item. New GetItemVisualPos() helper computes page/slot from boIdx (so saved-arrangement gaps are reflected). Both items announce paths (main + SUBMENU-DELAYED) use it. Theory: BATTLE_SUBMENU_CURSOR is a compacted item-index cursor; engine renders items at their battle_order POSITIONS with empties preserved. Fix part 2 (diagnostic): added DumpItemMenuState() that runs once per items submenu open. Logs raw 32 bytes of battle_order, inv lookup for each non-FF entry, hex dump of all 10 pool slots first 0x40 bytes, every code-pointer DWORD found in pool memory (0x004XXXXX or 0x6E70XXXX range), explicit search for ITEM_HANDLER (0x4F81F0) at every offset, and 32 bytes around BATTLE_SUBMENU_CURSOR for cursor-neighborhood inspection.

// v0.14.39: Bug A items-ordering attempted fix via pool-node helper. BAT proved helper returns NULL across the entire battle — handler offset/address don't match this build.

